WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.010
- Thank you.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.593
Can I call the meeting to order?

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.680
Thank you.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.200
Can I first of welcome
members of the public

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.460
as our guests today,

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:17.493
who've joined to observe the meeting?

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.070
We do have apologies for
absence this afternoon

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.300
from Laura Wade-Gery and Susan Kilsby

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:25.980
and Rakesh Kapoor

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.303
and Jacqui Rock.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:29.527
Can I just ask the board,

00:00:29.527 --> 00:00:33.210
are there any interest to
declare in addition to the,

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:36.840
those already declared or
actually as circulated?

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.280
- No.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:39.210
- Thank you very much.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:42.570
And can I get the board's
approval of the minutes

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.303
of the last meeting held
on the 2nd of February?

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:48.840
Thank you very much.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.890
Let me just give you a
brief chair's update.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:57.890
We had a very good private
board meeting this morning

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:03.170
and we covered a number
of areas including EDI,

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.760
including capital strategy,

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.000
including an update on the
federated data platform

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.820
currently out for procurement.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.350
We also looked at a
very good piece of work

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.783
around productivity and efficiency.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.730
We also spent some time...

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.440
looking at...

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.600
essentially the merger of,

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.280
in this case, HEE.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.020
Sorry, we're getting some interference.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.250
The merger with HEE,

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.490
so the board is aware that we've now,

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:36.810
essentially, over the
last couple of years,

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:41.160
merged five other
entities into NHS England

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:42.946
and as of the 1st of April,

00:01:42.946 --> 00:01:46.533
HEE will join NHS England.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.940
Digital joined actually
a couple of months ago

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:54.550
and I would just like to
compliment the executive team

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:59.820
for such a well managed,

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.540
but very complex, set of transactions.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.030
Wol has led that work for us

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:09.870
and that new NHS England
committee work has been,

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:11.673
as I say, excellent.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.980
This afternoon's agenda.
there are a number of items,

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.620
the normal updates but also
the two particular items

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.060
I think of real interest.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.700
One, Matt Whitty is
going to be leading us,

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.620
will be on innovation,

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.480
and then separately, and secondly,

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.430
and Ruth, I think you'll kick it off,

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.440
and we have our colleagues here

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.900
will actually be on our
three year delivery plan

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.080
for Maternity and Neonatal Services.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.910
Both really important topics, I think,

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:41.853
for us to discuss this afternoon.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:46.470
Can I then just give a brief update

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.780
on some of my more recent activities?

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.710
So I visited a number of
primary care practises,

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:54.240
one in particular in Harringay,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.220
was very interesting where
I met not just the GPs

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:57.780
but also community pharmacists,

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:59.790
the front of house staff

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:02.400
and also the patient participation group.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.630
And we explored a number of the issues

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:05.760
currently facing primary care,

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.160
but also the transformation
work being done there

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.690
at a site which had a very complex,

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.390
I think, patient demographics.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:15.600
Again, another patient group
in in Northeast London,

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.710
the Hurley Group,

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:19.620
actually, and I also, we then
went to Hamilton Hospital.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:21.930
What was interesting
there was actually seeing

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:23.550
potentially primary care of the future,

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:26.110
the use of iPads at the reception of ED

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:31.050
and how they helped to
navigate patients coming to,

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:34.020
coming to ED, into one of four pathways

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.570
according to urgency
and according to need.

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:38.520
And then actually with the Hurley Group,

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.220
a really excellent
conversation with the GPs

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:44.040
about actually how in
this case e-consultation,

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.380
can help them essentially
triage and manage

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.800
the flow of demand that they face,

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:53.040
actually steering people either
into a urgent appointment

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.650
or actually into a
different area for support

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.810
or actually just in terms of prescription

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:01.110
or other response.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.410
We've had a couple of round
table discussion groups,

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:07.650
one on adult social
care where, with Amanda,

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:09.600
we met with senior leaders from across

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.700
the adult social care sector

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.670
and also really to help
work out what is a clearly

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:18.180
hugely important and symbiotic
relationship that exists

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:21.780
between the NHS social care services

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:22.980
and local authorities.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.750
And talking about local authorities,

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.360
I also had a very good meeting, actually,

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.160
with counsellor James Jameson,

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:33.660
who chairs the local
government association.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:37.530
I joined the Accelerated
Access Collaborative Board

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:40.020
and we were discussing clinical trials

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.030
and how to improve access
to clinical trials.

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:44.820
Again, really helpful
discussion to be able

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.226
to take part in.

00:04:47.226 --> 00:04:49.260
The second of the round tables,

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:50.370
I should have mentioned, actually,

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:53.550
which we only held yesterday
with senior leaders

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.860
from urgent and emergency care sector,

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.620
and again, Chris Hopson,
you were chairing that,

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:00.390
thank you very much.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:02.370
And we were looking at
Urgent Emergency Care,

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:04.620
discussing preparations for next winter,

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.180
thinking about capacitization.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:07.437
Again, very, very good discussion.

00:05:07.437 --> 00:05:10.650
And with the group of
people with whom we work

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.150
and who will help us essentially

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.850
to deal with those challenges.

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:16.740
And then, separately,

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:19.530
I had a meeting again with
Amanda and with Tim Ferris,

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:22.110
actually, with Genomics England.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:23.820
And what's interesting
about Genomics England,

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:26.010
and I'm learning all the time,

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:28.050
that we're we, the National
Health Service, here,

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:30.390
with Genomics England, are world leaders,

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:31.740
actually, in genomics

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:34.020
and that we do whole genome sequencing now

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:38.910
for newborns or young children
who have particular illnesses

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:40.440
and then we do whole genome sequencing

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:42.930
and look at how we can intervene.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:46.620
I think it's a remarkable
leadership that this country has

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:51.620
and we now scan for 3,000
rare diseases and 200 cancers.

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:54.720
So, again, it was a
really very, very good,

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:55.713
good discussion.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:01.680
Separately, let me just inform the board,

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:04.320
we are about to go out,

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.620
and I'm delighted to have the
support of the department,

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:10.080
to advertise essentially
for new non-executives

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:11.880
to join the board.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.220
That advert hopefully will go out tomorrow

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:16.890
and we're looking for a
range of particular skills

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:21.390
to add to those that are
here around the board table.

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:23.640
On a less cheerful note,

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:25.170
I think we ought just to note

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:27.600
the British Social Attitude Survey

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.013
published earlier this week.

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:32.100
You know, the public
satisfaction with the NHS,

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:34.080
we have to recognise has slumped, I think,

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:37.560
to its lowest level
ever recorded, I think,

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:39.030
by the BSA

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:43.320
and with accident and emergency services,

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:44.970
seeing actually the biggest increase

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:47.640
in levels of dissatisfaction.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:49.170
And so that's something we
really need to focus on.

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:50.790
What's really also important though

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:52.530
is that in these surveys,

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:55.050
the public absolutely
still, the population,

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:57.941
still absolutely believes in
the fundamental principles

00:06:57.941 --> 00:07:01.290
that found, are the foundations for,

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:02.400
the National Health Service,

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:05.250
essentially, free at
the point of delivery,

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:09.120
accessible to all and primarily
funded through taxation,

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.220
but it's free at the point of delivery

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:13.527
and accessible to all I think are,

00:07:13.527 --> 00:07:17.940
are the underlying principles
that we all actually espouse.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:22.110
That's all I was going to
say by way of introduction,

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:25.380
and some brief insight
to some of the activities

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:29.940
that I've been undertaking
over the last few weeks or so.

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:33.450
Can I hand, Amanda, to you
for Chief Exec's update?

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:34.283
- Great.

00:07:34.283 --> 00:07:35.730
Thank you very much, indeed.

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:38.400
So just echoing Richard's words, if I may,

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:41.250
we are very much looking
forward to formally welcoming

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:44.010
our new colleagues from
Health Education England

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:45.660
from next week.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:47.880
And I would also just like
to take this opportunity,

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:52.740
if I may, to thank former
NHS Digital colleagues

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:56.160
for welcoming me to
Wellington Place up in Leeds

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:58.950
a couple of weeks ago to meet
several of the teams up there

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:01.080
who are doing, yeah, actually
incredibly impressive

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:05.040
but also vital work to
support the whole of the NHS,

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:09.000
like cybersecurity,
maintaining the NHS Spine.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.980
And I also had the opportunity to meet

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:12.960
with some of the staff network leads

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:15.690
from what was NHS Digital,
but is now, of course,

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:19.824
a completely integral part
of the new NHS England.

00:08:19.824 --> 00:08:23.760
The timing is, I think, really important

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:26.940
for us in welcoming HEE colleagues

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:28.140
in particularly, of course,

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:31.320
thanks to David and Navina for,

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:33.930
for their leadership
over the last few years

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:37.710
and their leadership
of the merger with us,

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:41.130
because it's a time, isn't
it, where NHS workforce

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:44.640
challenges are right at the
top of the agenda for us

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:47.490
and HE has been the expert voice

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:50.820
on training and education for a decade now

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:52.410
and over that last year,

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:55.200
I think that expertise
has been absolutely vital

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.030
as we've worked together to develop

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:58.890
the long-term workforce plan.

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:01.410
So I'm sure I speak on behalf of all of us

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:05.070
when I say thank you to HE
colleagues for that contribution,

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:07.050
for the leadership of
the work on the plan,

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:09.780
as well, of course, as to that just vital

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:13.710
day-to-day business of
supporting learners into the NHS

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:16.350
and developing their careers here.

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:18.630
As I said at the time,

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:23.630
the sheer breadth of organisations
represented now around,

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:25.203
what I said at the time,

00:09:25.203 --> 00:09:27.330
what we were talking about,
the merger at the beginning.

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:30.090
I say it again now, you know,
the sheer range of people

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:32.520
that we now have around our table,

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:34.260
but also the sheer range of stakeholders

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:38.340
that we now work with is
I think a real testament

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:40.380
to the strength of partnership working,

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:43.980
which we absolutely hold at
the centre of how we work

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:48.510
as an NHS and going to an event
which David and Navina held

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:50.430
just a couple of weeks ago to mark

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:53.610
the 10th anniversary of HEE
really brought that home.

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:56.430
We just look at the number
of of different voices

00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:59.820
in the room, all absolutely
committed to the same ambitions

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:01.950
and to working together to achieve them.

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:05.160
So, you know, what we do, what we do well,

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:08.700
we all do in service of the
wider health and care sector

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:12.670
and we do internally to
make sure that we are

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:18.030
making sure that our working
practises, our culture,

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:20.670
means that ultimately the new NHS England,

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.860
we can absolutely be proud
to say will be greater

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:24.930
than the sum of its individual parts.

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:27.900
That is the ambition we've had
right from the very beginning

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:29.790
and delighted that we're now at the point

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:32.520
where we're beginning to
see that come to fruition.

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:35.300
But worth saying, and I did
want to just say this, if I may,

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:38.220
in the context of what I've
just said about culture,

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:42.810
is we can only function
well as an organisation

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:46.140
if all of our staff
feel able and supported

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:47.760
to do their best work.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:51.300
And we have had a reminder
over the past few weeks

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:54.030
that sometimes we fall short on this.

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:56.670
So, I know others, David, Ruth, others,

00:10:56.670 --> 00:10:59.010
may want to say more about this,

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:01.500
but the outcome of the
recent employment tribunal

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:05.370
brought by Michelle Cox made
for extremely challenging

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:07.020
reading for all of us.

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:09.030
The tribunal found, for those of you

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:10.860
who haven't yet read the outcome

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:14.550
that Michelle was subject to
acts of race discrimination

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:19.550
as well as detriment for
whistle blowing in 2019, 2020.

00:11:20.091 --> 00:11:22.410
We very clearly let Michelle down,

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:26.040
and in doing so, we also
missed an opportunity to learn

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:27.990
and to change earlier.

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:30.900
So I think we are all
very grateful to Michelle

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:34.260
for her courage and her
resilience in speaking up

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:38.340
about practise that was
inappropriate and discriminatory

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:41.520
and we are committed to using
the outcome of the tribunal

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.560
to deliver action so that others

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:47.160
do not face the same
or similar experience.

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:50.010
We've already taken action since then

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:53.850
and we have set that out
to all of our colleagues

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:55.920
along with the further
actions that we will be taking

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:57.690
to try and ensure no other colleagues

00:11:57.690 --> 00:11:59.553
go through what she did.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:03.150
Moving on,

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:05.070
and I'm sure, again, this
is something by the way

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:06.810
we will come back to in
future board meetings

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:08.970
and we will keep talking about a lot.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:10.770
But moving on, because
I'm conscious that we have

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:12.870
other things we need
talk about on the agenda,

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:16.110
and, again, others will talk
about this, David, in a moment,

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:20.472
but in relation to pressures
in the wider NHS system,

00:12:20.472 --> 00:12:24.930
we can see in the performance report

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:27.960
that the most pressing
challenge for the whole system

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:29.430
has continued to be around

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:32.558
the Urgent Emergency Care pathway.

00:12:32.558 --> 00:12:35.742
On the specifics,

00:12:35.742 --> 00:12:39.090
you will all know that when we met

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:40.590
as a whole group in February,

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:42.870
we talked about the fact that we'd had

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:45.150
just the most challenging winter, I think,

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:48.150
that any of us had experienced,

00:12:48.150 --> 00:12:49.860
and it has been really welcome

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:52.740
to see that progresses has been made

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:54.960
through those additional
actions we set out

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:58.650
to boost capacity has
endured over the course

00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:00.030
of the last couple of months

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:02.460
that we have seen sustained improvement

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:04.410
in ambulance response times,

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:08.550
in access through accident
emergency in particular.

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:11.610
So I think, to your
point earlier, Richard,

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:14.880
about the British Social Attitude Survey,

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:17.430
against that backdrop of
extraordinary pressure

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:20.673
in Urgent Emergency Care, I think we can,

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:25.650
we can point to, now, the publication

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:27.630
of the Urgent Emergency Care Recovery Plan

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:30.060
and the evidence of the actions
that we've taken over winter

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:33.180
as evidence that even now we can see

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:35.880
that we are making progress
in dealing with the things

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:38.370
that really matter most to patients.

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:41.520
Equally true, of course, and
David will say more about this,

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:43.170
about Recovering Elective Care,

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:45.840
where we published a plan a
year ago recognising that things

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:48.090
were going to get more
challenging before they got better

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:50.730
but better they have got,
particularly in relation

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:52.950
to patients who've waited the longest.

00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:54.420
And we are hopeful that
we're going to be able

00:13:54.420 --> 00:13:56.430
to publish something
shortly at setting out

00:13:56.430 --> 00:13:57.840
a similar sort of recovery plan

00:13:57.840 --> 00:13:59.490
around access to primary care,

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:00.840
which, again, is something we know

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:02.820
patients really care about.

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:04.590
And the pressure on our
Primary Care colleagues

00:14:04.590 --> 00:14:06.930
is something I think, again,
we'd all share an ambition

00:14:06.930 --> 00:14:08.820
to make sure that we do everything we can

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:11.130
to try and support colleagues to take to,

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:12.990
to make it easier for them to do

00:14:12.990 --> 00:14:16.461
the incredibly important work
that they do every single day.

00:14:16.461 --> 00:14:20.130
So those things really matter,
they matter to patients.

00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:22.623
David will talk about
them more and we know

00:14:22.623 --> 00:14:26.070
that those are the areas
where we are giving

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:28.350
particular focus now to try and make sure

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:30.360
that we're in a better
starting place when we do hit

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:32.430
the inevitable pressures next winter.

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:35.460
However, I should just say
that industrial action,

00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:38.880
of course, has provided
an additional challenge

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:41.610
on top of those winter pressures.

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:45.390
Local teams have coped, admirably,

00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:47.760
and my thanks to all of them,

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:49.827
as well as to national colleagues
and regional colleagues

00:14:49.827 --> 00:14:52.440
for everything that they have done

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:55.110
over the course of industrial action.

00:14:55.110 --> 00:14:57.707
But it has taken a huge amount of work

00:14:57.707 --> 00:15:01.290
and it is clearly not sustainable.

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:03.480
So I think we all really welcomed

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:05.040
the announcement from the government

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:07.440
and most of the unions representing staff

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:11.370
on a Agenda For Change that has
meant a suspension of action

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:13.440
from those staff groups.

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:15.420
We are all conscious, I think,

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:17.820
that it is not a done deal yet,

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:19.560
pending ballots of
union members on whether

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:21.540
to accept the offer on the table,

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:25.590
but we are all hopeful of a resolution.

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:28.470
And, of course, we dealt with
our first period of action

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:32.850
from junior doctors earlier
this month with further action.

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:34.620
Talk about that more in a moment

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:37.890
scheduled for after the Easter weekend.

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:40.980
David will share some
insights into the impact

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:42.000
of that first period,

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:45.690
but also preparations for
the second in a moment.

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:48.000
Suffice to say though, I
think that none of us wants

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:50.280
to see further action take place.

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:53.760
So we do hope for similar
progress with junior doctors

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:56.310
that we have seen with the
Agenda For Change staff,

00:15:56.310 --> 00:15:59.790
but ultimately it is our
job to support the NHS

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:01.800
to plan and to manage services

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:05.043
to minimise disruption
and risk for patients.

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:11.430
If I may, the next thing
I just wanted to mention,

00:16:11.430 --> 00:16:14.640
sticking a bit with a theme of pressure

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:18.060
and what we're doing about
it, is the staff survey.

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:20.490
So you will have noticed colleagues

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:23.190
that the NHS staff survey was published

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:25.980
a couple of weeks ago
and that that did show

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:28.460
a decrease in satisfaction
and ongoing challenges

00:16:28.460 --> 00:16:30.000
in a number of areas.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:32.940
Now some of that will be down
to pay and some of it will be

00:16:32.940 --> 00:16:35.430
certainly a reflection of
the operational pressures

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:36.510
that colleagues are under,

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:40.486
but by no means is that all of the story.

00:16:40.486 --> 00:16:43.350
And we should note in
offsetting that though,

00:16:43.350 --> 00:16:45.990
that there were some encouraging
findings from the survey

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:48.120
about how people feel supported,

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:50.130
particularly by their line managers,

00:16:50.130 --> 00:16:52.590
and that they feel they
have more opportunities

00:16:52.590 --> 00:16:54.600
to develop their careers than when

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:56.250
the survey was done last year.

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:58.650
And, you know, those
things do really matter.

00:16:58.650 --> 00:17:01.920
So we are continuing to support employers

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:03.930
to address those things
that matter to staff

00:17:03.930 --> 00:17:07.230
and that we can impact
on, like flexible working,

00:17:07.230 --> 00:17:10.980
line management, leadership
development, career pathways,

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:13.920
whilst continuing to do all
the things I've just mentioned,

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:15.990
which hopefully are talking about,

00:17:15.990 --> 00:17:19.080
talking to relieving some
of that broader pressure

00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:21.060
that we're experiencing on
the service at the moment

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:21.893
and, of course, that people

00:17:21.893 --> 00:17:24.240
are living and breathing every day.

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:26.070
So that leads us on, I've
just got a few more things,

00:17:26.070 --> 00:17:28.560
if I may, Richard, just to
the long term workforce plan.

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:30.480
I've mentioned it already
in terms of the joint work

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:32.730
we've been doing with HEE on it.

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:37.730
We know that having a focus on workforce

00:17:37.830 --> 00:17:40.980
is absolutely central to
everything else we do.

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:43.843
Having a long-term workforce plan will be

00:17:43.843 --> 00:17:47.880
a really important moment for
us to be able to plan ahead,

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:51.540
both in terms of some of
the roots into training

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:55.440
and training numbers, but also
skill mix, new career paths,

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:57.480
and crucially, of course,

00:17:57.480 --> 00:17:59.340
reflecting at the very heart of it

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:01.530
that focus on looking after our own staff

00:18:01.530 --> 00:18:03.420
and retaining those
colleagues that we do have.

00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:04.893
So we're just in the final,

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:06.650
final...

00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:11.310
part of the process of
negotiating with government

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:12.960
around just how we land the plan

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:15.090
and timing and choreography,

00:18:15.090 --> 00:18:18.360
but delighted to have
the welcome confirmation

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:19.830
from the chancellor in the budget,

00:18:19.830 --> 00:18:22.920
the publication will be expected shortly.

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:24.840
So, just as important
as the plan of course

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:26.670
will then be how we deliver it.

00:18:26.670 --> 00:18:28.860
And I know that Navina and
the team, in particular,

00:18:28.860 --> 00:18:31.320
are using this time to
think about how we get ready

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:33.570
to make sure that as soon
as the plan is published

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:36.360
we are hitting the ground
running on delivery.

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:37.410
I've mentioned that we're hoping that

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:40.770
the Primary Care Plan will be
similarly published shortly.

00:18:40.770 --> 00:18:43.740
Primary Care Access Plan, I
should say, just to be clear,

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:46.620
it's about access
recovery and that, again,

00:18:46.620 --> 00:18:49.530
we're hoping we'll set out just
some very practical actions

00:18:49.530 --> 00:18:53.040
to support colleagues who are delivering

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:54.960
such as essential
services in primary care,

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:56.550
but also support patients to be able

00:18:56.550 --> 00:18:59.460
to access those services more easily.

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:01.080
Two final things to mention.

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:05.520
We are, today, publishing
the delivery plan

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:07.950
for Maternity and Neonatal Services.

00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:11.740
That is a hugely important moment for us

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:14.070
and I know we're going to
have detailed conversation

00:19:14.070 --> 00:19:15.600
about it shortly,

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:18.180
but I just know,
personally, from attending

00:19:18.180 --> 00:19:20.940
the NHS Maternity and
Neonatal Services Summit

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:24.450
with Ruth and with Jacqui,

00:19:24.450 --> 00:19:27.060
when was it, last week, week before?

00:19:27.060 --> 00:19:29.763
Very recently, I can thoroughly say,

00:19:29.763 --> 00:19:33.240
that this is a, this
document is really important

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:34.680
to the people who are working

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.990
in Maternity and Neonatal Services

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:41.430
and to those who have
worked with us and with them

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:44.280
to ensure the voices of families are heard

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:47.970
and to people like Bill
Kirkup and Donna Ockenden,

00:19:47.970 --> 00:19:51.510
who, again, have been so
instrumental in shining a light

00:19:51.510 --> 00:19:53.040
on what we need to do to improve

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:55.110
Maternity and Neonatal Services.

00:19:55.110 --> 00:19:57.800
And, again, have been kind
of critical colleagues

00:19:57.800 --> 00:20:00.525
in the development of the plan today.

00:20:00.525 --> 00:20:02.700
And can I also just take
the liberty of being

00:20:02.700 --> 00:20:04.920
the first person who've,
probably a number of people

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:07.560
will all repeat this through
today's board meeting,

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:10.440
but just in congratulating Jacqui,

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:13.440
who was the first Chief Midwifery,

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:17.010
First National Chief Midwifery Officer,

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:20.220
extraordinarily big boots to fill, Jacqui,

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:21.720
when you do leave us, but she,

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:23.220
Jacqui will be leaving us shortly

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:25.920
to join the International
Confederation of Midwives.

00:20:25.920 --> 00:20:27.810
So as we said before, England's loss is

00:20:27.810 --> 00:20:30.979
the world's gain on this one.

00:20:30.979 --> 00:20:33.360
And then the very final
thing to mention is just,

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:34.290
we've talked about it before,

00:20:34.290 --> 00:20:37.060
but we have been working closely,

00:20:37.060 --> 00:20:40.140
as many, many stakeholders have,

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:43.950
with Patricia Hewitt on
her review of how ICSs

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:46.590
can grow and develop
with a right governance

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:48.300
and oversight to succeed.

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:51.390
We're expecting, I think that
she is going to be publishing

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:54.990
that final report potentially
even as early as next week.

00:20:54.990 --> 00:20:57.870
And, again, this is one we
know that is highly anticipated

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:00.630
by local teams who really
want to just kind of

00:21:00.630 --> 00:21:03.600
get on with setting
themselves up in the right way

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:05.550
to deliver all the things they need to

00:21:05.550 --> 00:21:07.680
and to plan for the future.

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:08.680
- Thank you, Amanda.

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:12.540
I'm going ask David Sloman, in a moment,

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:15.180
just to give us an operational
performance update,

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:16.800
but before I do that, I should have,

00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:21.180
and Laura, as I said, can't
be here this afternoon,

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:24.090
but she's Chaired NHS
Digital superbly well

00:21:24.090 --> 00:21:25.740
and that's now part of NHS England.

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:30.077
I just want to thank David
Behanand for chairing HEE so ably

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:36.500
as now HEE is, joins with NHS England,

00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:39.240
and in doing that, just to reflect that

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:41.580
as we talked this morning,
David, the baton comes back,

00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:45.360
because to ensure good oversight
by this board, actually,

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:47.880
we have, as you know,
set up a subcommittee

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:49.710
which actually, WTE,

00:21:49.710 --> 00:21:52.770
so Workforce, Training and
Education, going forward,

00:21:52.770 --> 00:21:55.200
and that just will make sure that we

00:21:55.200 --> 00:22:00.200
do not lose sight of the
very broad and busy agenda

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:03.660
that HEE has historically been overseeing.

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:05.970
And we've done the same
obviously with Digital,

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:08.400
in terms of data and technology subgroup,

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:10.860
because both of those entities carry,

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:13.080
carry, currently or did carry,

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:15.720
huge burdens and huge responsibilities.

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:18.180
And as we merge these entities together,

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:21.120
it's really important that
we don't lose sight and focus

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:23.610
on that range of issues.

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:27.000
And the other thing, David,
just before I bring you in,

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:28.200
we've had a discussion as the board,

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:31.410
and I think it's really
important that we reflect

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:33.960
on what the purpose of the board is.

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:38.910
We're going to have a strategy
review, strategy day, in May,

00:22:38.910 --> 00:22:41.520
early May, and what should a board do?

00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:44.190
Well the board obviously,
working with the execs,

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:46.290
providing challenge to the execs,

00:22:46.290 --> 00:22:48.330
as we deal with the immediate issues.

00:22:48.330 --> 00:22:50.370
And you can tell from
Amanda's report, actually,

00:22:50.370 --> 00:22:52.080
just has substantial and wide range

00:22:52.080 --> 00:22:54.690
in the immediate pressures
on the service are.

00:22:54.690 --> 00:22:56.880
So we engage on that,

00:22:56.880 --> 00:22:58.170
and then actually we need to lift up

00:22:58.170 --> 00:23:00.660
and we need to keep our eyes very much on

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:03.450
relatively pure key priorities,

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:06.150
like the long-term workforce
plan that Amanda's referenced

00:23:06.150 --> 00:23:08.310
and which will then make
sure we can implement

00:23:08.310 --> 00:23:10.320
and capacitize once it's published,

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:14.250
like in technology, whether
it's a federated data platform

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:18.150
or increasing functionality
in the NHS app,

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:21.090
whether it's engaging
behind life sciences,

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:25.173
behind genomics, behind MedTech,
staying focused on cancer,

00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:30.660
there are half a dozen,
six, seven, eight key areas

00:23:30.660 --> 00:23:32.940
where I think the board
can stay very focused

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:35.970
strategically on those in five years time

00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:36.900
when we look back.
- Yeah.

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:39.333
- And we deliver along
those pathways, actually,

00:23:39.333 --> 00:23:42.720
that the NHS will have been transformed,

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:44.610
actually, will have been
significantly changed,

00:23:44.610 --> 00:23:48.250
potentially transformed and operating

00:23:49.170 --> 00:23:50.400
perhaps very differently.

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:52.290
I mean, Chris Hopson uses a phrase

00:23:52.290 --> 00:23:56.400
which I have copied from
him and I keep using,

00:23:56.400 --> 00:23:58.470
which is actually, we are
potentially on the cusp

00:23:58.470 --> 00:24:01.680
of a data and technology
revolution that can utterly change

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:05.010
the way we offer health services

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:07.650
and how we engage with our population,

00:24:07.650 --> 00:24:10.440
and it's really important
that is one feature

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:12.210
of the board's strategic agenda,

00:24:12.210 --> 00:24:13.770
that we keep focus on those

00:24:13.770 --> 00:24:15.870
and we've repetitively come back to them

00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:17.670
and we walk along that march.

00:24:17.670 --> 00:24:19.380
Ruth, you want to say something, I think.

00:24:19.380 --> 00:24:20.910
- I wanted to come back on Michelle Cox,

00:24:20.910 --> 00:24:22.819
well, I wait until the right time.

00:24:22.819 --> 00:24:24.090
- Okay, well I know I finished

00:24:24.090 --> 00:24:27.896
my sort of rallying call around

00:24:27.896 --> 00:24:31.140
the twin obligations of the board,

00:24:31.140 --> 00:24:33.600
focus on the immediate,
working with the executive,

00:24:33.600 --> 00:24:35.433
and challenging the executive
and then making sure

00:24:35.433 --> 00:24:38.820
that we stay focused on that
relatively few major issue,

00:24:38.820 --> 00:24:40.830
major steps and major priorities

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:43.747
that can actually transform
what the NHS does.

00:24:44.970 --> 00:24:46.134
Ruth, do you want to come in?

00:24:46.134 --> 00:24:46.967
- Yes, thank you.

00:24:46.967 --> 00:24:49.560
So just to build on what Amanda has said,

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:54.150
there's lots of learning
around how we did and didn't

00:24:54.150 --> 00:24:57.180
respond to Michelle Cox and her concerns.

00:24:57.180 --> 00:25:00.630
And so, racism is unacceptable

00:25:00.630 --> 00:25:05.070
and has no place in health and
care, nor indeed in society,

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:09.480
but we know it exists and we
know the impact it can have,

00:25:09.480 --> 00:25:12.090
can be devastating for people.

00:25:12.090 --> 00:25:13.530
As registered professionals,

00:25:13.530 --> 00:25:16.770
we all have a responsibility
under our code of the NMC

00:25:16.770 --> 00:25:20.040
to challenge that discriminator behaviour

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:22.110
and create the environment that supports

00:25:22.110 --> 00:25:26.760
and treats all individuals
with dignity and respect.

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:30.750
So combating racial
discrimination is so important

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:33.030
and that's why in November last year,

00:25:33.030 --> 00:25:35.250
in partnership with the
Nursing Midwifery Council,

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:38.246
the NMC and the NHS Confederation,

00:25:38.246 --> 00:25:41.220
NHS England published a new resource

00:25:41.220 --> 00:25:43.890
about combating racial
discrimination resource,

00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:46.920
and that is there to support
all registered nurses,

00:25:46.920 --> 00:25:49.020
midwives and...

00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:53.400
of course, into health
and into social care.

00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:54.690
And finally, Chair, if I may,

00:25:54.690 --> 00:25:57.210
I'd just like to say I'm very grateful

00:25:57.210 --> 00:25:59.190
for a number of groups and networks

00:25:59.190 --> 00:26:02.250
that support me and support Jacqui

00:26:02.250 --> 00:26:03.900
as Chief Midwifery Officer,

00:26:03.900 --> 00:26:05.940
whether that's the CNO, CMO,

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:08.790
BME Strategic Advisory
Group, the Jabali Network,

00:26:08.790 --> 00:26:11.040
or, indeed, the executive directors,

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:13.860
because they, together with us,

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:16.650
and they will support
us and direct and guide

00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:20.040
and challenge us to
see how we can, indeed,

00:26:20.040 --> 00:26:22.440
combat racial discrimination.

00:26:22.440 --> 00:26:23.490
Thank you.

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:26.610
- Thank you, that was
important and well said.

00:26:26.610 --> 00:26:28.920
So can we go, I think, David,

00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:31.500
to you for the Operational
Performance Update?

00:26:31.500 --> 00:26:32.676
- Thank you.

00:26:32.676 --> 00:26:34.050
And throughout this, as I talk,

00:26:34.050 --> 00:26:37.980
I will be referring to Paper
4.1, which is the usual report,

00:26:37.980 --> 00:26:39.450
which I hope people find slightly

00:26:39.450 --> 00:26:40.740
more accessible this month.

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:42.960
There's a few less words
and a few more charts

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:46.020
and bits of data in
there, so, as requested.

00:26:46.020 --> 00:26:48.090
So, as Amanda has highlighted,

00:26:48.090 --> 00:26:50.790
we have continued to be
under significant pressure

00:26:50.790 --> 00:26:51.780
under recent months,

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:55.203
mostly due to continued demand
in the Urgent Care pathway.

00:26:56.040 --> 00:26:58.560
The peaking of a fifth wave of Covid,

00:26:58.560 --> 00:27:01.560
but also a series of, protracted series,

00:27:01.560 --> 00:27:03.210
of industrial action that has taken place

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:04.043
during recent months.

00:27:04.043 --> 00:27:05.850
So what I will do, Richard,

00:27:05.850 --> 00:27:08.040
I'll talk briefly about
Covid and the impact

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:09.600
that's had on performance,

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:11.160
then I'll hand over to
Navina to talk about

00:27:11.160 --> 00:27:13.380
where we are with the
Industrial Action in the round,

00:27:13.380 --> 00:27:15.600
then I will tell and share where we are

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:17.190
with our preparations for the forthcoming

00:27:17.190 --> 00:27:18.843
junior doctors action.

00:27:19.770 --> 00:27:21.729
So, in terms of Covid,

00:27:21.729 --> 00:27:24.780
it is worthy of note, and
I guess as we reflect,

00:27:24.780 --> 00:27:26.130
as we conclude a financial year,

00:27:26.130 --> 00:27:27.450
that we have indeed managed our way

00:27:27.450 --> 00:27:29.460
through five successive waves of Covid

00:27:29.460 --> 00:27:31.230
over the last 12 month period,

00:27:31.230 --> 00:27:33.120
and I would certainly
personally like to thank

00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:35.940
all of our staff out there who have really

00:27:35.940 --> 00:27:38.970
doggedly and determinedly
worked their way through this.

00:27:38.970 --> 00:27:41.490
The latest wave peaked
on the 23rd of February

00:27:41.490 --> 00:27:44.670
with 8,107 beds occupied.

00:27:44.670 --> 00:27:47.340
It's dipped down but it's
dipped back up again to 8,000.

00:27:47.340 --> 00:27:51.510
So we still have pressure,
pressure in the system.

00:27:51.510 --> 00:27:53.400
Also worthy of note in my report,

00:27:53.400 --> 00:27:55.320
that means the NHS has now treated

00:27:55.320 --> 00:27:57.570
one million patients with Covid-19.

00:27:57.570 --> 00:28:01.470
Again, I think a real
tribute to all of our staff.

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:04.170
In terms of the impact on performance,

00:28:04.170 --> 00:28:07.347
as Amanda said, I think we
are making real progress,

00:28:07.347 --> 00:28:09.330
and I won't dwell on all
aspects of the reports,

00:28:09.330 --> 00:28:12.150
but I will touch on urgent care,

00:28:12.150 --> 00:28:14.910
the elective recovery and cancer.

00:28:14.910 --> 00:28:17.430
So on urgent care, that's
covered in my report

00:28:17.430 --> 00:28:19.620
on paras 18....

00:28:19.620 --> 00:28:22.383
through 20, 29.

00:28:23.220 --> 00:28:25.740
Two things to highlight, firstly, again,

00:28:25.740 --> 00:28:29.460
in February we saw 1.9 million attendances

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:32.460
of the A&amp;E department,
which is 4.8% higher

00:28:32.460 --> 00:28:34.110
than the previous February.

00:28:34.110 --> 00:28:37.500
And we also saw 473 emergency admissions,

00:28:37.500 --> 00:28:39.870
2.8% higher than the previous year.

00:28:39.870 --> 00:28:42.570
So, again, real pressure in the system,

00:28:42.570 --> 00:28:44.340
but nonetheless, those patients who spent

00:28:44.340 --> 00:28:47.910
less than four hours in the
A&amp;E in February this year

00:28:47.910 --> 00:28:52.683
was 71.5% with a 2.5%
improvement on previous years.

00:28:54.090 --> 00:28:57.750
In the round on the urgent care,
in urgent care performance,

00:28:57.750 --> 00:29:00.447
the six week rolling average
at the moment about the,

00:29:00.447 --> 00:29:04.260
the 30 minute handover
is working at 37 minutes,

00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:06.990
whereas against the 95% A&amp;E target,

00:29:06.990 --> 00:29:10.590
the six weeks rolling average is 69.2%,

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:12.720
both of which stand us in good stead

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:14.820
as we move into the next financial year

00:29:14.820 --> 00:29:18.060
where that 30 minute
target will remain in situ,

00:29:18.060 --> 00:29:21.810
and also we will be asked
to achieve a 76% performance

00:29:21.810 --> 00:29:23.850
against the 95% standard.

00:29:23.850 --> 00:29:25.260
So I think real progress

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:28.200
in a very pressurised urgent care pathway,

00:29:28.200 --> 00:29:31.410
as we enter into the next financial year.

00:29:31.410 --> 00:29:34.680
On our performance
against 78 week waiters,

00:29:34.680 --> 00:29:37.310
you will again see the data...

00:29:38.280 --> 00:29:42.060
on page 13, para 1.2, which
is is that graph there

00:29:42.060 --> 00:29:45.840
and that chart, you can see,
is moving very, very healthily

00:29:45.840 --> 00:29:47.130
in the right direction

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:48.870
as we've worked our way through the year,

00:29:48.870 --> 00:29:51.330
and overall 78 week wait numbers are down,

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:54.120
are now down 80% since its peak.

00:29:54.120 --> 00:29:56.790
And we are still aiming
to all but eradicate them

00:29:56.790 --> 00:29:58.980
by the end of this, this financial year

00:29:58.980 --> 00:30:00.333
as we work our way through.

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:03.390
Finally, on this section on cancer,

00:30:03.390 --> 00:30:05.700
I think I would like to
draw further attention

00:30:05.700 --> 00:30:08.070
to the good news on the
BBC News this morning

00:30:08.070 --> 00:30:10.920
about the major new bowel trial

00:30:10.920 --> 00:30:13.710
with a blood test which can
detect traces of cancer cells

00:30:13.710 --> 00:30:15.360
that could spare thousands of patients

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:16.860
from chemotherapy every year.

00:30:16.860 --> 00:30:19.980
I mean, and again, incredibly promising

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:22.380
for future patients in the NHS,

00:30:22.380 --> 00:30:24.693
and, again, I think emphasise
of the world leading research

00:30:24.693 --> 00:30:26.580
that the UK does in this area,

00:30:26.580 --> 00:30:28.530
and I think something we should reflect on

00:30:28.530 --> 00:30:30.120
very, very proudly.

00:30:30.120 --> 00:30:32.640
But, meanwhile, we are
still very, very focused

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:34.290
on the waiting list and those waiting

00:30:34.290 --> 00:30:36.300
beyond 62 weeks for their cancer.

00:30:36.300 --> 00:30:37.890
And, again, we are making progress,

00:30:37.890 --> 00:30:39.660
it's heading in the right direction.

00:30:39.660 --> 00:30:42.480
That list has been reduced
by 15,000 patients,

00:30:42.480 --> 00:30:46.830
which takes us now down to
around 19,000 as we end,

00:30:46.830 --> 00:30:49.230
to the end of this financial year.

00:30:49.230 --> 00:30:50.250
I wasn't going to touch

00:30:50.250 --> 00:30:52.560
on any other areas of
performance, Richard,

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:54.600
it's all there in the report to read,

00:30:54.600 --> 00:30:56.040
and if people are comfortable,

00:30:56.040 --> 00:30:58.620
I will hand over to Navina
to give the headline

00:30:58.620 --> 00:31:01.110
on where we are with industrial action.

00:31:01.110 --> 00:31:02.143
- Yes, okay.

00:31:02.143 --> 00:31:02.976
- Navina?

00:31:02.976 --> 00:31:03.809
- Yep, just very quickly, the headline.

00:31:03.809 --> 00:31:07.800
So, for most of our trade unions,

00:31:07.800 --> 00:31:11.400
we are awaiting the results of the ballot

00:31:11.400 --> 00:31:15.120
on the offer for the settlement

00:31:15.120 --> 00:31:16.500
with the junior doctors.

00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:19.950
We are learning an understanding impact

00:31:19.950 --> 00:31:22.950
from the previous three
day industrial action.

00:31:22.950 --> 00:31:25.200
We want to take this opportunity to thank

00:31:25.200 --> 00:31:29.280
all of our colleagues for
managing during that time,

00:31:29.280 --> 00:31:31.140
before and afterwards.

00:31:31.140 --> 00:31:34.620
We have another period of
four day action coming up

00:31:34.620 --> 00:31:36.030
during the Easter break.

00:31:36.030 --> 00:31:39.450
So we are taking the
learning from the last time

00:31:39.450 --> 00:31:41.880
but also understanding
it's different this time.

00:31:41.880 --> 00:31:46.680
And so our, all of our services
are planning and preparing

00:31:46.680 --> 00:31:49.320
for how they would manage
to cope to run services

00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:50.493
as best they can.

00:31:51.360 --> 00:31:54.480
I think the main message is that

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:57.120
we continue to work in partnership,

00:31:57.120 --> 00:31:59.790
with everybody concerned,

00:31:59.790 --> 00:32:02.610
to make sure that we can
keep running NHS services

00:32:02.610 --> 00:32:04.410
as safely as possible.

00:32:04.410 --> 00:32:08.220
And the main focus for
the planning this time

00:32:08.220 --> 00:32:09.930
is on local planning,

00:32:09.930 --> 00:32:12.570
because there will be a
lot of local variation

00:32:12.570 --> 00:32:14.940
and it's really important
that we understand that

00:32:14.940 --> 00:32:18.900
for our services, for how our
services support each other,

00:32:18.900 --> 00:32:20.700
depending on the local arrangements,

00:32:20.700 --> 00:32:23.220
and also making sure that the communities

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:26.520
and local populations
understand what's going on

00:32:26.520 --> 00:32:28.530
over that period of time.

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:29.490
David.

00:32:29.490 --> 00:32:32.040
- Thank you, so just to, again,
to sum up where we are here.

00:32:32.040 --> 00:32:34.620
So, progress has been made with an offer

00:32:34.620 --> 00:32:37.110
to the agenda for change staff

00:32:37.110 --> 00:32:39.840
and that's been endorsed
by the NHS Staff Council,

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:43.080
meaning that industrial
action has been paused, yeah?

00:32:43.080 --> 00:32:45.360
That is not, however, the
case with the junior doctors,

00:32:45.360 --> 00:32:47.070
and I think it's really
important that we understand

00:32:47.070 --> 00:32:50.220
what junior doctors do and
what junior doctors are.

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:52.140
So junior doctors make up about half

00:32:52.140 --> 00:32:54.000
of all doctors in the NHS.

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:56.250
They are qualified
doctors who have anywhere

00:32:56.250 --> 00:32:59.550
up to eight years experience
working as a hospital doctor,

00:32:59.550 --> 00:33:00.810
depending on their specialty,

00:33:00.810 --> 00:33:02.850
or up to three years in general practise.

00:33:02.850 --> 00:33:05.580
So these people who make
a material contribution

00:33:05.580 --> 00:33:08.962
to patient safety on a day-to-day basis.

00:33:08.962 --> 00:33:11.310
The BMA and government did meet last week,

00:33:11.310 --> 00:33:13.020
as has been well publicised,

00:33:13.020 --> 00:33:15.330
but this did not progress
to intensive talks

00:33:15.330 --> 00:33:16.950
to resolve the dispute.

00:33:16.950 --> 00:33:18.960
So, as such, and you'll be aware,

00:33:18.960 --> 00:33:23.100
that on the 24th of March the
BMA announced a 96 hour strike

00:33:23.100 --> 00:33:25.260
from the 11th to the 15th of April,

00:33:25.260 --> 00:33:27.090
which is the days immediately following

00:33:27.090 --> 00:33:29.073
the Easter bank holiday weekend.

00:33:30.990 --> 00:33:33.660
This industrial action
is set to be the biggest

00:33:33.660 --> 00:33:36.753
and most disruptive strike in NHS history.

00:33:37.950 --> 00:33:40.470
So during the strike, we
will prioritise resources

00:33:40.470 --> 00:33:43.680
to protect emergency
treatment, critical care,

00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:46.320
maternity, neonatal care and trauma,

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:48.510
and that will be our absolute priority.

00:33:48.510 --> 00:33:51.141
But the scale and length
of its industrial action

00:33:51.141 --> 00:33:53.400
means that we cannot fully mitigate

00:33:53.400 --> 00:33:55.200
the risk of patient harm.

00:33:55.200 --> 00:33:57.360
So I would not want
colleagues to underestimate

00:33:57.360 --> 00:34:00.420
the impact of this industrial action

00:34:00.420 --> 00:34:02.880
will potentially have on our patients.

00:34:02.880 --> 00:34:06.450
So we have set up the
usual machine to ensure

00:34:06.450 --> 00:34:10.200
that we oversee the preparations
for the industrial action.

00:34:10.200 --> 00:34:12.270
It will mean some probably changes

00:34:12.270 --> 00:34:15.360
to whether organising services
to ensure patient safety,

00:34:15.360 --> 00:34:16.860
but I don't think we should underestimate

00:34:16.860 --> 00:34:19.833
the potential impact of
the action that week.

00:34:21.600 --> 00:34:22.433
- Thank you.

00:34:22.433 --> 00:34:25.320
Are there any comments or question,

00:34:25.320 --> 00:34:26.250
sorry, excuse me.

00:34:26.250 --> 00:34:29.490
Comments or questions
for David on the report,

00:34:29.490 --> 00:34:30.440
the broader report?

00:34:31.320 --> 00:34:32.703
Any comments or questions?

00:34:33.810 --> 00:34:35.258
Wol, you?

00:34:35.258 --> 00:34:36.091
Andrew, sorry.

00:34:36.091 --> 00:34:37.890
- I just wanted to make a point around

00:34:37.890 --> 00:34:41.400
performance for the
elective recovery work.

00:34:41.400 --> 00:34:45.090
I mean, if you go back
to April of last year,

00:34:45.090 --> 00:34:49.830
we had 1.2 million people
waiting 78 weeks or more

00:34:49.830 --> 00:34:51.330
on our waiting list.

00:34:51.330 --> 00:34:53.340
We now will have, at
the end of this month,

00:34:53.340 --> 00:34:55.590
about 11,000 people.

00:34:55.590 --> 00:34:57.510
And I think we should
pay tribute, Richard,

00:34:57.510 --> 00:35:01.230
to the immense effort that
our NHS staff have put into

00:35:01.230 --> 00:35:03.780
reducing waiting times, and trust boards,

00:35:03.780 --> 00:35:04.920
in focusing on this,

00:35:04.920 --> 00:35:06.810
given that this is probably,

00:35:06.810 --> 00:35:08.670
this financial year has
probably been the worst

00:35:08.670 --> 00:35:10.770
in terms of disruptions that we've had.

00:35:10.770 --> 00:35:13.320
Covid, we've had the
worst winter on record,

00:35:13.320 --> 00:35:14.970
we've had staffing challenges,

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:16.890
we've now got industrial action.

00:35:16.890 --> 00:35:19.140
So to see this level of progress,

00:35:19.140 --> 00:35:21.510
I mean, it takes me back
to the Tony Blair days,

00:35:21.510 --> 00:35:25.470
the early reforms in, you
know, turn of the century

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:28.470
where we've made these kind of changes.

00:35:28.470 --> 00:35:30.330
They're, they're pretty seismic.

00:35:30.330 --> 00:35:33.900
So I think we should pay
tribute and thank our staff

00:35:33.900 --> 00:35:36.933
on the front line here.

00:35:38.100 --> 00:35:40.620
And we've also got the
challenge going forward

00:35:40.620 --> 00:35:43.050
around delivering 65 weeks,

00:35:43.050 --> 00:35:46.710
and we've got some good funding
from government to do this

00:35:46.710 --> 00:35:48.990
and I'm sure we can
make some real progress,

00:35:48.990 --> 00:35:51.690
'cause waiting times,
along with cancer waits,

00:35:51.690 --> 00:35:53.940
are probably the two significant things

00:35:53.940 --> 00:35:57.030
that most people have in terms of,

00:35:57.030 --> 00:35:59.130
you know, concerns around NHS performance.

00:35:59.130 --> 00:36:03.060
So I think the NHS has done
really, really well on this

00:36:03.060 --> 00:36:05.340
given all the other issues
that it's had to face.

00:36:05.340 --> 00:36:06.780
- So I'll bring Wol in in a moment,

00:36:06.780 --> 00:36:09.060
but I would just endorse that,

00:36:09.060 --> 00:36:12.750
I mean, thanks and appreciation
to the huge hard work

00:36:12.750 --> 00:36:15.843
of all the NHS staff, actually,
who've delivered that.

00:36:16.770 --> 00:36:19.590
Clearly, we don't stop
now, we don't stop here.

00:36:19.590 --> 00:36:22.410
We've got good support from
government and we've got funding

00:36:22.410 --> 00:36:23.640
and we're going to keep on going

00:36:23.640 --> 00:36:25.860
and we're going to keep on driving these,

00:36:25.860 --> 00:36:27.990
driving this elective backlog down.

00:36:27.990 --> 00:36:29.280
But, actually, you are quite right

00:36:29.280 --> 00:36:31.660
to think to highlight
from that 1.2 million

00:36:33.225 --> 00:36:36.999
a few months ago down to 11,000
at the end of this month.

00:36:36.999 --> 00:36:39.020
That's a remarkable shortening, I think,

00:36:39.020 --> 00:36:40.860
of the list at the longer end.

00:36:40.860 --> 00:36:43.800
But there's more to do,
actually, in general

00:36:43.800 --> 00:36:45.480
on the stats you were giving us David.

00:36:45.480 --> 00:36:48.990
I think, actually, what we
do see as we sit here today

00:36:48.990 --> 00:36:50.790
is we see encouraging progress,

00:36:50.790 --> 00:36:53.130
really encouraging progress actually,

00:36:53.130 --> 00:36:54.870
but clearly there's there's further to go.

00:36:54.870 --> 00:36:55.987
- Yep.
- Yeah.

00:36:55.987 --> 00:36:57.300
- Wol, so--
- David,

00:36:57.300 --> 00:36:59.010
if I might return you to the strikes,

00:36:59.010 --> 00:37:02.400
and you talked very well about the focus

00:37:02.400 --> 00:37:04.890
you're going to have
on the upcoming strike,

00:37:04.890 --> 00:37:08.850
and I'm just wondering about
the impact on other initiatives

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:10.530
because we're pressing very hard in terms

00:37:10.530 --> 00:37:12.840
of Primary Care Access,
I think Amanda mentioned,

00:37:12.840 --> 00:37:14.634
pressing very hard on
Urgent Emergency Care,

00:37:14.634 --> 00:37:16.200
there's a whole range
of things we're doing.

00:37:16.200 --> 00:37:19.260
I mean, there's only so
much bandwidth you have,

00:37:19.260 --> 00:37:20.460
and the system has.

00:37:20.460 --> 00:37:22.200
Do you want to just say a
little bit more about that?

00:37:22.200 --> 00:37:24.120
- Sure, just to reflect the few things.

00:37:24.120 --> 00:37:27.810
First of all, I would
say our absolute priority

00:37:27.810 --> 00:37:29.490
always is patient safety.

00:37:29.490 --> 00:37:33.090
That's what we will put
before anything else.

00:37:33.090 --> 00:37:35.370
In terms of the trade offs you make,

00:37:35.370 --> 00:37:37.620
clearly nobody wants
to cancel the patient,

00:37:37.620 --> 00:37:39.690
nobody wants to cancel
an outpatient appointment

00:37:39.690 --> 00:37:41.820
or an inpatient appointment
or an elected procedure.

00:37:41.820 --> 00:37:43.890
When I used to run a
hospital back in the day,

00:37:43.890 --> 00:37:46.710
the thing I used to hate most
was cancelling a patient,

00:37:46.710 --> 00:37:49.050
and particularly those very rare times

00:37:49.050 --> 00:37:50.430
when you used to have to cancel a patient

00:37:50.430 --> 00:37:51.510
on the day of surgery.

00:37:51.510 --> 00:37:55.170
It used to be make me
physically ache, you know,

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:57.210
the stuff they'd organise for themselves,

00:37:57.210 --> 00:37:58.530
or their families, or their children's,

00:37:58.530 --> 00:38:00.030
let alone the trauma

00:38:00.030 --> 00:38:02.580
that people will be going
through and the anxiety.

00:38:02.580 --> 00:38:04.800
But, nonetheless, it may be that we may

00:38:04.800 --> 00:38:07.530
have to stand down some elective activity,

00:38:07.530 --> 00:38:08.820
particularly, I think, during this week

00:38:08.820 --> 00:38:11.820
of the junior doctors
strike that is inevitable.

00:38:11.820 --> 00:38:14.460
And so these are difficult trade-offs

00:38:14.460 --> 00:38:16.170
which we will need to continue to make,

00:38:16.170 --> 00:38:17.880
and they will have an impact

00:38:17.880 --> 00:38:20.340
on one set of targets or another.

00:38:20.340 --> 00:38:22.050
We will determinedly try to catch up

00:38:22.050 --> 00:38:24.540
and I think we've done
really well so far, actually.

00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:27.870
But I also wouldn't underestimate the,

00:38:27.870 --> 00:38:30.360
the emotional fatigue,

00:38:30.360 --> 00:38:32.640
actually, out there
with the frontline staff

00:38:32.640 --> 00:38:34.230
and those managers who are running around

00:38:34.230 --> 00:38:35.160
organising this staff.

00:38:35.160 --> 00:38:38.433
It's really hard yards and it
is not sustainable over time.

00:38:39.660 --> 00:38:40.620
- Okay.

00:38:40.620 --> 00:38:41.453
Steve?

00:38:41.453 --> 00:38:42.810
- Yes, so I'd endorse everything

00:38:42.810 --> 00:38:45.330
that Amanda, David, and Navina have said

00:38:45.330 --> 00:38:47.280
concerning industrial action.

00:38:47.280 --> 00:38:51.150
But our message to the public,
in terms of use of services,

00:38:51.150 --> 00:38:54.870
will be very similar to the
message that we have given

00:38:54.870 --> 00:38:56.730
during all the days of industrial action

00:38:56.730 --> 00:38:58.140
that we've seen since December,

00:38:58.140 --> 00:39:00.660
which is to do what we've asked,

00:39:00.660 --> 00:39:04.110
which is to use services
wisely and appropriately.

00:39:04.110 --> 00:39:07.680
So do, for emergencies, for
life-threatening emergencies,

00:39:07.680 --> 00:39:09.630
do still use those emergency services.

00:39:09.630 --> 00:39:12.270
We are prioritising
them, as David has said,

00:39:12.270 --> 00:39:15.320
but for other non-life
threatening emergent...

00:39:16.800 --> 00:39:19.890
conditions then 111, 111 online

00:39:19.890 --> 00:39:21.900
should be the first port of call.

00:39:21.900 --> 00:39:24.330
Other services, although there
will be widespread impact

00:39:24.330 --> 00:39:26.130
because, of course, junior doctors do work

00:39:26.130 --> 00:39:28.590
at other parts of the
system other than hospitals.

00:39:28.590 --> 00:39:29.790
Remember, as David has said,

00:39:29.790 --> 00:39:33.633
that junior doctors are key
professional members of teams,

00:39:34.549 --> 00:39:38.670
and although we will be putting in support

00:39:38.670 --> 00:39:41.580
to ensure that those
services can continue,

00:39:41.580 --> 00:39:42.480
there will be an impact.

00:39:42.480 --> 00:39:44.490
So do use services wisely.

00:39:44.490 --> 00:39:45.750
And we're incredibly grateful,

00:39:45.750 --> 00:39:47.370
because we have seen over the course

00:39:47.370 --> 00:39:49.590
of the months since December,

00:39:49.590 --> 00:39:52.110
the public have responded
to those messages

00:39:52.110 --> 00:39:55.580
and those messages will
undoubtedly be going out again

00:39:55.580 --> 00:39:58.080
in the days up to industrial action.

00:39:58.080 --> 00:39:59.730
- Thank you.

00:39:59.730 --> 00:40:02.100
Any other comments or questions?

00:40:02.100 --> 00:40:04.080
And if not, okay, thank,
thank you very much.

00:40:04.080 --> 00:40:05.490
Thanks, Steve.

00:40:05.490 --> 00:40:06.813
Julian, finance update?

00:40:07.860 --> 00:40:09.570
- So, again, I, as normal,

00:40:09.570 --> 00:40:11.483
I assume people have read the paper.

00:40:11.483 --> 00:40:14.850
I mean, in one sense, on 22-23,

00:40:14.850 --> 00:40:15.990
there's not a huge amount to add

00:40:15.990 --> 00:40:17.160
from the last board meeting.

00:40:17.160 --> 00:40:20.520
We are basically, as I said at the time,

00:40:20.520 --> 00:40:23.610
bringing the budget in, I
mean pretty much on the nail.

00:40:23.610 --> 00:40:25.770
The board paid per county
has an underspend forecast

00:40:25.770 --> 00:40:27.750
for the end of the year of 125 million,

00:40:27.750 --> 00:40:30.600
of which around a hundred
million of that is

00:40:30.600 --> 00:40:33.360
a ring-fenced vaccine spending,

00:40:33.360 --> 00:40:34.800
which actually is
testament to the great job

00:40:34.800 --> 00:40:36.900
that Steve and his team have done

00:40:36.900 --> 00:40:38.160
in terms of the booster campaigns

00:40:38.160 --> 00:40:39.810
with the money that's been given.

00:40:39.810 --> 00:40:43.830
So it is tight but we are on
track to bring the budget in

00:40:43.830 --> 00:40:46.023
on revenue and capital.

00:40:48.960 --> 00:40:52.590
Right now, the focus is
on 23-24 and planning.

00:40:52.590 --> 00:40:56.430
We are getting the kind of
next draught of plans back

00:40:56.430 --> 00:40:59.070
over the course of today and tomorrow.

00:40:59.070 --> 00:41:00.870
Good, steady progress being made,

00:41:00.870 --> 00:41:04.020
but a lot of work still
to be done with systems,

00:41:04.020 --> 00:41:07.590
with providers to make
sure we've got plans

00:41:07.590 --> 00:41:11.250
that properly balance
performance, money and quality.

00:41:11.250 --> 00:41:14.733
So that's really the focus of
the next kind of four weeks.

00:41:16.020 --> 00:41:18.093
Clearly an issue is pay.

00:41:20.820 --> 00:41:23.400
The government and unions
have reached an agreement,

00:41:23.400 --> 00:41:25.170
staff are balloting, we will see where,

00:41:25.170 --> 00:41:28.050
you know, how that turns out.

00:41:28.050 --> 00:41:30.900
Just though to reflect for
the purposes of the money,

00:41:30.900 --> 00:41:32.430
the government and the
treasury have been clear

00:41:32.430 --> 00:41:35.103
that they are committed
to providing new funding,

00:41:36.330 --> 00:41:38.340
and that...

00:41:38.340 --> 00:41:40.233
there will be no, sort of,

00:41:41.820 --> 00:41:45.243
impact on frontline services
and quality of care.

00:41:46.200 --> 00:41:50.010
Therefore, whilst we will no
doubt in the next few weeks

00:41:50.010 --> 00:41:53.280
be in discussion with the
department and the treasury

00:41:53.280 --> 00:41:56.520
as to precisely how that is going to work,

00:41:56.520 --> 00:41:59.340
we are asking systems
clearly to plan on the basis

00:41:59.340 --> 00:42:01.710
that the pay award will be fully funded

00:42:01.710 --> 00:42:04.792
on top of the allocations
that we have given them,

00:42:04.792 --> 00:42:08.400
given the stretching efficiency
and performance targets

00:42:08.400 --> 00:42:10.890
we are asking them to plan for this year

00:42:10.890 --> 00:42:13.890
and the government's
commitment that, you know,

00:42:13.890 --> 00:42:15.600
funding the new pay deal will not impact

00:42:15.600 --> 00:42:18.330
on frontline services and quality of care.

00:42:18.330 --> 00:42:20.400
And, you know, look, we recognise actually

00:42:20.400 --> 00:42:22.170
the way the government has
responded to the sort of

00:42:22.170 --> 00:42:26.103
changing circumstances as as
they've evolved over the last,

00:42:27.360 --> 00:42:28.983
over the last couple of years.

00:42:29.970 --> 00:42:33.100
So, you know, we're just asking people,

00:42:33.100 --> 00:42:36.273
focus on the job, sort the plans,

00:42:37.170 --> 00:42:40.770
the pay will be appropriately funded

00:42:40.770 --> 00:42:42.400
and that's where we are.

00:42:42.400 --> 00:42:43.413
- Okay, thank you.

00:42:44.940 --> 00:42:46.713
Comments, questions for Julian?

00:42:48.360 --> 00:42:50.223
Do you have a perspective on,

00:42:51.300 --> 00:42:53.700
I mean, it's the first year of the ICBs,

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:56.789
and so actually they're
as sort of a new dynamic

00:42:56.789 --> 00:42:58.260
in the piece,

00:42:58.260 --> 00:43:00.210
although they come from, you
know, they come from history,

00:43:00.210 --> 00:43:01.645
but there's a new dynamic in the piece.

00:43:01.645 --> 00:43:03.312
How do you see that?

00:43:04.859 --> 00:43:05.883
- So,

00:43:05.883 --> 00:43:06.716
look...

00:43:07.937 --> 00:43:09.570
I think as I said in the private board,

00:43:09.570 --> 00:43:11.190
the context is clearly both

00:43:11.190 --> 00:43:13.607
the first full year of ICB operation.

00:43:13.607 --> 00:43:15.330
- [Richard] Right.

00:43:15.330 --> 00:43:16.890
- I think Steve has
previously described one

00:43:16.890 --> 00:43:19.620
sort of a five year journey through Covid,

00:43:19.620 --> 00:43:20.610
and this is almost like,

00:43:20.610 --> 00:43:23.010
I dunno if it's year
two and a half or three,

00:43:23.010 --> 00:43:25.290
so just recognising
that we are still trying

00:43:25.290 --> 00:43:29.040
to take a lot of cost out
as we come out of Covid,

00:43:29.040 --> 00:43:33.510
as we drive performance improvements,

00:43:33.510 --> 00:43:37.650
actually in just about every care setting

00:43:37.650 --> 00:43:39.450
and the first year of ICB.

00:43:39.450 --> 00:43:44.450
So there is a lot for
both ICBs and providers

00:43:44.520 --> 00:43:48.393
to sort of get their heads
round and to plan for.

00:43:49.470 --> 00:43:53.937
Look, you can see that some
of the ICBs with providers

00:43:53.937 --> 00:43:55.800
are actually doing a really good job--

00:43:55.800 --> 00:43:56.633
- [Richard] Yeah.

00:43:56.633 --> 00:43:58.440
- Oh this, and I think we're pretty close

00:43:58.440 --> 00:44:00.060
with a whole number of
them to having plans

00:44:00.060 --> 00:44:03.810
which are sorted and settled
and we can just get on.

00:44:03.810 --> 00:44:06.540
There are clearly some who, you know,

00:44:06.540 --> 00:44:09.510
there are genuine sort of
underlying issues as we,

00:44:09.510 --> 00:44:11.550
you know, sort of look
at the position in 19-20,

00:44:11.550 --> 00:44:14.670
how it's evolved through
the last three years that,

00:44:14.670 --> 00:44:18.030
you know, as we come
through last year into this,

00:44:18.030 --> 00:44:20.588
we're going to have
some issues to resolve,

00:44:20.588 --> 00:44:22.637
and in some places,

00:44:22.637 --> 00:44:24.627
you know, the relationships
are working really well

00:44:24.627 --> 00:44:27.450
and some people are
still learning, you know,

00:44:27.450 --> 00:44:28.740
how they operate.
- Yeah.

00:44:28.740 --> 00:44:31.810
- So I suppose it's slightly
long-winded way of me saying

00:44:32.700 --> 00:44:36.260
it's a really challenging context and...

00:44:37.620 --> 00:44:40.610
some are, you know, stronger set of...

00:44:41.640 --> 00:44:44.070
relationships working really well,

00:44:44.070 --> 00:44:47.070
you can see everyone making
the effort, and in some,

00:44:47.070 --> 00:44:50.010
both NHSE, providers, systems,

00:44:50.010 --> 00:44:52.230
I just think we're going
to have more work to do

00:44:52.230 --> 00:44:54.600
over the next four to six weeks

00:44:54.600 --> 00:44:57.480
to make sure everyone
is in the right place.

00:44:57.480 --> 00:45:00.180
But I have to say, you see the importance

00:45:00.180 --> 00:45:02.700
of having really good
integrated care boards.

00:45:02.700 --> 00:45:05.190
Because where they're
operating really well,

00:45:05.190 --> 00:45:08.940
the ability to actually see
across all the providers,

00:45:08.940 --> 00:45:11.730
actually work out where
the right trade offs are,

00:45:11.730 --> 00:45:15.360
where the right capacity is,
where they do the investment,

00:45:15.360 --> 00:45:18.450
where it's working well, you
can see why we need them.

00:45:18.450 --> 00:45:19.283
- Very good.

00:45:19.283 --> 00:45:20.116
Thank you, Julian.

00:45:21.510 --> 00:45:23.760
Right, we are going to go on, I think,

00:45:23.760 --> 00:45:26.100
it's item five on my agenda,

00:45:26.100 --> 00:45:31.100
so it's NHS Innovation
Research and Life Sciences.

00:45:31.170 --> 00:45:32.970
When I said earlier on about,

00:45:32.970 --> 00:45:34.230
you know, what the board should keep

00:45:34.230 --> 00:45:36.000
certain strategic priorities

00:45:36.000 --> 00:45:37.350
absolutely at the front of its mind,

00:45:37.350 --> 00:45:40.800
whilst at the same time
challenging the exec

00:45:40.800 --> 00:45:44.220
and working with the executive
on the immediate pressures,

00:45:44.220 --> 00:45:47.100
this focal point is a really important,

00:45:47.100 --> 00:45:49.440
I think, strategic mindset to have.

00:45:49.440 --> 00:45:53.040
Amanda has explained to me
on a number of occasions now,

00:45:53.040 --> 00:45:54.090
and quite rightly, actually,

00:45:54.090 --> 00:45:57.540
that the NHS never stops
evolving in any event.

00:45:57.540 --> 00:46:00.000
And the NHS today is a very different NHS

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:04.500
than it was 10 years or 15
years ago or 25 years ago.

00:46:04.500 --> 00:46:06.510
And you think about the scale of provision

00:46:06.510 --> 00:46:08.670
and the range of different
conditions, actually,

00:46:08.670 --> 00:46:11.430
that the NHS now can treat and does treat

00:46:11.430 --> 00:46:14.280
and the way actually we
interact, user technology,

00:46:14.280 --> 00:46:16.770
the NHS continues to evolve

00:46:16.770 --> 00:46:19.650
and that is because of innovation

00:46:19.650 --> 00:46:22.200
and bring those innovations
I think into play.

00:46:22.200 --> 00:46:23.820
So this is a really important discussion

00:46:23.820 --> 00:46:25.080
I think we're going to have.

00:46:25.080 --> 00:46:27.090
And, Tim, I think you
are going to lead off

00:46:27.090 --> 00:46:28.170
and I think Matt's here,

00:46:28.170 --> 00:46:30.326
Matt Whitty is here as
well, which is great.

00:46:30.326 --> 00:46:31.200
Thank you, Tim.

00:46:31.200 --> 00:46:34.020
- Thank you very much, Chair,
and it's a real pleasure

00:46:34.020 --> 00:46:36.570
to have the opportunity
to present the paper

00:46:36.570 --> 00:46:40.890
and have Matt here for this discussion.

00:46:40.890 --> 00:46:44.370
So, as you said, "Innovation,
Research, Life Sciences,"

00:46:44.370 --> 00:46:47.700
I'm going to just pause on
those words for a second

00:46:47.700 --> 00:46:50.550
because they are really,
really important words

00:46:50.550 --> 00:46:52.533
for the future of the NHS,

00:46:53.400 --> 00:46:57.390
and as Amanda Pritchard
frequently points out

00:46:57.390 --> 00:47:02.390
in both her external addresses
and her internal addresses,

00:47:02.850 --> 00:47:06.390
we need to be really good

00:47:06.390 --> 00:47:10.470
at the Innovation, Research
and Life Sciences ecosystem

00:47:10.470 --> 00:47:12.750
for the patients that we serve

00:47:12.750 --> 00:47:15.030
and for the staff to deliver--

00:47:15.030 --> 00:47:17.580
- Yeah.
- The very best of care.

00:47:17.580 --> 00:47:19.590
Bringing together, first of all,

00:47:19.590 --> 00:47:22.020
bringing together
improvement, digital and data,

00:47:22.020 --> 00:47:26.520
with the Innovation, Research
and Life Sciences group

00:47:26.520 --> 00:47:28.320
in the transformation directorate,

00:47:28.320 --> 00:47:33.320
I think was a really bold and
strong move several years ago,

00:47:34.200 --> 00:47:36.270
because actually those things

00:47:36.270 --> 00:47:38.550
are mutually supportive of each other.

00:47:38.550 --> 00:47:43.110
It is, in fact, the digital
and data with the innovation

00:47:43.110 --> 00:47:46.920
and the improvement, all of those together

00:47:46.920 --> 00:47:51.510
are levers that are much
stronger when they work together

00:47:51.510 --> 00:47:54.750
rather than as individual
separate activities,

00:47:54.750 --> 00:47:56.310
which I will say, historically,

00:47:56.310 --> 00:47:59.760
they were parcelled out
into individual silos.

00:47:59.760 --> 00:48:02.400
We are already experiencing the benefits

00:48:02.400 --> 00:48:06.240
of having them work
together on a shorter list

00:48:06.240 --> 00:48:07.773
of high priorities.

00:48:08.670 --> 00:48:12.360
So, I also want to point out
that bringing us all together

00:48:12.360 --> 00:48:15.870
has enabled closer
collaboration with key partners,

00:48:15.870 --> 00:48:20.070
and the partnership opportunities
here are really important.

00:48:20.070 --> 00:48:21.423
The Department of Health,

00:48:24.390 --> 00:48:27.000
OLS, NICE, MHRA

00:48:27.000 --> 00:48:28.620
and HIR,

00:48:28.620 --> 00:48:31.590
these are really critical that we have,

00:48:31.590 --> 00:48:35.430
the NHS is able to work in partnership

00:48:35.430 --> 00:48:37.620
with the entire ecosystem here,

00:48:37.620 --> 00:48:39.480
because we are not a,

00:48:39.480 --> 00:48:41.220
actually, we are the,

00:48:41.220 --> 00:48:42.600
we're the big,

00:48:42.600 --> 00:48:46.290
we're the gorilla in this organisation,

00:48:46.290 --> 00:48:51.030
but actually we depend
entirely on close collaboration

00:48:51.030 --> 00:48:53.130
with all those organisations and more.

00:48:53.130 --> 00:48:56.670
And can I just say that our
organisational partnerships

00:48:56.670 --> 00:48:58.920
are functioning really, really well.

00:48:58.920 --> 00:49:00.480
Always room for improvement,

00:49:00.480 --> 00:49:03.870
but we've seen quite a bit of improvement

00:49:03.870 --> 00:49:05.910
under, actually, Matt's leadership

00:49:05.910 --> 00:49:09.330
with the Accelerated Access Collaborative.

00:49:09.330 --> 00:49:11.460
I would also say that our commitment

00:49:11.460 --> 00:49:14.850
to supporting the Testing
Adoption and Spread of Innovation.

00:49:14.850 --> 00:49:18.150
Innovation comes from both
the inside of the NHS,

00:49:18.150 --> 00:49:21.420
and my Fridays, which I cherish,

00:49:21.420 --> 00:49:24.690
where I go to various places in the NHS

00:49:24.690 --> 00:49:27.060
and see the innovation that is occurring,

00:49:27.060 --> 00:49:29.046
it is astounding.

00:49:29.046 --> 00:49:32.430
I don't think it's too much
of an exaggeration to say

00:49:32.430 --> 00:49:37.430
that the NHS has actually solved
most of its major problems

00:49:38.070 --> 00:49:40.350
in one place or another.

00:49:40.350 --> 00:49:44.274
Our key challenge is
spreading those innovations

00:49:44.274 --> 00:49:48.030
and having the ecosystem
that's required to spread that.

00:49:48.030 --> 00:49:50.820
But I also don't want to
de-emphasize the importance

00:49:50.820 --> 00:49:52.620
of external partnerships.

00:49:52.620 --> 00:49:56.700
So it is actually our
commercial partnerships in

00:49:56.700 --> 00:50:01.290
the MedTech areas, the
DataTech areas, BioTech areas.

00:50:01.290 --> 00:50:04.380
We get so much better when we are able

00:50:04.380 --> 00:50:07.410
to rapidly bring those commercial,

00:50:07.410 --> 00:50:11.100
honestly, those commercial
companies into partnership

00:50:11.100 --> 00:50:14.130
where we can spread the
innovations and benefits

00:50:14.130 --> 00:50:18.600
that they are providing to
our patients and citizens.

00:50:18.600 --> 00:50:20.760
Our commitment to being full partners

00:50:20.760 --> 00:50:22.950
with the academic and life sciences

00:50:22.950 --> 00:50:24.900
is really, really important.

00:50:24.900 --> 00:50:26.390
And the paper...

00:50:27.240 --> 00:50:30.540
describes this, and Matt will
go into a little more detail.

00:50:30.540 --> 00:50:33.390
And I am also very
pleased to announce that

00:50:33.390 --> 00:50:36.450
a new member of our team, Roland
Sinker, the Chief Executive

00:50:36.450 --> 00:50:39.480
at Cambridge University
Hospital Foundation Trust,

00:50:39.480 --> 00:50:41.730
has agreed to join our team,

00:50:41.730 --> 00:50:44.880
and this is, I think, a terrific addition.

00:50:44.880 --> 00:50:48.390
He has enormous experience
and expertise in this area

00:50:48.390 --> 00:50:51.000
and we're very pleased
that that announcement

00:50:51.000 --> 00:50:52.560
went out earlier today.

00:50:52.560 --> 00:50:57.150
So with that as an introduction is,

00:50:57.150 --> 00:51:00.330
with your permission, Chair,
I'll turn it over to Matt.

00:51:00.330 --> 00:51:03.090
- Thank you Tim and thank
you, thank you, Chair.

00:51:03.090 --> 00:51:05.850
I think you've mentioned
the strategic importance

00:51:05.850 --> 00:51:06.870
really neatly there, Tim,

00:51:06.870 --> 00:51:09.180
and actually sort of our
role in the whole ecosystem

00:51:09.180 --> 00:51:12.180
for driving forward both
research and innovation,

00:51:12.180 --> 00:51:13.770
as well as the importance
this actually makes

00:51:13.770 --> 00:51:16.860
to patient care and patient outcomes.

00:51:16.860 --> 00:51:18.300
I've spoken to the board previously about

00:51:18.300 --> 00:51:20.400
the five priorities that were agreed

00:51:20.400 --> 00:51:22.140
at the Accelerated Access
Collaborative Board,

00:51:22.140 --> 00:51:23.640
which brings together many of the partners

00:51:23.640 --> 00:51:25.140
and others that you, that you discussed.

00:51:25.140 --> 00:51:27.870
So I'll quickly run through
those five priorities

00:51:27.870 --> 00:51:29.730
and some of the updates since we last met.

00:51:29.730 --> 00:51:31.020
And then I'll talk a little bit more about

00:51:31.020 --> 00:51:33.330
some of the future work that
we'll be looking at with Roland

00:51:33.330 --> 00:51:36.810
in order to make further
progress because of the,

00:51:36.810 --> 00:51:39.900
because the challenges that we see ahead.

00:51:39.900 --> 00:51:42.450
So the first priority that
the AAC board has agreed

00:51:42.450 --> 00:51:47.220
is increasing the scale,
diversity, and speed of research.

00:51:47.220 --> 00:51:50.340
This is really, really important
to patients in the NHS.

00:51:50.340 --> 00:51:51.930
We want research done in the NHS

00:51:51.930 --> 00:51:53.340
because it better reflects the patients

00:51:53.340 --> 00:51:54.990
that we are here to serve.

00:51:54.990 --> 00:51:57.540
It makes the results of that
research much more translatable

00:51:57.540 --> 00:51:59.880
so that when clinicians
are seeing patients,

00:51:59.880 --> 00:52:01.620
when the research is done in the UK,

00:52:01.620 --> 00:52:03.810
there's much greater
alignment with the systems

00:52:03.810 --> 00:52:05.400
that we work within the UK

00:52:05.400 --> 00:52:06.540
and there's much greater alignment

00:52:06.540 --> 00:52:07.680
with the types of patients
that we're seeing,

00:52:07.680 --> 00:52:09.150
that they actually, the
demographics of the patients

00:52:09.150 --> 00:52:09.983
match our patients.

00:52:09.983 --> 00:52:12.000
So the decision making for
clinicians is much easier

00:52:12.000 --> 00:52:14.430
where that research is
done in done in the UK.

00:52:14.430 --> 00:52:16.410
Really, really important there.

00:52:16.410 --> 00:52:18.480
We work really closely
with the National Institute

00:52:18.480 --> 00:52:21.570
for Health Research on
all of our research work.

00:52:21.570 --> 00:52:24.660
We now have research back up to a level

00:52:24.660 --> 00:52:26.700
ahead of where we were pre-Covid.

00:52:26.700 --> 00:52:29.370
So we have around 70,000 patients a month

00:52:29.370 --> 00:52:31.830
being recruited into trials in the UK.

00:52:31.830 --> 00:52:36.750
That was up from about 61,000
in pre-Covid, pre-Covid.

00:52:36.750 --> 00:52:39.540
That's not sort of equitably
spread across all areas.

00:52:39.540 --> 00:52:42.690
We see that in particular
phase three studies,

00:52:42.690 --> 00:52:44.310
so these are later studies,

00:52:44.310 --> 00:52:46.530
that actually is recovering more slowly

00:52:46.530 --> 00:52:48.000
and there's been a greater recovery

00:52:48.000 --> 00:52:49.740
in the non-commercial research.

00:52:49.740 --> 00:52:52.530
So we are seeing more
challenges with the recovery

00:52:52.530 --> 00:52:54.030
of commercial research,

00:52:54.030 --> 00:52:55.590
where trust, getting
comfort driving research.

00:52:55.590 --> 00:52:58.350
So plenty more work to
do around there as well.

00:52:58.350 --> 00:53:00.660
Since we last met, we
have published guidance

00:53:00.660 --> 00:53:02.430
for integrated care
systems who are developing

00:53:02.430 --> 00:53:04.530
research strategies so
that we can actually help

00:53:04.530 --> 00:53:06.480
to give them guidance
about how they can develop

00:53:06.480 --> 00:53:08.730
those efficiently and
who to include and how.

00:53:08.730 --> 00:53:10.590
We've also produced a
good practise guidance

00:53:10.590 --> 00:53:13.560
on how we can increase diversity
in research participation

00:53:13.560 --> 00:53:15.120
for those groups.

00:53:15.120 --> 00:53:17.970
Really importantly, that was
co-created with patient groups

00:53:17.970 --> 00:53:21.660
from underserved parts of the population.

00:53:21.660 --> 00:53:23.610
So military veterans,

00:53:23.610 --> 00:53:25.800
patients from particular
ethnic minorities,

00:53:25.800 --> 00:53:27.780
and people who are digitally excluded.

00:53:27.780 --> 00:53:31.230
So really practical advice
for systems to help develop

00:53:31.230 --> 00:53:33.930
research programmes that
can actually target patients

00:53:33.930 --> 00:53:36.210
who are underserved as well.

00:53:36.210 --> 00:53:37.287
David mentioned earlier the,

00:53:37.287 --> 00:53:39.987
the cancer study that was rolled out

00:53:39.987 --> 00:53:41.760
and mentioned in the BBC earlier.

00:53:41.760 --> 00:53:42.960
We had a really similar trial

00:53:42.960 --> 00:53:46.050
that was an NHS sponsored
trial, the Galleri Trial,

00:53:46.050 --> 00:53:47.760
and lots of the learnings from Covid,

00:53:47.760 --> 00:53:49.470
particularly around
underserved populations,

00:53:49.470 --> 00:53:50.400
were fed into that as well.

00:53:50.400 --> 00:53:52.530
So targeting patients who are least likely

00:53:52.530 --> 00:53:54.120
to normally be picked up,

00:53:54.120 --> 00:53:56.790
or as well as those who
typically have cancer diagnosed

00:53:56.790 --> 00:53:58.010
at a later stage as well.

00:53:58.010 --> 00:53:59.880
So really important that
actually the health inequality

00:53:59.880 --> 00:54:01.380
of work that we focus on feeds soon

00:54:01.380 --> 00:54:02.520
to all aspects of our work.

00:54:02.520 --> 00:54:05.310
So both on the research
side and the innovation side

00:54:05.310 --> 00:54:08.190
on providing fantastic patient care.

00:54:08.190 --> 00:54:10.770
Second priority is more
clearly identifying

00:54:10.770 --> 00:54:13.920
in articulating NHS needs
and systematically searching

00:54:13.920 --> 00:54:15.270
for solutions to these.

00:54:15.270 --> 00:54:17.370
So we have produced what we call

00:54:17.370 --> 00:54:21.090
our demand signalling
documents around stroke,

00:54:21.090 --> 00:54:23.550
learning disability and mental health.

00:54:23.550 --> 00:54:25.680
And on the back of those we
have funded a number of projects

00:54:25.680 --> 00:54:27.690
to look for innovations
to help those areas

00:54:27.690 --> 00:54:29.310
where we can see gaps
in those patient care.

00:54:29.310 --> 00:54:32.970
So some early stage support
for these technologies

00:54:32.970 --> 00:54:35.370
where we can see gaps in the types of care

00:54:35.370 --> 00:54:37.020
that we want to provide.

00:54:37.020 --> 00:54:39.510
And this is going to be a
lot of our focus for the work

00:54:39.510 --> 00:54:41.580
that we pick up with Roland
for how we can play our parts

00:54:41.580 --> 00:54:44.790
nationally for looking for these solutions

00:54:44.790 --> 00:54:46.000
to the challenges that we see.

00:54:46.000 --> 00:54:48.169
- [Richard] Matt, you may
need to project your voice,

00:54:48.169 --> 00:54:50.410
you may need to project your
voice a little bit more.

00:54:50.410 --> 00:54:51.410
- Thank you.

00:54:52.350 --> 00:54:54.150
Third priority is supporting the uptake

00:54:54.150 --> 00:54:55.530
of proven innovation.

00:54:55.530 --> 00:54:57.120
So it's all very well having the research,

00:54:57.120 --> 00:54:59.130
having a sort of a view for
the things coming through,

00:54:59.130 --> 00:55:01.140
but we really want the things
that have been NICE approved

00:55:01.140 --> 00:55:03.007
to be used within the NHS.

00:55:03.948 --> 00:55:06.930
In the long-term plan, we
had a commitment to launch

00:55:06.930 --> 00:55:08.850
something called the
MedTech Funding Mandate.

00:55:08.850 --> 00:55:10.980
So for particular types
of medical technologies,

00:55:10.980 --> 00:55:12.450
so these are medical devices,

00:55:12.450 --> 00:55:14.400
diagnostics and digital products,

00:55:14.400 --> 00:55:17.014
we introduced a contractual
requirement for providers

00:55:17.014 --> 00:55:19.560
to make funding available for technologies

00:55:19.560 --> 00:55:22.050
that met the criteria that we defined.

00:55:22.050 --> 00:55:25.650
One of those was a technology
called Spectra Optia.

00:55:25.650 --> 00:55:27.510
So this is a treatment
for sickle cell disease,

00:55:27.510 --> 00:55:28.650
which particularly affects people

00:55:28.650 --> 00:55:32.730
from various ethnic minority backgrounds.

00:55:32.730 --> 00:55:34.650
We have almost tripled the number of sites

00:55:34.650 --> 00:55:36.360
that have the facilities
to offer this treatment,

00:55:36.360 --> 00:55:38.520
which is much better
tolerated for patients.

00:55:38.520 --> 00:55:40.080
It's faster and it's more cost effective.

00:55:40.080 --> 00:55:42.690
So really, really important
treatment for patients.

00:55:42.690 --> 00:55:44.310
And when we look at sort
of the way that we work

00:55:44.310 --> 00:55:45.990
across NHS England, so bringing together

00:55:45.990 --> 00:55:47.940
specialised commissioning
who fund a lot of this,

00:55:47.940 --> 00:55:49.650
bringing together our
horizon scanning approach

00:55:49.650 --> 00:55:51.000
for these technologies,

00:55:51.000 --> 00:55:53.990
we have seen the uptake of
the treatments for this reach

00:55:53.990 --> 00:55:56.880
sort of year three levels
within the first year.

00:55:56.880 --> 00:55:59.610
So really accelerating the adoption

00:55:59.610 --> 00:56:01.650
of important technologies
in important areas

00:56:01.650 --> 00:56:03.300
by working together across the system.

00:56:03.300 --> 00:56:05.212
And there's a lot more
that we would like to do

00:56:05.212 --> 00:56:07.440
around those areas.

00:56:07.440 --> 00:56:08.970
Fourthly, it's really
important that we build

00:56:08.970 --> 00:56:10.140
innovation capacity.

00:56:10.140 --> 00:56:12.900
So we are actually
working to support systems

00:56:12.900 --> 00:56:15.330
with the capacity to adopt innovation.

00:56:15.330 --> 00:56:17.220
So some of this is done through

00:56:17.220 --> 00:56:18.840
the Academic Health Science Networks,

00:56:18.840 --> 00:56:21.720
who provide resources to local systems

00:56:21.720 --> 00:56:23.460
to adopt technologies.

00:56:23.460 --> 00:56:25.290
Over the last year we've
really focused around

00:56:25.290 --> 00:56:27.270
the areas in Core20PLUS5.

00:56:27.270 --> 00:56:29.400
So working with systems to understand

00:56:29.400 --> 00:56:31.230
what are the clinical
innovations that they see

00:56:31.230 --> 00:56:33.600
that are NICE approved,
that meet those areas,

00:56:33.600 --> 00:56:35.340
and what support is it
that systems are telling us

00:56:35.340 --> 00:56:37.410
they want and targeting our research,

00:56:37.410 --> 00:56:40.294
sorry, our support into those areas.

00:56:40.294 --> 00:56:43.251
We'll have much more update
about the impact of that

00:56:43.251 --> 00:56:45.060
later this year, which we
would love to come back

00:56:45.060 --> 00:56:48.120
and talk to the board
about at a future date.

00:56:48.120 --> 00:56:50.430
It's also really important
that we support our workforce.

00:56:50.430 --> 00:56:52.980
So we've got a mentoring
programme for NHS staff

00:56:52.980 --> 00:56:56.190
called the NHS Clinical
Entrepreneurs Programme.

00:56:56.190 --> 00:56:58.680
We've now recruited our
1,000th member of that.

00:56:58.680 --> 00:57:01.560
So this is a programme that
provides support to clinicians

00:57:01.560 --> 00:57:04.140
who can see ideas and
solutions to problems

00:57:04.140 --> 00:57:05.580
that they see on the frontline.

00:57:05.580 --> 00:57:07.410
And it enables them to have the support

00:57:07.410 --> 00:57:09.660
and the commercialisation,
the pitching support,

00:57:09.660 --> 00:57:12.150
the understanding of
what research is needed,

00:57:12.150 --> 00:57:13.770
what the evidential thresholds are

00:57:13.770 --> 00:57:15.210
from people who have done this.

00:57:15.210 --> 00:57:17.190
So it enables them to
develop these solutions

00:57:17.190 --> 00:57:19.530
and remain in the NHS
while they're doing so.

00:57:19.530 --> 00:57:22.230
These 1,000 clinical entrepreneurs
over the last six years

00:57:22.230 --> 00:57:24.360
have now raised over 850 million pounds.

00:57:24.360 --> 00:57:27.030
So these are real solutions
that are used in the NHS

00:57:27.030 --> 00:57:28.320
and right across the world.

00:57:28.320 --> 00:57:30.510
So fantastic, fantastic point there,

00:57:30.510 --> 00:57:32.670
which goes to the point that
Amanda was making earlier

00:57:32.670 --> 00:57:34.010
about how innovative the NHS is

00:57:34.010 --> 00:57:38.280
and how the NHS continues to evolve.

00:57:38.280 --> 00:57:41.340
We also look at what support
we can give to innovators.

00:57:41.340 --> 00:57:43.170
So I mentioned a little bit
about our demand sampling

00:57:43.170 --> 00:57:44.370
and our horizon scanning work,

00:57:44.370 --> 00:57:48.180
how we target our support
and our finances around areas

00:57:48.180 --> 00:57:49.740
where we want to develop solutions

00:57:49.740 --> 00:57:51.030
that develop our problems.

00:57:51.030 --> 00:57:53.880
We also see this done at a
national level for big areas.

00:57:53.880 --> 00:57:55.860
So we're working really
closely with government

00:57:55.860 --> 00:57:57.750
on some of the national agreements

00:57:57.750 --> 00:57:59.370
around BioNTech and Moderna,

00:57:59.370 --> 00:58:02.790
both of whom are developing
really exciting technologies

00:58:02.790 --> 00:58:05.310
based on MRNA for novel areas,

00:58:05.310 --> 00:58:07.320
including cancer vaccines as well.

00:58:07.320 --> 00:58:09.630
So some really exciting
areas that we're working

00:58:09.630 --> 00:58:11.910
across government to develop.

00:58:11.910 --> 00:58:13.590
And, finally, since we last met,

00:58:13.590 --> 00:58:16.110
we have launched the
NHS Innovation Service.

00:58:16.110 --> 00:58:18.810
So this is a way in which
innovators can log on

00:58:18.810 --> 00:58:21.390
to a single service and
navigate what can be

00:58:21.390 --> 00:58:24.300
a quite complicated research
and innovation landscape.

00:58:24.300 --> 00:58:25.590
And we're already seeing,

00:58:25.590 --> 00:58:29.220
we've seen about 750 innovators
go through this process

00:58:29.220 --> 00:58:31.740
and many of these are now reaching,

00:58:31.740 --> 00:58:34.140
getting tailored support to reach NICE

00:58:34.140 --> 00:58:35.790
and get into the pipeline for NICE.

00:58:35.790 --> 00:58:37.110
We'll get regulatory support for

00:58:37.110 --> 00:58:39.240
the Medicine and Healthcare
Regulatory Authority

00:58:39.240 --> 00:58:40.080
and so forth,

00:58:40.080 --> 00:58:42.240
whatever the particular
support is that they need.

00:58:42.240 --> 00:58:46.020
So, loads of progress since we last met.

00:58:46.020 --> 00:58:49.620
And the two final things that
I would just like to add.

00:58:49.620 --> 00:58:52.560
At a national level, there's
more we want to do around

00:58:52.560 --> 00:58:54.570
playing our part in that ecosystem,

00:58:54.570 --> 00:58:57.060
particularly around sort
of the flow of innovations

00:58:57.060 --> 00:58:59.160
that come through, the safety checks that

00:58:59.160 --> 00:59:01.020
the Medicine and Healthcare
Regulatory Authority

00:59:01.020 --> 00:59:02.850
do that flow into NICE,

00:59:02.850 --> 00:59:04.740
who undertake the clinical assessment

00:59:04.740 --> 00:59:06.660
and the cost effectiveness assessment

00:59:06.660 --> 00:59:08.430
and the affordability assessment,

00:59:08.430 --> 00:59:11.280
and how do we best set
ourselves up at a national level

00:59:11.280 --> 00:59:13.440
to respond to the things
coming through the pipeline.

00:59:13.440 --> 00:59:14.730
We do this really well,

00:59:14.730 --> 00:59:16.200
particularly in specialised commissioning,

00:59:16.200 --> 00:59:17.790
where we're completely set up to do that.

00:59:17.790 --> 00:59:19.470
So a lot of what we'll
be working with Roland on

00:59:19.470 --> 00:59:21.630
is how do we actually
systematically do that

00:59:21.630 --> 00:59:24.030
in other areas that are important to us?

00:59:24.030 --> 00:59:25.050
And the second part of that is,

00:59:25.050 --> 00:59:27.870
how do we better serve systems

00:59:27.870 --> 00:59:29.210
in order to provide that support?

00:59:29.210 --> 00:59:30.330
So what we really want to do,

00:59:30.330 --> 00:59:31.950
alongside producing a blueprint

00:59:31.950 --> 00:59:33.630
and operating model for how we work,

00:59:33.630 --> 00:59:35.310
is actually learn by doing with systems.

00:59:35.310 --> 00:59:37.380
So pick a number of areas that link into

00:59:37.380 --> 00:59:39.210
our life science vision areas

00:59:39.210 --> 00:59:41.010
and our healthcare inequality areas,

00:59:41.010 --> 00:59:42.990
and work with systems
to understand what are

00:59:42.990 --> 00:59:45.150
the most effective ways
that we can support systems

00:59:45.150 --> 00:59:47.490
in order to drive through
forward this adoption.

00:59:47.490 --> 00:59:49.170
- So, Matt, thank you.

00:59:49.170 --> 00:59:52.700
As always, your enthusiasm is very clear

00:59:52.700 --> 00:59:54.840
in your, in your descriptions.

00:59:54.840 --> 00:59:56.860
And for those of us who were able

00:59:57.724 --> 00:59:59.490
to sit as a board last night,

00:59:59.490 --> 01:00:03.720
and we were joined by Mike
Richards and Peter Johnson,

01:00:03.720 --> 01:00:07.650
I had a very good conversation
and deep dive into cancer,

01:00:07.650 --> 01:00:09.480
actually, and into the innovation

01:00:09.480 --> 01:00:11.520
and the range of different treatments.

01:00:11.520 --> 01:00:13.953
And Peter, in particular,
talked about Galleri,

01:00:15.167 --> 01:00:16.000
or the Grail,

01:00:16.000 --> 01:00:18.900
the Grail Programme.

01:00:18.900 --> 01:00:21.753
I mean, the innovation
is really very clear,

01:00:22.950 --> 01:00:26.070
certainly in the conversation
we had last night.

01:00:26.070 --> 01:00:27.690
There are going to be loads
of questions for you, Matt.

01:00:27.690 --> 01:00:29.490
I'm pretty sure I can
see hands going already,

01:00:29.490 --> 01:00:31.920
so I'm going to go Mark, first, I think.

01:00:31.920 --> 01:00:32.753
- Okay, well, thank you very much, indeed.

01:00:32.753 --> 01:00:34.380
That was a terrific presentation.

01:00:34.380 --> 01:00:36.390
I welcome this totally.

01:00:36.390 --> 01:00:39.030
I think there is one
missing piece, actually,

01:00:39.030 --> 01:00:41.820
which is the education,
training and support

01:00:41.820 --> 01:00:45.180
of the key people in the form
of researchers and innovators.

01:00:45.180 --> 01:00:48.690
And I think, unfortunately,
the training environment for

01:00:48.690 --> 01:00:52.620
doctors, nurses, allied health
professionals who want to be,

01:00:52.620 --> 01:00:54.870
who want to do research,
who want to innovate,

01:00:54.870 --> 01:00:57.450
incentives aren't there and we
can see the numbers falling.

01:00:57.450 --> 01:00:59.250
So I think that is a huge challenge.

01:00:59.250 --> 01:01:01.023
And, actually, I welcome the opportunity

01:01:01.023 --> 01:01:03.930
that we can have now
working closely with HEE,

01:01:03.930 --> 01:01:06.711
because I think that's critical.

01:01:06.711 --> 01:01:08.580
So mentoring entrepreneurs is good,

01:01:08.580 --> 01:01:11.133
but it's only a small part of the piece.

01:01:11.970 --> 01:01:13.800
The second thing is I
think there probably needs

01:01:13.800 --> 01:01:15.390
to be more work on how we regulate

01:01:15.390 --> 01:01:17.490
how to handle new technologies as well

01:01:17.490 --> 01:01:20.310
and provide tested environments
because we are seeing

01:01:20.310 --> 01:01:22.620
all sorts of extraordinary innovations.

01:01:22.620 --> 01:01:25.320
You know, how do we use Grail in practise?

01:01:25.320 --> 01:01:26.223
AI.

01:01:27.840 --> 01:01:29.610
We were talking earlier about biosimilars,

01:01:29.610 --> 01:01:33.030
so I think there's big
opportunities there,

01:01:33.030 --> 01:01:35.460
but I really endorse the
point that we need to move

01:01:35.460 --> 01:01:37.200
from a sort of push model of innovation,

01:01:37.200 --> 01:01:39.450
where innovation is pushed at the NHS,

01:01:39.450 --> 01:01:41.280
to one where there's pull.

01:01:41.280 --> 01:01:43.290
And I think that that's really critical.

01:01:43.290 --> 01:01:45.150
And I just think there's
one lot of partners

01:01:45.150 --> 01:01:46.530
that we missed in the discussion as well,

01:01:46.530 --> 01:01:48.663
which is I think the other
public funders as research,

01:01:48.663 --> 01:01:50.850
such as UK Research and Innovation,

01:01:50.850 --> 01:01:53.460
also our extraordinary charity sector.

01:01:53.460 --> 01:01:55.590
And there's no question
that we have to work closely

01:01:55.590 --> 01:01:56.610
with business because there's

01:01:56.610 --> 01:01:58.560
an enormous amount of innovation there.

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:00.833
- Thank you.

01:02:00.833 --> 01:02:03.330
Matt, I won't get you to comment
or respond to those points

01:02:03.330 --> 01:02:06.060
as yet, or Tim, but let's see, who else?

01:02:06.060 --> 01:02:08.340
I mean, I saw Munir, you
wanted to raise a point,

01:02:08.340 --> 01:02:10.710
and then David, and then
we'll batch those up, Matt,

01:02:10.710 --> 01:02:12.990
and between you and
Tim, we'll answer those

01:02:12.990 --> 01:02:15.570
and then we'll go out
for further questions.

01:02:15.570 --> 01:02:16.800
Munir?
- Thank you.

01:02:16.800 --> 01:02:18.510
Really good, great paper.

01:02:18.510 --> 01:02:19.830
Really important area.

01:02:19.830 --> 01:02:22.740
So the first thing I
wanted to say was, again,

01:02:22.740 --> 01:02:25.110
echo what Mark just
said about the fact that

01:02:25.110 --> 01:02:27.270
it's great that you are liaising

01:02:27.270 --> 01:02:29.970
with all these organisations,
MHRA, NIHI, et cetera.

01:02:29.970 --> 01:02:33.147
But don't forget the MRC and UKRI.

01:02:33.147 --> 01:02:36.000
And don't forget the charity sector,

01:02:36.000 --> 01:02:38.340
which funds a lot of
research in this country

01:02:38.340 --> 01:02:40.920
and engagement with that
sector is really important.

01:02:40.920 --> 01:02:45.060
Also, there are many doctors and nurses,

01:02:45.060 --> 01:02:47.460
pharmacists that work
between the university sector

01:02:47.460 --> 01:02:50.520
and the NHS and produce
a lot of research within.

01:02:50.520 --> 01:02:53.190
So it is important to be able
to link up with them as well.

01:02:53.190 --> 01:02:55.050
I know entrepreneurs are really important,

01:02:55.050 --> 01:02:58.140
but the wider workforce that actually does

01:02:58.140 --> 01:03:00.060
some of this research and innovation

01:03:00.060 --> 01:03:02.100
is really important to interact with.

01:03:02.100 --> 01:03:04.437
The question I had was
really about roadblocks with,

01:03:04.437 --> 01:03:08.700
and our focus on priority
one and priority three.

01:03:08.700 --> 01:03:11.130
If you talk to researchers at the moment,

01:03:11.130 --> 01:03:12.840
one of the major issues they have is that

01:03:12.840 --> 01:03:15.933
it takes too long to
start research in the NHS.

01:03:16.800 --> 01:03:18.690
And then if you talk to innovators,

01:03:18.690 --> 01:03:22.230
they say it takes too long to
get the innovation introduced

01:03:22.230 --> 01:03:23.580
into the NHS.

01:03:23.580 --> 01:03:27.570
So can you identify what you
feel are the main roadblocks

01:03:27.570 --> 01:03:29.070
in priority one, priority three,

01:03:29.070 --> 01:03:30.960
and how you're going to tackle them?

01:03:30.960 --> 01:03:34.677
- So if you can hold
that as another question

01:03:34.677 --> 01:03:37.200
and that, how you get them
introduced, or scaled,

01:03:37.200 --> 01:03:38.720
I think is the thing
I often hear actually.

01:03:38.720 --> 01:03:41.490
So something can work quite well in one,

01:03:41.490 --> 01:03:43.650
say one acute hospital or in one location.

01:03:43.650 --> 01:03:45.510
But how do we get it to scale?

01:03:45.510 --> 01:03:48.600
David, do you want to come
in and then I'll ask Matt.

01:03:48.600 --> 01:03:52.263
- Again, thanks Matt, very
good paper and presentation.

01:03:53.280 --> 01:03:54.840
I wonder whether you could say more about

01:03:54.840 --> 01:03:57.000
the Academic Health
Science Network Review,

01:03:57.000 --> 01:03:59.040
because if I've understood their role

01:03:59.040 --> 01:04:02.910
on the push and pull and how
things are taken to scale,

01:04:02.910 --> 01:04:05.820
that's a key part of the
capacity I think we've got

01:04:05.820 --> 01:04:07.590
across the system.

01:04:07.590 --> 01:04:12.590
And I just wonder how far that
review is going to contribute

01:04:12.930 --> 01:04:14.630
to answering some of the questions

01:04:16.802 --> 01:04:19.750
that Mark and Munir
have just asked, thanks.

01:04:21.073 --> 01:04:22.830
- Do you want to have a go at that group

01:04:22.830 --> 01:04:25.980
of comments or questions, Tim or Matt?

01:04:25.980 --> 01:04:27.669
- Shall I, shall I pick up first, Tim,

01:04:27.669 --> 01:04:31.080
and then you correct me
when I miss things out?

01:04:31.080 --> 01:04:32.250
- Obviously difficult questions

01:04:32.250 --> 01:04:35.033
'cause neither are
racing to get on with it.

01:04:35.033 --> 01:04:37.110
- Well, I thought if I go
first I'll have first go.

01:04:37.110 --> 01:04:39.450
So, Mark, so I really,
really strongly agree

01:04:39.450 --> 01:04:42.300
about the importance of clinical academics

01:04:42.300 --> 01:04:44.814
and actually, and also with,

01:04:44.814 --> 01:04:47.280
with Munir about actually
some of the other partners

01:04:47.280 --> 01:04:48.630
who I did neglect to mention,

01:04:48.630 --> 01:04:51.090
but they do also sit
on and are represented

01:04:51.090 --> 01:04:52.920
on the Accelerated Access
Collaborative Board.

01:04:52.920 --> 01:04:56.490
So UKRI and MRC and
other partners as well.

01:04:56.490 --> 01:04:58.410
So we do very, very
strongly engage with them

01:04:58.410 --> 01:04:59.580
and actually is, you know,

01:04:59.580 --> 01:05:01.740
one of the things I get when
I present this work, actually,

01:05:01.740 --> 01:05:03.840
I'm not just presenting my team's work

01:05:03.840 --> 01:05:05.370
or the transformation directorate's work

01:05:05.370 --> 01:05:06.240
or the NHS England's work.

01:05:06.240 --> 01:05:08.430
This is a collaborative agreement between

01:05:08.430 --> 01:05:09.900
a number of organisations.

01:05:09.900 --> 01:05:13.230
So that is one point to make.

01:05:13.230 --> 01:05:15.636
I think, on sort of the, some of the role

01:05:15.636 --> 01:05:18.060
of clinical academics and
some of those challenges

01:05:18.060 --> 01:05:19.691
that you mentioned, Mark.

01:05:19.691 --> 01:05:22.170
So there's two things going on
that I think will help that.

01:05:22.170 --> 01:05:24.990
So first is Lord James O'Shaughnessy

01:05:24.990 --> 01:05:27.840
has an independent review into research.

01:05:27.840 --> 01:05:30.090
We're expecting to read
out from that next week

01:05:30.090 --> 01:05:30.923
to go back to government.

01:05:30.923 --> 01:05:33.090
So actually I think we'll be

01:05:33.090 --> 01:05:35.670
really interested in the
recommendations from that report

01:05:35.670 --> 01:05:37.560
and have a look at what
those recommendations are,

01:05:37.560 --> 01:05:40.110
which may touch on some of those areas.

01:05:40.110 --> 01:05:41.640
Secondly, on clinical academics,

01:05:41.640 --> 01:05:43.260
specifically, there was
also a House of Lord's

01:05:43.260 --> 01:05:46.380
inquiry into this and
there are recommendations

01:05:46.380 --> 01:05:49.290
that I think are heading over
to Secretary of State as well.

01:05:49.290 --> 01:05:51.720
So I think there are sort of
government led initiatives

01:05:51.720 --> 01:05:53.760
that we will look at
once they come through

01:05:53.760 --> 01:05:55.020
that we will touch on all of these,

01:05:55.020 --> 01:05:56.997
all of these areas that
we, that you discussed.

01:05:56.997 --> 01:05:59.640
But we would love to
follow up offline as well

01:05:59.640 --> 01:06:02.090
to keep these plans, to
keep these plans aligned.

01:06:03.120 --> 01:06:04.140
Tim, on those two points,

01:06:04.140 --> 01:06:07.350
I dunno if there was anything
that you wanted to raise.

01:06:07.350 --> 01:06:08.777
- Well, just that I think it's,

01:06:08.777 --> 01:06:11.760
it's a very useful, Mark and Munir,

01:06:11.760 --> 01:06:14.430
to distinguish between the support

01:06:14.430 --> 01:06:17.070
one needs to provide to entrepreneurs

01:06:17.070 --> 01:06:20.130
and the support one needs
to provide to academic,

01:06:20.130 --> 01:06:22.110
more basic science research.

01:06:22.110 --> 01:06:25.200
They are different phenotypes,

01:06:25.200 --> 01:06:27.273
if you'll pardon my use of that word,

01:06:28.439 --> 01:06:31.383
and they require a
different set of supports.

01:06:32.760 --> 01:06:35.820
We did emphasise the entrepreneurs,

01:06:35.820 --> 01:06:39.466
but I wanted to assure you that
we recognise the distinction

01:06:39.466 --> 01:06:43.030
and with our partners are working on

01:06:45.786 --> 01:06:50.357
the clinical basic science
more academic faculty support.

01:06:52.110 --> 01:06:54.870
I think it's fair to say that there

01:06:54.870 --> 01:06:58.350
are challenges in this space
that need to be reversed,

01:06:58.350 --> 01:07:02.370
is maybe the, over recent trends,

01:07:02.370 --> 01:07:05.760
and look forward to working
with you, getting your support

01:07:05.760 --> 01:07:07.800
in addressing that.

01:07:07.800 --> 01:07:10.290
I think the other question
was around roadblocks.

01:07:10.290 --> 01:07:11.123
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:07:11.123 --> 01:07:12.154
Should I test that?

01:07:12.154 --> 01:07:14.580
Just try by hand at that one?

01:07:14.580 --> 01:07:18.270
So, the blocks to...

01:07:21.270 --> 01:07:23.640
at multiple stages,

01:07:23.640 --> 01:07:26.940
they're usually broken
down into four stages,

01:07:26.940 --> 01:07:29.880
and they are very different
blocks at each point

01:07:29.880 --> 01:07:32.940
in the translation of
research into practise.

01:07:32.940 --> 01:07:37.170
And I just make a couple of comments where

01:07:37.170 --> 01:07:39.930
I think, I am hopeful,

01:07:39.930 --> 01:07:42.390
connecting this conversation
to an earlier conversation

01:07:42.390 --> 01:07:43.830
about the ICSs.

01:07:43.830 --> 01:07:48.830
That in fact, ICSs will
be a really positive force

01:07:49.260 --> 01:07:51.780
for the entry of innovation,

01:07:51.780 --> 01:07:54.270
particularly in the later stages

01:07:54.270 --> 01:07:56.430
of the blocks to the translation,

01:07:56.430 --> 01:07:58.920
because a lot of times

01:07:58.920 --> 01:08:02.580
blocks to the adoption of
innovation are institutional.

01:08:02.580 --> 01:08:07.320
And I think having an
integrated delivery board

01:08:07.320 --> 01:08:09.750
looking across organisation

01:08:09.750 --> 01:08:11.880
takes some of the organisational politics

01:08:11.880 --> 01:08:13.950
out of the decisions for adoption

01:08:13.950 --> 01:08:18.333
because often innovations,
by their very nature,

01:08:21.095 --> 01:08:23.730
are helpful to one group of stakeholders

01:08:23.730 --> 01:08:27.420
and not helpful, or not
perceived as helpful, to another.

01:08:27.420 --> 01:08:30.360
And having ICS oversight of that,

01:08:30.360 --> 01:08:33.267
I think is a really positive,

01:08:33.267 --> 01:08:36.330
has has great potential
for helping in what's

01:08:36.330 --> 01:08:39.090
often called the fourth
translational block.

01:08:39.090 --> 01:08:43.773
I also just want to say
that the risk appetite,

01:08:45.030 --> 01:08:49.470
and this is my own perspective
on this, is important,

01:08:49.470 --> 01:08:54.000
and it's important in
hearing about the status quo,

01:08:54.000 --> 01:08:55.650
like the situation we currently face.

01:08:55.650 --> 01:08:57.570
And I'm going to refer
back to what you just heard

01:08:57.570 --> 01:09:01.290
from David Sloman about performance.

01:09:01.290 --> 01:09:05.910
Actually, the demographic trend for the,

01:09:05.910 --> 01:09:10.910
and the scaling of the
requirements on the NHS

01:09:10.980 --> 01:09:14.640
of increased services
puts us in a position

01:09:14.640 --> 01:09:17.070
where we really need to consider

01:09:17.070 --> 01:09:21.180
the counterfactual risk of not changing.

01:09:21.180 --> 01:09:23.400
And I think...

01:09:23.400 --> 01:09:27.400
We are, I see increasing acceptance

01:09:28.620 --> 01:09:33.360
and enthusiasm about the
changes that will be required

01:09:33.360 --> 01:09:36.150
for us to actually be able to provide

01:09:36.150 --> 01:09:39.878
the future needs of the NHS',

01:09:39.878 --> 01:09:43.200
to our patients and
citizens in the future.

01:09:43.200 --> 01:09:47.550
So I think both of those things are...

01:09:47.550 --> 01:09:50.160
are important in thinking about

01:09:50.160 --> 01:09:51.907
this fourth translational block.

01:09:51.907 --> 01:09:54.214
- I mean I think that last
point of them being some other,

01:09:54.214 --> 01:09:56.610
I could see people waving at me,

01:09:56.610 --> 01:09:58.140
that last point I think
is really important.

01:09:58.140 --> 01:09:59.400
We've talked before,

01:09:59.400 --> 01:10:00.690
and I may misdescribe it,

01:10:00.690 --> 01:10:02.460
but actually what we see is

01:10:02.460 --> 01:10:04.590
an exponential growth in demand,

01:10:04.590 --> 01:10:07.470
partly from population growth
and from an ageing population.

01:10:07.470 --> 01:10:09.000
And then actually just our,

01:10:09.000 --> 01:10:11.310
just our very real
scientific skills, et cetera,

01:10:11.310 --> 01:10:14.400
we find more conditions
that we're able to treat.

01:10:14.400 --> 01:10:16.518
And so actually the demand

01:10:16.518 --> 01:10:18.930
for the health service
goes up all the time.

01:10:18.930 --> 01:10:21.810
Unless we continue to innovate and adapt

01:10:21.810 --> 01:10:24.420
in whether it's pharma or life sciences

01:10:24.420 --> 01:10:25.920
or MedTech or technology, actually,

01:10:25.920 --> 01:10:29.040
our ability to travel with
that growth and demand

01:10:29.040 --> 01:10:30.180
is going to be very difficult.

01:10:30.180 --> 01:10:33.510
So I think the NHS will
continue to innovate.

01:10:33.510 --> 01:10:35.550
My questions are all about

01:10:35.550 --> 01:10:37.560
can we make access to clinical trials?

01:10:37.560 --> 01:10:39.750
Sort of, I'll say, this
is not a technical term,

01:10:39.750 --> 01:10:41.310
but less clunky,

01:10:41.310 --> 01:10:43.920
and then it is a question of
how do we scale the number

01:10:43.920 --> 01:10:48.300
of innovating, innovators
who've talked to me

01:10:48.300 --> 01:10:50.880
at various events and they've
got something that's working,

01:10:50.880 --> 01:10:54.600
but actually can they get
that spread throughout the NHS

01:10:54.600 --> 01:10:58.320
even though it's clearly working
in one particular location?

01:10:58.320 --> 01:11:00.900
That seems to be one of the
practical challenges we have,

01:11:00.900 --> 01:11:03.570
but don't go anywhere near those.

01:11:03.570 --> 01:11:05.721
Mary waved her hand, you
put your hand up first.

01:11:08.194 --> 01:11:10.097
- [David] I didn't hear
anything on the extra HSNs.

01:11:10.097 --> 01:11:13.350
And I think the capacity
to do exactly some of that.

01:11:13.350 --> 01:11:15.744
- The 50 of HSMs, which
are under review, yeah.

01:11:15.744 --> 01:11:16.836
- Sorry, sorry.
- Can I comment,

01:11:16.836 --> 01:11:19.020
because actually my question
is almost a reflection

01:11:19.020 --> 01:11:20.790
of yours, David, if not same,

01:11:20.790 --> 01:11:24.900
except particularly the
applied research collaboratives

01:11:24.900 --> 01:11:25.800
within that.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:11:25.800 --> 01:11:30.800
- Who aid people in
adopting new innovation.

01:11:31.050 --> 01:11:32.650
And I'd really like to hear how

01:11:33.575 --> 01:11:35.970
we're going to make sure they're not lost.

01:11:35.970 --> 01:11:40.650
- So hold HSNs and the
collaboratives in mind.

01:11:40.650 --> 01:11:42.884
And I'm going to go Steve and then Navina.

01:11:42.884 --> 01:11:44.717
Steve.
- So I wanted...

01:11:46.620 --> 01:11:48.450
I wanted to build on the workforce point

01:11:48.450 --> 01:11:50.013
that both Mark and Munir made,

01:11:50.880 --> 01:11:52.680
because obviously none
of this would happen

01:11:52.680 --> 01:11:55.650
without the appropriately
trained supported staff

01:11:55.650 --> 01:11:58.533
who then have the opportunity
to deliver this agenda.

01:11:59.632 --> 01:12:01.230
And so the first point

01:12:01.230 --> 01:12:03.630
is I tend to think of this in a group,

01:12:03.630 --> 01:12:05.190
certainly with the medical professional,

01:12:05.190 --> 01:12:08.070
a group of people who, like
myself, Mark and Munir,

01:12:08.070 --> 01:12:11.940
we're career academics
employed by universities

01:12:11.940 --> 01:12:13.350
working in NHS hospitals.

01:12:13.350 --> 01:12:15.360
So I think there's a
lot of work to be done

01:12:15.360 --> 01:12:16.650
with the university sector,

01:12:16.650 --> 01:12:19.290
but also those other third party funders,

01:12:19.290 --> 01:12:20.670
including charities, as how we,

01:12:20.670 --> 01:12:24.630
we support those clinicians
both in training,

01:12:24.630 --> 01:12:27.330
as Mark has said, but also in their work.

01:12:27.330 --> 01:12:28.620
But there's a second group which is

01:12:28.620 --> 01:12:32.370
the group within the NHS, NHS employed,

01:12:32.370 --> 01:12:34.470
who are also interested in delivering

01:12:34.470 --> 01:12:35.430
research and innovation.

01:12:35.430 --> 01:12:36.930
So I think at district general hospitals,

01:12:36.930 --> 01:12:41.190
which don't often don't have
the same strong academic links,

01:12:41.190 --> 01:12:44.040
they delivered
magnificently, for instance,

01:12:44.040 --> 01:12:45.960
in the recovery trial during

01:12:45.960 --> 01:12:47.970
the first phases of the pandemic.

01:12:47.970 --> 01:12:50.580
And I think there's a real
opportunity within that workforce

01:12:50.580 --> 01:12:53.910
appropriately supported
and appropriately trained

01:12:53.910 --> 01:12:56.520
to drive much more
activity clinical trials.

01:12:56.520 --> 01:12:57.690
And then the second point is,

01:12:57.690 --> 01:12:59.310
it's not to fall into
the trap thinking this

01:12:59.310 --> 01:13:01.087
is just about doctors, because it's not,

01:13:01.087 --> 01:13:04.670
there are many, many nurses delivering...

01:13:05.580 --> 01:13:07.920
trials, research nurses, pharmacists,

01:13:07.920 --> 01:13:09.390
laboratory scientists.

01:13:09.390 --> 01:13:11.397
So it's a whole of workforce challenge,

01:13:11.397 --> 01:13:13.433
and I really think we need
to set the opportunity

01:13:13.433 --> 01:13:16.830
for the work coming out of
the long-term workforce plan,

01:13:16.830 --> 01:13:19.680
when it's published, to
really grasp this agenda

01:13:19.680 --> 01:13:22.230
and work out how we
support those workforces.

01:13:22.230 --> 01:13:24.560
- So Tim and Matt, you
hold on these questions,

01:13:24.560 --> 01:13:26.160
'cause there are two more
questions coming at you.

01:13:26.160 --> 01:13:28.830
If you can, if you can
batch up, that'd be helpful.

01:13:28.830 --> 01:13:30.747
So Navina and then Wol.

01:13:30.747 --> 01:13:32.310
- So, I think it's worth sharing

01:13:32.310 --> 01:13:34.260
what actually happens right now

01:13:34.260 --> 01:13:36.690
in education and training

01:13:36.690 --> 01:13:40.710
and what our ambition could
be in response to this.

01:13:40.710 --> 01:13:44.160
So, currently there are,
there's twofold approach.

01:13:44.160 --> 01:13:47.880
One is embedding research,
the understanding

01:13:47.880 --> 01:13:51.270
and the ability to use research
in our clinical practise,

01:13:51.270 --> 01:13:53.640
in our learning, multi-professional,

01:13:53.640 --> 01:13:56.640
that's across all of our curricula,

01:13:56.640 --> 01:14:00.390
that is there so that
our staff are equipped

01:14:00.390 --> 01:14:02.970
to understand research and apply it

01:14:02.970 --> 01:14:04.470
in their day-to-day practise.

01:14:04.470 --> 01:14:05.940
That's one thing that is happening.

01:14:05.940 --> 01:14:09.390
The second is that existing staff,

01:14:09.390 --> 01:14:10.800
as Steve describes,

01:14:10.800 --> 01:14:15.060
clinical staff of all professions
who express an interest

01:14:15.060 --> 01:14:20.060
and can get hold of a
university or a school

01:14:20.850 --> 01:14:24.210
and have the right conditions
in the trust to support them

01:14:24.210 --> 01:14:27.240
to pursue interest in research,

01:14:27.240 --> 01:14:29.310
it is supposed to happen,

01:14:29.310 --> 01:14:30.720
and it's really patchy.

01:14:30.720 --> 01:14:32.220
We were talking about it yesterday,

01:14:32.220 --> 01:14:34.380
and I get quite frequently approached

01:14:34.380 --> 01:14:36.637
by trainees or students who say,

01:14:36.637 --> 01:14:39.157
"I've got this idea and the
trust says I can't do it

01:14:39.157 --> 01:14:41.190
"or I haven't got time or I
can't be allowed to do this"

01:14:41.190 --> 01:14:42.023
or that and the other.

01:14:42.023 --> 01:14:43.680
So I think that is something that,

01:14:43.680 --> 01:14:46.740
now that we're one organisation,
we can certainly address.

01:14:46.740 --> 01:14:48.780
The third, which we
don't have at the moment,

01:14:48.780 --> 01:14:53.780
is a really clear understanding
of what a career pathway is

01:14:54.240 --> 01:14:59.240
and how we start from, to
there, to professor or,

01:14:59.700 --> 01:15:01.647
and once we get that absolutely clear,

01:15:01.647 --> 01:15:04.050
and I think perhaps the
review that's been done

01:15:04.050 --> 01:15:05.730
will help us to understand that

01:15:05.730 --> 01:15:07.890
and then we can really
start planning properly

01:15:07.890 --> 01:15:10.923
for that workforce like we
do for our other workforce.

01:15:12.930 --> 01:15:13.866
- Wol.

01:15:13.866 --> 01:15:17.970
- So so my question
revolves around access,

01:15:17.970 --> 01:15:18.803
or the barriers.

01:15:18.803 --> 01:15:20.850
I think we talked about them.

01:15:20.850 --> 01:15:22.890
So, Tim, you talked about the importance

01:15:22.890 --> 01:15:25.920
of having Innovation, Research,
Life Sciences together,

01:15:25.920 --> 01:15:28.650
but I would also say you
need to split them up,

01:15:28.650 --> 01:15:30.660
because a lot of what we've
talked about is research,

01:15:30.660 --> 01:15:33.714
life sciences and it's
focused on the workforce

01:15:33.714 --> 01:15:35.160
and that's all fantastic.

01:15:35.160 --> 01:15:37.860
But innovation can just be
just workflow stuff, right?

01:15:37.860 --> 01:15:40.110
And there's an example
in here of eConsult,

01:15:40.110 --> 01:15:43.200
and that can happen both in
the system and out the system.

01:15:43.200 --> 01:15:44.520
I can tell you from personal experience,

01:15:44.520 --> 01:15:47.970
the out of the system trying
to focus in is so difficult

01:15:47.970 --> 01:15:49.740
to actually introduce any
sort in the innovation

01:15:49.740 --> 01:15:51.570
into the system because part

01:15:51.570 --> 01:15:53.250
it's to deal with risk appetite.

01:15:53.250 --> 01:15:54.900
It's just not there.

01:15:54.900 --> 01:15:56.760
And that's the first question.

01:15:56.760 --> 01:15:59.130
Second thing is, in some
of the things we're doing,

01:15:59.130 --> 01:16:01.650
given that we all talk
about the funding challenge,

01:16:01.650 --> 01:16:06.650
how much do we, as the NHS
England, take stakes in companies

01:16:06.690 --> 01:16:08.700
so we can actually benefit
from any of the scaling

01:16:08.700 --> 01:16:09.930
we get involved with?

01:16:09.930 --> 01:16:11.160
Is that something that's been done?

01:16:11.160 --> 01:16:13.083
I just made notice on this subject.

01:16:15.060 --> 01:16:18.390
- So, do you want to have
a go, Tim, more than Matt,

01:16:18.390 --> 01:16:20.243
at those comments?
- Yeah.

01:16:21.330 --> 01:16:22.200
- Yeah, so actually,

01:16:22.200 --> 01:16:25.770
Matt, why don't I let you
talk about AHSNs first,

01:16:25.770 --> 01:16:29.309
'cause we are very stuck
into a process around,

01:16:29.309 --> 01:16:33.840
and I know Gary Ford, the
Chair of the Oversight Board,

01:16:33.840 --> 01:16:36.510
is working with all of the HSN leaders

01:16:36.510 --> 01:16:41.510
on actually what is the
going forward plan for AHSNs.

01:16:41.520 --> 01:16:43.983
And that is, that is actively occurring.

01:16:45.150 --> 01:16:46.860
- So yes, and one thing
I just want to quickly

01:16:46.860 --> 01:16:47.970
come back on about barriers.

01:16:47.970 --> 01:16:49.770
So one thing that I think is
really important to mention

01:16:49.770 --> 01:16:53.130
that is not a barrier,
particularly around research,

01:16:53.130 --> 01:16:55.710
is the public's appetite for
being involved in research.

01:16:55.710 --> 01:16:57.270
That is absolutely not a barrier.

01:16:57.270 --> 01:16:58.500
We saw in Covid, you know,

01:16:58.500 --> 01:17:01.200
over a million people volunteered
to participate in research

01:17:01.200 --> 01:17:02.580
and we've seen through
the new functionality

01:17:02.580 --> 01:17:03.840
we've built into the NHS app,

01:17:03.840 --> 01:17:06.330
which builds in, that links into

01:17:06.330 --> 01:17:08.100
NIHRs being part of research,

01:17:08.100 --> 01:17:10.140
that we've seen over
50,000 people volunteer

01:17:10.140 --> 01:17:11.880
since we launched that in February.

01:17:11.880 --> 01:17:13.710
So tens of thousands of people,

01:17:13.710 --> 01:17:15.210
I think there was 8,000 people last week,

01:17:15.210 --> 01:17:17.700
are actively trying to
participate in research.

01:17:17.700 --> 01:17:19.740
So it's worth noting that actually

01:17:19.740 --> 01:17:22.230
the public wants to
participate in research.

01:17:22.230 --> 01:17:23.490
So I think I just wanted
to to mention that

01:17:23.490 --> 01:17:25.530
as a non-barrier.

01:17:25.530 --> 01:17:27.780
And on the Academic
Health Science Network.

01:17:27.780 --> 01:17:29.070
So we've recently reviewed,

01:17:29.070 --> 01:17:32.400
so their relicensing period
was for a period of five years,

01:17:32.400 --> 01:17:34.080
that comes to an end this month.

01:17:34.080 --> 01:17:36.420
We have renewed that for a
year whilst we understand

01:17:36.420 --> 01:17:37.560
what is it that the functions

01:17:37.560 --> 01:17:39.120
that we really want them to undertake are,

01:17:39.120 --> 01:17:41.820
because over the period
of that five years,

01:17:41.820 --> 01:17:43.653
and actually their existence before that,

01:17:43.653 --> 01:17:46.050
what people have wanted
to work with them on

01:17:46.050 --> 01:17:48.900
and use them for has changed and evolved.

01:17:48.900 --> 01:17:50.400
And what we've noticed is happening

01:17:50.400 --> 01:17:52.860
is actually more and more
sort of programmes of work

01:17:52.860 --> 01:17:55.800
are landing with Academic
Health Science Networks,

01:17:55.800 --> 01:17:59.070
sometimes because people see
them as the best place to work

01:17:59.070 --> 01:18:00.630
and to deliver that work.

01:18:00.630 --> 01:18:02.940
Other times because there
isn't a clear alternative

01:18:02.940 --> 01:18:04.804
for other places that that work can do.

01:18:04.804 --> 01:18:05.910
So a lot of the work that Tim and I

01:18:05.910 --> 01:18:07.590
and Amanda are working on is actually

01:18:07.590 --> 01:18:11.010
what are the key functions
around the adoption innovation

01:18:11.010 --> 01:18:13.200
that we want the Academic
Health Science Networks

01:18:13.200 --> 01:18:14.520
to focus on?

01:18:14.520 --> 01:18:16.380
So we'll have more in the next few months

01:18:16.380 --> 01:18:18.363
on where we're going with that.

01:18:19.237 --> 01:18:20.130
There was a really important link

01:18:20.130 --> 01:18:22.860
to the applied research collaboratives.

01:18:22.860 --> 01:18:25.740
So these are organisations with experts

01:18:25.740 --> 01:18:28.980
in translation-type research.

01:18:28.980 --> 01:18:31.621
Really interestingly, when
I first joined NHS England,

01:18:31.621 --> 01:18:34.263
there was a meeting between
Academic Health Science Network,

01:18:34.263 --> 01:18:35.730
chairs and chief officers,

01:18:35.730 --> 01:18:37.740
and the Applied Research Collaboratives

01:18:37.740 --> 01:18:39.270
chairs and chief officers.

01:18:39.270 --> 01:18:41.130
And there was sort of a loose agreement

01:18:41.130 --> 01:18:44.160
to potentially work together on one area,

01:18:44.160 --> 01:18:47.640
but this was after about seven
or eight hours of discussions

01:18:47.640 --> 01:18:49.260
and that was as far as it got.

01:18:49.260 --> 01:18:50.370
Fast forward three years,

01:18:50.370 --> 01:18:53.580
they have much more joined
up programmes of work.

01:18:53.580 --> 01:18:56.070
My team has jointly
funded work between them

01:18:56.070 --> 01:18:58.170
to look at areas that are
coming out of the research.

01:18:58.170 --> 01:18:59.580
So what we're trying
to do through that work

01:18:59.580 --> 01:19:02.130
is work with the Academic
Health Science Networks

01:19:02.130 --> 01:19:03.330
who are closer to the systems

01:19:03.330 --> 01:19:05.220
and looking at the
problems systems are facing

01:19:05.220 --> 01:19:07.050
and actually link that into the work plans

01:19:07.050 --> 01:19:08.820
of the applied research collaboratives

01:19:08.820 --> 01:19:10.380
so that you're actually looking to put

01:19:10.380 --> 01:19:12.780
the evaluation expertise onto problems

01:19:12.780 --> 01:19:14.340
that local systems are facing.

01:19:14.340 --> 01:19:16.140
And you don't end up with what it was

01:19:16.140 --> 01:19:17.640
sort of two or three years ago,

01:19:17.640 --> 01:19:20.940
whereby you would see researchers

01:19:20.940 --> 01:19:22.680
researching interesting questions

01:19:22.680 --> 01:19:24.750
and wondering why the
outputs of that research

01:19:24.750 --> 01:19:27.750
were not being translated
into changes in practise.

01:19:27.750 --> 01:19:30.540
So there is work on going
to look at that has been,

01:19:30.540 --> 01:19:32.070
that has really sort of
transformed the way that

01:19:32.070 --> 01:19:34.530
they are working together
and that will form part

01:19:34.530 --> 01:19:36.540
of the work that was there,

01:19:36.540 --> 01:19:38.580
what we want Academic
Health Science Networks

01:19:38.580 --> 01:19:39.473
to work on in the future.

01:19:39.473 --> 01:19:41.490
I think the important thing to note with

01:19:41.490 --> 01:19:43.604
the Academic Health
Science Networks is that

01:19:43.604 --> 01:19:46.680
they're not providing
direct clinical care.

01:19:46.680 --> 01:19:49.170
They don't employ the clinicians
that are making the changes

01:19:49.170 --> 01:19:51.150
and they're not sort of
accountable for delivery.

01:19:51.150 --> 01:19:53.400
They are a delivery
partner within the system.

01:19:53.400 --> 01:19:54.810
So a lot of this work
will actually have to

01:19:54.810 --> 01:19:58.800
sort of rebalance all of
those different factors.

01:19:58.800 --> 01:19:59.760
- Yeah.

01:19:59.760 --> 01:20:03.900
And then, I know we're
running on time, so apologies.

01:20:03.900 --> 01:20:06.450
I just wanted to say, thank you, Navina,

01:20:06.450 --> 01:20:07.825
for your comments

01:20:07.825 --> 01:20:11.553
and I think they're exactly on point.

01:20:12.551 --> 01:20:16.290
And, yes, is is the quick answer.

01:20:16.290 --> 01:20:17.950
I think we should support

01:20:20.220 --> 01:20:23.700
the going forward programmes
that you described.

01:20:23.700 --> 01:20:26.380
And then, Wol, to your question

01:20:27.900 --> 01:20:30.150
around the difficulty getting stuff in,

01:20:30.150 --> 01:20:32.130
I completely agree with you.

01:20:32.130 --> 01:20:34.110
This goes back to the
comment I made earlier

01:20:34.110 --> 01:20:35.523
about risk appetite.

01:20:38.245 --> 01:20:40.380
And I do think we are better now

01:20:40.380 --> 01:20:44.520
with the signposting programme
that we just launched.

01:20:44.520 --> 01:20:47.010
I think that is, we are
getting positive signals

01:20:47.010 --> 01:20:49.580
from the commercial...

01:20:51.720 --> 01:20:56.370
from the commercial industry
about ability to manage

01:20:56.370 --> 01:21:00.180
the complexity of getting in

01:21:00.180 --> 01:21:03.960
to understand what the steps are.

01:21:03.960 --> 01:21:07.710
But, again, it is, the marketplace
for innovation coming in

01:21:07.710 --> 01:21:09.750
has been highly fragmented,

01:21:09.750 --> 01:21:14.750
and I do think that 40,
managing 42 relationships

01:21:15.480 --> 01:21:20.480
is a lot simpler than managing
260-odd trust relationships.

01:21:22.380 --> 01:21:24.780
I also will just say,

01:21:24.780 --> 01:21:27.450
and Julian can come in on this point,

01:21:27.450 --> 01:21:32.340
but we are more and more,
when it is appropriate,

01:21:32.340 --> 01:21:35.610
moving to national deals,

01:21:35.610 --> 01:21:39.540
following on the leverage
that was clearly seen

01:21:39.540 --> 01:21:42.690
in the pharmaceutical areas,

01:21:42.690 --> 01:21:46.230
and particularly around technology.

01:21:46.230 --> 01:21:49.800
We signed a very important
contract with Microsoft

01:21:49.800 --> 01:21:51.500
on the 365...

01:21:52.380 --> 01:21:53.790
a few years ago.

01:21:53.790 --> 01:21:56.370
That really was a big change in the way,

01:21:56.370 --> 01:21:58.080
how we approach technology.

01:21:58.080 --> 01:22:02.560
And that arrangement saved
hundreds of millions of pounds

01:22:03.570 --> 01:22:05.460
by doing it at scale when,

01:22:05.460 --> 01:22:07.230
when you're talking about a commodity,

01:22:07.230 --> 01:22:10.579
a technology change in
a commodity service.

01:22:10.579 --> 01:22:14.760
So I think all of these are part of the,

01:22:14.760 --> 01:22:18.120
there is no, I don't
think there is a panacea,

01:22:18.120 --> 01:22:19.653
a silver bullet for,

01:22:20.700 --> 01:22:24.150
for the challenges in
the adoption and spread.

01:22:24.150 --> 01:22:28.710
But I have actually
great confidence that we

01:22:28.710 --> 01:22:31.530
have itemised the barriers

01:22:31.530 --> 01:22:34.620
and we are going after
each of them one at a time.

01:22:34.620 --> 01:22:38.670
And I think that is already
showing some progress.

01:22:38.670 --> 01:22:39.603
- Sorry, stakes?

01:22:41.520 --> 01:22:42.353
We take equity stakes.

01:22:42.353 --> 01:22:43.320
- Oh, equity stakes.

01:22:43.320 --> 01:22:48.320
So, actually we do have, this
is a very hot topic right now.

01:22:48.720 --> 01:22:53.580
And there, we do have an
economist working on exactly

01:22:53.580 --> 01:22:55.650
the framing that is a,

01:22:55.650 --> 01:22:58.533
something that we happy to
bring back to the board.

01:22:59.760 --> 01:23:02.704
It's in the later stages of development.

01:23:02.704 --> 01:23:06.185
I think it's a terrific
topic to bring back.

01:23:06.185 --> 01:23:10.230
It has all kinds of nuances with it.

01:23:10.230 --> 01:23:12.300
I think it'll be a great discussion.

01:23:12.300 --> 01:23:13.133
- [Wol] Thank you.

01:23:13.133 --> 01:23:14.850
- Amanda, would you like to close up?

01:23:14.850 --> 01:23:16.140
You've talked a lot with me

01:23:16.140 --> 01:23:17.580
about innovation.
- Yeah.

01:23:17.580 --> 01:23:18.616
- Do you want to?

01:23:18.616 --> 01:23:21.243
- Yeah, just look, can
I just, very quickly,

01:23:22.195 --> 01:23:27.090
just thank Tim and Matt for
their leadership in this space,

01:23:27.090 --> 01:23:28.230
but also, he's not here though,

01:23:28.230 --> 01:23:30.420
he used to be a member
of our board, Ara Darzi.

01:23:30.420 --> 01:23:32.010
- Yeah.
- Who chairs

01:23:32.010 --> 01:23:33.300
the Accelerated Access Collaborative

01:23:33.300 --> 01:23:35.940
and has been a real architect
of a lot of what we're now

01:23:35.940 --> 01:23:38.220
seeing the benefit of
and talking about today,

01:23:38.220 --> 01:23:42.600
continues to be a huge
part of this agenda.

01:23:42.600 --> 01:23:45.030
And I think what's been really
helpful in this discussion

01:23:45.030 --> 01:23:47.820
is we've done, we've done the balance well

01:23:47.820 --> 01:23:49.530
between acknowledging the staff,

01:23:49.530 --> 01:23:51.630
which is really positive,

01:23:51.630 --> 01:23:54.120
and the huge progress that has been made

01:23:54.120 --> 01:23:56.130
in all of the priority
areas we've discussed

01:23:56.130 --> 01:23:58.200
and many other areas, Richard,

01:23:58.200 --> 01:24:00.270
as you and I often reflect
on the amount of change

01:24:00.270 --> 01:24:02.730
that's happened in the NHS
over the past few years,

01:24:02.730 --> 01:24:04.560
but we've also acknowledged,
I think really clearly,

01:24:04.560 --> 01:24:06.150
where some of the challenges are.

01:24:06.150 --> 01:24:08.640
And actually, that's the
point of this paper, isn't it?

01:24:08.640 --> 01:24:11.190
To say, how do we now
take all of the stuff

01:24:11.190 --> 01:24:13.770
that we have built, the
foundational layers,

01:24:13.770 --> 01:24:16.410
some of the infrastructure
that we now have in place,

01:24:16.410 --> 01:24:19.522
recognise the progress,
but also recognise the,

01:24:19.522 --> 01:24:23.280
the current challenges
and the future ambition

01:24:23.280 --> 01:24:25.380
and make sure that we're
lining all of that up.

01:24:25.380 --> 01:24:28.290
So we're doing two things, and again,

01:24:28.290 --> 01:24:32.610
hugely welcome the both and
focus we're doing under,

01:24:32.610 --> 01:24:33.717
again, your leadership.

01:24:33.717 --> 01:24:35.940
And with Roland's support now as well,

01:24:35.940 --> 01:24:40.350
that long-term blueprint for
how the NHS continues to be,

01:24:40.350 --> 01:24:43.380
and it's an even better
innovation partner.

01:24:43.380 --> 01:24:45.510
And that's just completely essential

01:24:45.510 --> 01:24:47.100
for us to deliver on the ambition here.

01:24:47.100 --> 01:24:50.250
We talk about our operational challenges,

01:24:50.250 --> 01:24:52.767
we talk about, we were
talking earlier about cancer,

01:24:52.767 --> 01:24:55.830
you know, if we really want to
go for that early diagnosis,

01:24:55.830 --> 01:24:56.700
they need to innovate.

01:24:56.700 --> 01:24:59.280
The Galleri trial is
just a fantastic example

01:24:59.280 --> 01:25:00.450
of that in practise right now.

01:25:00.450 --> 01:25:03.390
So this isn't two different
agendas, this is one agenda.

01:25:03.390 --> 01:25:05.400
So that long-term blueprint,

01:25:05.400 --> 01:25:08.250
but secondly, and I think,
again, I hugely welcome this,

01:25:08.250 --> 01:25:10.530
it's a what can we do
now, the here and now.

01:25:10.530 --> 01:25:13.230
Some of that is embedded in
things like the AHSN review,

01:25:13.230 --> 01:25:15.390
but some of it is also just
the really practical stuff

01:25:15.390 --> 01:25:18.620
that we can build on the
scale and spread challenge

01:25:18.620 --> 01:25:20.460
of the market, entry challenge, you know,

01:25:20.460 --> 01:25:22.020
all of the things we've just discussed,

01:25:22.020 --> 01:25:24.420
and for me that's the right balance

01:25:24.420 --> 01:25:27.750
between "do now," but also, "do tomorrow."

01:25:27.750 --> 01:25:29.322
- Thank you.

01:25:29.322 --> 01:25:30.283
So, we're going to move on.

01:25:30.283 --> 01:25:32.697
I would just reflect though that,

01:25:32.697 --> 01:25:34.920
actually was Wol's point that...

01:25:34.920 --> 01:25:38.010
innovation is not just in
research and life science,

01:25:38.010 --> 01:25:40.650
it's actually innovation, how we operate.

01:25:40.650 --> 01:25:41.483
- Absolutely.

01:25:41.483 --> 01:25:42.420
- Our earlier conversation today

01:25:42.420 --> 01:25:45.145
on the Federated Data Platform,

01:25:45.145 --> 01:25:47.460
and once procured and once rolled out,

01:25:47.460 --> 01:25:49.050
how that might change workflow

01:25:49.050 --> 01:25:50.010
and working perhaps--
- Absolutely.

01:25:50.010 --> 01:25:52.500
- Is actually it could be revolutionary,

01:25:52.500 --> 01:25:55.113
hopefully revolutionary.

01:25:56.190 --> 01:25:58.200
Part of thing, I think it
did maybe risk appetite.

01:25:58.200 --> 01:26:00.540
I also think given the pressure,
the immediate pressures,

01:26:00.540 --> 01:26:02.880
it's also, I'll call it head space,

01:26:02.880 --> 01:26:04.920
or capacity to experiment to try.

01:26:04.920 --> 01:26:07.050
And I think a lot of our systems

01:26:07.050 --> 01:26:08.400
are under very, very real pressure.

01:26:08.400 --> 01:26:10.260
So it may partly be risk appetite,

01:26:10.260 --> 01:26:14.760
it may actually partly just
be the capacity actually to,

01:26:14.760 --> 01:26:15.810
to pursue these initiatives.

01:26:15.810 --> 01:26:19.470
But, Matt, thank you very much
and for your huge enthusiasm,

01:26:19.470 --> 01:26:21.780
and, Tim, thank you for your leadership.

01:26:21.780 --> 01:26:23.490
And Amanda, absolutely right, Ara Darzi

01:26:23.490 --> 01:26:25.947
has been a brilliant per janitor

01:26:25.947 --> 01:26:28.710
of much of what we see today.

01:26:28.710 --> 01:26:30.900
So, can we go on then to the next item?

01:26:30.900 --> 01:26:33.000
So, Ruth, I think you're going to lead in

01:26:33.000 --> 01:26:35.040
and you've got three colleagues with us.

01:26:35.040 --> 01:26:37.440
So, again, welcome.

01:26:37.440 --> 01:26:40.353
So, Jacqui and Matthew
and Duncan, welcome.

01:26:41.220 --> 01:26:42.660
Ruth, to you on maternity.

01:26:42.660 --> 01:26:44.880
- Thank you very much, and
thank you for the opportunity

01:26:44.880 --> 01:26:48.210
to talk all things maternity and neonatal.

01:26:48.210 --> 01:26:49.230
And first, apology,

01:26:49.230 --> 01:26:51.630
we are missing one very important person,

01:26:51.630 --> 01:26:54.780
Ngozi, our Clinical Lead for Neonatology,

01:26:54.780 --> 01:26:57.300
but she's on annual leave and
that's quite important too,

01:26:57.300 --> 01:27:02.040
so, but I wanted to recognise
that we come as a whole team.

01:27:02.040 --> 01:27:04.590
So, a year ago today,

01:27:04.590 --> 01:27:06.510
the final Ockenden Report was published,

01:27:06.510 --> 01:27:09.240
setting out the findings, conclusions,

01:27:09.240 --> 01:27:12.360
and the essential actions
from the independent review

01:27:12.360 --> 01:27:15.453
of Maternity Services at
Shrewsbury and Telford.

01:27:16.470 --> 01:27:20.190
Improving Maternity Services
continues to be a priority

01:27:20.190 --> 01:27:22.320
for the whole of the NHS,

01:27:22.320 --> 01:27:24.960
and implementation of
the essential actions

01:27:24.960 --> 01:27:29.550
has been a key focus for us
all over the last 12 months.

01:27:29.550 --> 01:27:32.100
Last week, as Amanda has already said,

01:27:32.100 --> 01:27:34.920
we joined, along with colleagues
on the end of the table,

01:27:34.920 --> 01:27:39.920
we joined over 600 NHS colleagues in Leeds

01:27:39.990 --> 01:27:42.690
for our first Maternity
and Neonatal Summit.

01:27:42.690 --> 01:27:45.570
But they weren't just NHS colleagues,

01:27:45.570 --> 01:27:49.520
there were colleagues as
well that were users and...

01:27:51.401 --> 01:27:54.637
experienced users that
were part of our day.

01:27:55.950 --> 01:27:57.840
It was an opportunity for colleagues

01:27:57.840 --> 01:28:01.170
across Maternity and Neonatal
Services to engage, to learn,

01:28:01.170 --> 01:28:03.090
to debate, to challenge,

01:28:03.090 --> 01:28:06.240
and to recommit to our shared ambition

01:28:06.240 --> 01:28:09.870
to improving Maternity
and Neonatal Services.

01:28:09.870 --> 01:28:13.200
The enthusiasm and drive to
improve Maternity Services

01:28:13.200 --> 01:28:16.290
for women and families from those present

01:28:16.290 --> 01:28:20.190
was absolutely crystal clear.

01:28:20.190 --> 01:28:23.820
We started the day by
hearing from service users,

01:28:23.820 --> 01:28:27.240
Chloe Abbey and Jenny Tatter.

01:28:27.240 --> 01:28:30.180
They opened the summit
with a powerful session

01:28:30.180 --> 01:28:32.820
and spoke with the importance
of listening to women

01:28:32.820 --> 01:28:36.810
and families and addressing inequalities.

01:28:36.810 --> 01:28:38.430
We also heard, firsthand,

01:28:38.430 --> 01:28:41.010
from Donna Ockenden and Bill Kirkup

01:28:41.010 --> 01:28:43.803
about their investigations
and the key learning.

01:28:44.760 --> 01:28:47.730
Great examples of work
taking place across England

01:28:47.730 --> 01:28:49.350
were shared at the summit

01:28:49.350 --> 01:28:51.030
and it allowed discussion

01:28:51.030 --> 01:28:55.353
on the importance of doing just
that, sharing the learning.

01:28:56.460 --> 01:28:59.100
Leaders of the service
reemphasize the need

01:28:59.100 --> 01:29:01.920
to simplify the number of asks,

01:29:01.920 --> 01:29:05.820
and for clarity on areas to concentrate on

01:29:05.820 --> 01:29:09.180
and to lift the focus on neonatal services

01:29:09.180 --> 01:29:12.813
within our approach to maternity
and neonatal going forward.

01:29:13.800 --> 01:29:15.600
This is why we are bringing together

01:29:15.600 --> 01:29:18.810
the recommendations from a
number of those key reports

01:29:18.810 --> 01:29:20.640
in a three year delivery plan

01:29:20.640 --> 01:29:23.430
for Maternity and Neonatal Services,

01:29:23.430 --> 01:29:25.383
which we are publishing today.

01:29:26.610 --> 01:29:29.160
At the board meeting
in December, last year,

01:29:29.160 --> 01:29:34.160
Wol, you asked us to
be direct, to be clear

01:29:34.230 --> 01:29:36.750
and to be challenging with the plan.

01:29:36.750 --> 01:29:38.673
That is what we have set out to do.

01:29:40.974 --> 01:29:42.690
Our new three year delivery plan

01:29:42.690 --> 01:29:46.200
for Maternity and Neonatal
Services sets out clearly

01:29:46.200 --> 01:29:49.260
how local teams and staff can take action

01:29:49.260 --> 01:29:52.800
to tackle the issues
raised by these reviews,

01:29:52.800 --> 01:29:56.400
providing four key areas of focus.

01:29:56.400 --> 01:30:00.120
Listening to women, growing the workforce,

01:30:00.120 --> 01:30:04.350
improving culture and equity of care

01:30:04.350 --> 01:30:06.630
to ensure women, babies and families

01:30:06.630 --> 01:30:08.670
receive the highest quality of care

01:30:08.670 --> 01:30:11.013
that meets their individual needs.

01:30:12.270 --> 01:30:14.390
It brings together recommendations set out

01:30:14.390 --> 01:30:19.350
in our previous national
strategy, Better Births,

01:30:19.350 --> 01:30:21.870
the Maternity Transformation Programme,

01:30:21.870 --> 01:30:24.660
Donna Ockenden's review
into Maternity Services

01:30:24.660 --> 01:30:26.370
at Shrewsbury and Telford

01:30:26.370 --> 01:30:30.483
and Bill Kirkup's report into East Kent.

01:30:31.560 --> 01:30:35.253
Services have asked for this
plan and we've listened.

01:30:37.080 --> 01:30:40.920
The plan has been co-designed
with women and families.

01:30:40.920 --> 01:30:42.660
In taking this forward.

01:30:42.660 --> 01:30:46.020
ICBs must ensure Maternity and Neonatal

01:30:46.020 --> 01:30:48.720
voice partnerships continue to be central

01:30:48.720 --> 01:30:50.793
to their work in delivering this plan.

01:30:52.110 --> 01:30:55.650
And recognising that
the review into services

01:30:55.650 --> 01:30:58.650
in Nottingham is currently underway,

01:30:58.650 --> 01:31:01.140
we will ensure any
additional learning from this

01:31:01.140 --> 01:31:04.263
is embraced and included in our work.

01:31:06.450 --> 01:31:08.940
As we have reported to
the board, previously,

01:31:08.940 --> 01:31:12.150
workforce has been a
substantial area of focus

01:31:12.150 --> 01:31:14.130
since the interim Ockenden Report

01:31:14.130 --> 01:31:16.683
was published back in December, 2020.

01:31:17.670 --> 01:31:21.540
We continue also to focus
on the government's half it,

01:31:21.540 --> 01:31:24.780
ambitions on neonatal maternity,

01:31:24.780 --> 01:31:28.350
maternal mortality and
serious brain injury.

01:31:28.350 --> 01:31:30.450
One example is a new version

01:31:30.450 --> 01:31:33.210
of the Saving Baby Lives Care Bundle,

01:31:33.210 --> 01:31:34.893
which will be launched very soon.

01:31:36.360 --> 01:31:38.850
Bill Kirkup's "Reading the Signals" report

01:31:38.850 --> 01:31:41.010
reinforce the improvements we know

01:31:41.010 --> 01:31:43.110
must be implemented across the NHS

01:31:43.110 --> 01:31:45.660
to make England the safest in the world

01:31:45.660 --> 01:31:47.043
for women to give birth.

01:31:48.000 --> 01:31:49.680
One of the recommendations relates

01:31:49.680 --> 01:31:51.960
to improved outcomes data.

01:31:51.960 --> 01:31:54.997
It says, "The prompt
establishment of a task force

01:31:54.997 --> 01:31:58.387
"with appropriate membership
to drive the introduction

01:31:58.387 --> 01:32:01.777
"of valid maternity and
neonatal outcome measures

01:32:01.777 --> 01:32:05.677
"capable of differentiating
signals amongst the noise

01:32:05.677 --> 01:32:09.090
"to display significant
trends and outliers."

01:32:09.090 --> 01:32:10.770
That's one of the recommendations

01:32:10.770 --> 01:32:13.680
from Bill Kirkup's East Ken Report.

01:32:13.680 --> 01:32:15.360
So we have therefore established

01:32:15.360 --> 01:32:18.000
a Maternity and Neonatal Outcomes Group,

01:32:18.000 --> 01:32:20.550
which will ensure that
effective outcome measures

01:32:20.550 --> 01:32:22.980
identify those early signals

01:32:22.980 --> 01:32:25.473
and early signals of changes in activity.

01:32:26.610 --> 01:32:29.010
Membership of this group
will include stakeholders

01:32:29.010 --> 01:32:32.670
from across the NHS, its
partner organisations

01:32:32.670 --> 01:32:35.673
and service user representation.

01:32:36.810 --> 01:32:40.080
I am pleased, in fact,
I'm delighted to announce

01:32:40.080 --> 01:32:43.140
that Dr. Edile Murdoch has been appointed

01:32:43.140 --> 01:32:44.730
as the chair of this group,

01:32:44.730 --> 01:32:48.243
and that Dr. Bill Kirkup will
act as a special advisor.

01:32:49.290 --> 01:32:52.440
Dr. Edile Murdoch is a
Consultant Neonatologist

01:32:52.440 --> 01:32:54.030
in NHS Lothian

01:32:54.030 --> 01:32:55.560
and has a wealth of experience

01:32:55.560 --> 01:32:59.340
in delivering improvements in
Maternity and Neonatal Care.

01:32:59.340 --> 01:33:01.410
She chairs a wide range of groups

01:33:01.410 --> 01:33:03.570
and has experience in external reviews

01:33:03.570 --> 01:33:06.453
of Maternity and Neonatal
services across the UK.

01:33:07.467 --> 01:33:10.320
Edile and Bill's extensive experience

01:33:10.320 --> 01:33:12.600
will be extremely valuable in leading

01:33:12.600 --> 01:33:14.253
this vital piece of work.

01:33:15.960 --> 01:33:19.200
I'd also like to take the
opportunity to thank and recognise

01:33:19.200 --> 01:33:22.050
the work of the Independent
Maternity Working Group.

01:33:22.050 --> 01:33:25.650
It provides strong
oversight of implementation

01:33:25.650 --> 01:33:29.700
of Ockenden and East Kent's
review recommendations.

01:33:29.700 --> 01:33:32.010
Establishment of this
group was one of the key

01:33:32.010 --> 01:33:34.740
immediate and essential actions

01:33:34.740 --> 01:33:39.033
that Donna Ockenden announced
this time today last year.

01:33:40.350 --> 01:33:42.600
It enables a collaborative approach,

01:33:42.600 --> 01:33:44.790
and I would like to thank the co-chairs,

01:33:44.790 --> 01:33:46.140
Gill Walton, Chief Executive

01:33:46.140 --> 01:33:49.080
of the Royal College of
Midwives and Dr. Eddie Morris,

01:33:49.080 --> 01:33:51.180
President of the Royal College

01:33:51.180 --> 01:33:54.540
of Obstetrics and Gynaecologist.

01:33:54.540 --> 01:33:57.493
He's obviously now passed
that baton to Ranee Thakar

01:33:57.493 --> 01:33:59.190
and I want to thank her

01:33:59.190 --> 01:34:01.323
and also all the members of the group.

01:34:02.340 --> 01:34:05.880
Also, as Sarah Jane Marsh
has now taken up her post

01:34:05.880 --> 01:34:08.400
and a new post within NHS England,

01:34:08.400 --> 01:34:10.710
we will be seeking to appoint a new chair

01:34:10.710 --> 01:34:14.880
of the NHS England Maternity
and Neonatal Delivery Board

01:34:14.880 --> 01:34:16.233
as soon as possible.

01:34:17.280 --> 01:34:19.838
I would also like to thank
Baroness Julia Cumberlege

01:34:19.838 --> 01:34:21.150
and Sir Cyril Chantler

01:34:21.150 --> 01:34:22.970
for their dedication and leadership

01:34:22.970 --> 01:34:25.260
of the Maternity Stakeholder Council,

01:34:25.260 --> 01:34:27.840
and who will be stepping down tomorrow

01:34:27.840 --> 01:34:30.720
following seven years in post.

01:34:30.720 --> 01:34:32.430
I'm delighted to inform you too,

01:34:32.430 --> 01:34:35.220
that we've appointed Louise Weaver-Lowe

01:34:35.220 --> 01:34:38.010
to the role of Neonatal Nurse Advisor.

01:34:38.010 --> 01:34:40.050
Louise, who is currently the Director

01:34:40.050 --> 01:34:43.800
of Northwest Neonatal
Operational Delivery Network,

01:34:43.800 --> 01:34:46.860
will work alongside our
neonatal clinical team,

01:34:46.860 --> 01:34:49.530
Jacqui, Ngozi and Matthew

01:34:49.530 --> 01:34:52.770
to provide a neonatal
nursing clinical advice

01:34:52.770 --> 01:34:55.593
and champion the neonatal workforce.

01:34:56.700 --> 01:34:59.100
I will now, Chair, handover to Duncan,

01:34:59.100 --> 01:35:01.710
who will explain how
we have built this plan

01:35:01.710 --> 01:35:04.410
and how collectively we will deliver it.

01:35:04.410 --> 01:35:08.250
Jacqui and Matthew will then
follow with some key points.

01:35:08.250 --> 01:35:09.083
- Okay, you must also,

01:35:09.083 --> 01:35:12.540
I know, it's a, must also
give us time for questions.

01:35:12.540 --> 01:35:13.373
- Of course.

01:35:13.373 --> 01:35:14.820
- There will be, I'm sure, questions.

01:35:14.820 --> 01:35:16.170
So, Duncan?

01:35:16.170 --> 01:35:17.940
- Yep, thanks, and good afternoon.

01:35:17.940 --> 01:35:21.180
So I guess just to start by just reminding

01:35:21.180 --> 01:35:22.890
the board of the scale of our services.

01:35:22.890 --> 01:35:27.340
So, on average, one baby is
welcomed into the NHS in England

01:35:27.340 --> 01:35:29.250
every 54 seconds.

01:35:29.250 --> 01:35:32.730
So just to remind you
really that the scale

01:35:32.730 --> 01:35:35.813
of what we are trying to do and to support

01:35:35.813 --> 01:35:37.980
for women and families

01:35:37.980 --> 01:35:39.930
and our colleagues across England.

01:35:39.930 --> 01:35:41.520
So, in developing the plan,

01:35:41.520 --> 01:35:44.130
we've spent a lot of time listening.

01:35:44.130 --> 01:35:47.430
We've held over 50 discussion
events, we've done surveys,

01:35:47.430 --> 01:35:50.220
we've engaged with over
3,000 service users,

01:35:50.220 --> 01:35:52.860
staff leaders and stakeholders.

01:35:52.860 --> 01:35:55.650
We've also brought together the learning,

01:35:55.650 --> 01:35:59.070
clearly from the Ockenden
Report and the Kirkup Reports,

01:35:59.070 --> 01:36:03.570
but also from areas such
as the CQC Maternity Survey

01:36:03.570 --> 01:36:05.824
and the learning that comes

01:36:05.824 --> 01:36:07.920
from their organisational reviews.

01:36:07.920 --> 01:36:09.630
And we've also taken, as Ruth has said,

01:36:09.630 --> 01:36:11.880
advice from the Independent Working Group

01:36:11.880 --> 01:36:12.990
made up of royal colleges,

01:36:12.990 --> 01:36:15.480
but also service user
voice representatives

01:36:15.480 --> 01:36:16.800
and frontline colleagues.

01:36:16.800 --> 01:36:18.180
And I'm really grateful, actually,

01:36:18.180 --> 01:36:19.440
for their perspective on this,

01:36:19.440 --> 01:36:22.800
and they've helped inform
the development of this plan.

01:36:22.800 --> 01:36:26.190
So it's enabled us to
not only listen to that,

01:36:26.190 --> 01:36:29.700
but to test out those four
themes and build upon them.

01:36:29.700 --> 01:36:31.710
And Jackie and Matthew
will talk a little bit more

01:36:31.710 --> 01:36:33.480
about them in a second.

01:36:33.480 --> 01:36:35.130
But that's also enabled us

01:36:35.130 --> 01:36:38.720
to identify 12 objectives within the plan.

01:36:38.720 --> 01:36:42.389
And what we've aimed
to do is not to set out

01:36:42.389 --> 01:36:46.980
new policy through this, but
to how we can deliver change.

01:36:46.980 --> 01:36:49.170
And, therefore, we've
set this out to make sure

01:36:49.170 --> 01:36:50.040
that it's really clear,

01:36:50.040 --> 01:36:52.050
all the responsibilities at every level.

01:36:52.050 --> 01:36:54.720
So that's NHS trust level,

01:36:54.720 --> 01:36:57.660
our integrated care boards and systems,

01:36:57.660 --> 01:37:00.360
including the local Maternity
and Neonatal Systems

01:37:00.360 --> 01:37:02.010
and our operational delivery networks

01:37:02.010 --> 01:37:04.080
that work within neonatal services,

01:37:04.080 --> 01:37:07.500
and, of course, NHS England,
nationally and regionally.

01:37:07.500 --> 01:37:10.230
And it's deliberately written and directed

01:37:10.230 --> 01:37:12.390
at frontline staff and leadership,

01:37:12.390 --> 01:37:14.220
describing those building blocks

01:37:14.220 --> 01:37:16.787
that we need to have in
place for our services

01:37:16.787 --> 01:37:19.683
and to support our women,
babies and our families.

01:37:20.850 --> 01:37:21.683
So,

01:37:22.830 --> 01:37:26.310
we've also, really pleased
to see through this,

01:37:26.310 --> 01:37:30.090
the programme of work also
includes how we listen to

01:37:30.090 --> 01:37:32.550
and learn and work with women and families

01:37:32.550 --> 01:37:35.220
through our Maternity and
Neonatal Voice Partnership.

01:37:35.220 --> 01:37:37.980
So you will see through the
plan that that is a very

01:37:37.980 --> 01:37:41.253
strong key theme within that.

01:37:42.660 --> 01:37:44.520
We've also put in here, and
actually building on the,

01:37:44.520 --> 01:37:46.800
the previous discussion, actually,

01:37:46.800 --> 01:37:49.920
you'll see there's some
case studies in the report,

01:37:49.920 --> 01:37:51.990
which is reflective really of the tone

01:37:51.990 --> 01:37:53.490
that we want to set through this plan

01:37:53.490 --> 01:37:55.083
about making sure that we,

01:37:55.920 --> 01:37:59.280
we learn, we share from good
practise and do more of that.

01:37:59.280 --> 01:38:02.130
And that's part of our role
here at NHS England to do that.

01:38:02.130 --> 01:38:04.470
And so we've heard
through that engagement,

01:38:04.470 --> 01:38:06.690
we need to learn more
from the good practise

01:38:06.690 --> 01:38:10.800
as well as when things
unfortunately go wrong.

01:38:10.800 --> 01:38:12.600
So, following the board meeting today,

01:38:12.600 --> 01:38:15.270
we will write out to
trust, to ICB leaders,

01:38:15.270 --> 01:38:17.040
to our Maternity and Neonatal Leaders

01:38:17.040 --> 01:38:18.690
to inform them about this plan

01:38:18.690 --> 01:38:23.100
and ask them to start embedding
the themes into their work.

01:38:23.100 --> 01:38:24.960
And we've recognised that we've got

01:38:24.960 --> 01:38:28.500
Maternity and Neonatal Services
at very different stages

01:38:28.500 --> 01:38:30.900
already in implementing
many, many of these elements.

01:38:30.900 --> 01:38:33.330
So we've deliberately constructed this

01:38:33.330 --> 01:38:35.520
so that Maternity and Neonatal Systems

01:38:35.520 --> 01:38:37.530
are able to take this
plan and agree locally

01:38:37.530 --> 01:38:40.080
the delivery over the next three years.

01:38:40.080 --> 01:38:42.990
And I guess just my last point
really is that we will now,

01:38:42.990 --> 01:38:43.980
as a result of this,

01:38:43.980 --> 01:38:46.200
look at our NHSE governance around this,

01:38:46.200 --> 01:38:48.210
we've got an opportunity,
particularly with colleagues

01:38:48.210 --> 01:38:52.050
from Health Education
England, joining us to refocus

01:38:52.050 --> 01:38:55.873
the work of our Maternity
Transformation Board

01:38:55.873 --> 01:38:59.160
and reflect on the benefits that we've had

01:38:59.160 --> 01:39:00.930
from the independent working group

01:39:00.930 --> 01:39:02.820
and how we can deliver some efficiencies,

01:39:02.820 --> 01:39:04.020
particularly working with colleagues

01:39:04.020 --> 01:39:06.930
from Health Education England
on the workforce elements.

01:39:06.930 --> 01:39:09.300
But I'll handover to Jacqui and Matthew.

01:39:09.300 --> 01:39:10.200
- Thank you, Duncan.

01:39:10.200 --> 01:39:15.200
And we are incredibly excited that we can

01:39:16.170 --> 01:39:18.003
publish this plan today.

01:39:19.230 --> 01:39:22.920
There are many plans,
documents, papers, guidance,

01:39:22.920 --> 01:39:25.380
and many other items that influence

01:39:25.380 --> 01:39:27.510
how we provide healthcare provision.

01:39:27.510 --> 01:39:31.827
And this is why, based on
the engagement experience

01:39:32.730 --> 01:39:35.940
that we undertook to build this plan,

01:39:35.940 --> 01:39:37.950
we wanted this plan to be something

01:39:37.950 --> 01:39:40.500
that doesn't sit on the
shelf and is lifeless,

01:39:40.500 --> 01:39:44.640
but is dynamic and that
every clinical person,

01:39:44.640 --> 01:39:46.710
no matter how junior or senior,

01:39:46.710 --> 01:39:51.090
can pick this plan up and
understand it and enact it.

01:39:51.090 --> 01:39:54.360
And that's why, in each
section of the plan,

01:39:54.360 --> 01:39:58.230
there are the themes, there
are the key achievements

01:39:58.230 --> 01:40:01.470
and then there are, the
key ambitions, sorry,

01:40:01.470 --> 01:40:05.250
and then there are the key objectives

01:40:05.250 --> 01:40:09.150
and, very specifically,
how we'll deliver on those.

01:40:09.150 --> 01:40:12.840
So everybody has a part to
play in this architecture.

01:40:12.840 --> 01:40:16.740
The ICB's role, NHS England's role,

01:40:16.740 --> 01:40:18.750
providers' role,

01:40:18.750 --> 01:40:22.830
and this is reflected under each section.

01:40:22.830 --> 01:40:27.450
So that's, I think that's really
important to outline that,

01:40:27.450 --> 01:40:29.550
that we want this plan to be dynamic

01:40:29.550 --> 01:40:31.650
and not another document.

01:40:31.650 --> 01:40:33.360
And as Duncan and Ruth has alluded to,

01:40:33.360 --> 01:40:36.570
this is not something
that is relatively new.

01:40:36.570 --> 01:40:39.750
This is a culmination of better births.

01:40:39.750 --> 01:40:41.730
The report of the
National Maternity Review,

01:40:41.730 --> 01:40:44.010
a culmination of the Chapter,

01:40:44.010 --> 01:40:47.580
the Maternity Chapter in
the NHS long term plan,

01:40:47.580 --> 01:40:50.160
the Ockenden interim, the Ockenden final,

01:40:50.160 --> 01:40:52.410
the East Kent "Reading
the Signals Report,"

01:40:52.410 --> 01:40:55.680
and there were three race equality reports

01:40:55.680 --> 01:40:57.570
that were published last year.

01:40:57.570 --> 01:41:01.620
They also infiltrate this delivery plan.

01:41:01.620 --> 01:41:03.960
So I'm going to just say a few things,

01:41:03.960 --> 01:41:07.130
just a few key things about a few...

01:41:08.280 --> 01:41:10.530
key points in the plan and
then I'm going to hand over

01:41:10.530 --> 01:41:11.910
to Matthew.

01:41:11.910 --> 01:41:14.520
So the theme that I'm
going to start with is

01:41:14.520 --> 01:41:18.390
the listening to and working
with women and families

01:41:18.390 --> 01:41:20.070
with compassion,

01:41:20.070 --> 01:41:22.740
because we can make this
as dynamic as we want.

01:41:22.740 --> 01:41:25.650
We are dealing with people
who are serving people.

01:41:25.650 --> 01:41:29.070
So listening and responding
but not just listening.

01:41:29.070 --> 01:41:32.006
Throughout this plan, one has to hear,

01:41:32.006 --> 01:41:33.480
once you've listened,

01:41:33.480 --> 01:41:35.430
and then you have to understand

01:41:35.430 --> 01:41:39.480
and then you have to act in a
way that is mutually agreeable

01:41:39.480 --> 01:41:42.060
to the person that's
receiving that narrative.

01:41:42.060 --> 01:41:43.740
And that is really, really important

01:41:43.740 --> 01:41:46.530
so that families can
leave their consultations

01:41:46.530 --> 01:41:49.980
and feel and be invested and valued

01:41:49.980 --> 01:41:53.100
and a part of that journey going forward.

01:41:53.100 --> 01:41:56.190
So that is a really key
point within the plan.

01:41:56.190 --> 01:41:58.260
Empowering our staff to be able

01:41:58.260 --> 01:42:00.690
to provide personalised care.

01:42:00.690 --> 01:42:02.970
You know, I'm delighted that
we've managed to include

01:42:02.970 --> 01:42:04.197
in the plan something that was in

01:42:04.197 --> 01:42:07.980
the NHS long-term plan about
Pelvic Health Services,

01:42:07.980 --> 01:42:09.870
because in this country today,

01:42:09.870 --> 01:42:13.830
and this presses into the
Women's Health Strategy,

01:42:13.830 --> 01:42:15.480
we have urinary incontinence

01:42:15.480 --> 01:42:17.190
within the first year of childbirth,

01:42:17.190 --> 01:42:19.500
faecal incontinence,
pelvic organ prolapse,

01:42:19.500 --> 01:42:23.040
and this Perinatal Pelvic Health Service

01:42:23.040 --> 01:42:25.020
will assist in that space.

01:42:25.020 --> 01:42:28.140
The Maternal Mental Health
Service is really profound

01:42:28.140 --> 01:42:30.060
and that is featured in this plan

01:42:30.060 --> 01:42:34.470
and reflected through the
objectives and the key actions

01:42:34.470 --> 01:42:36.063
for all parts of the system.

01:42:36.930 --> 01:42:41.157
An this is an equally
significant part of the plan,

01:42:41.157 --> 01:42:44.730
at the least because we heard last year,

01:42:44.730 --> 01:42:47.910
autumn, from Embrace,
that death from suicide,

01:42:47.910 --> 01:42:50.580
maternal death from suicide is increasing.

01:42:50.580 --> 01:42:54.000
So really pleased that we've
got something very detailed

01:42:54.000 --> 01:42:56.500
in the plan about Maternal
Mental Health Services.

01:42:57.420 --> 01:43:02.040
Also, consistent bereavement
services across England

01:43:02.040 --> 01:43:04.560
so that we reduce the
unwarranted variation

01:43:04.560 --> 01:43:06.520
so that every bereaved family

01:43:08.756 --> 01:43:11.790
has the opportunity to have consistent

01:43:11.790 --> 01:43:14.880
high quality bereavement support.

01:43:14.880 --> 01:43:17.700
And, of course, then the Inequality Plan.

01:43:17.700 --> 01:43:20.070
Now we published the Inequality,

01:43:20.070 --> 01:43:23.610
the Equity Guidance 18 months ago,

01:43:23.610 --> 01:43:26.340
and that Equity Guidance
provides a framework

01:43:26.340 --> 01:43:31.340
for how we ensure safe and
personal care, equitably,

01:43:31.590 --> 01:43:34.680
regardless of skin colour,
socioeconomic disadvantage,

01:43:34.680 --> 01:43:36.750
or the way one speaks,

01:43:36.750 --> 01:43:39.330
and this plan reflects that guidance.

01:43:39.330 --> 01:43:41.700
So, there is nothing new.

01:43:41.700 --> 01:43:45.510
We are packaging all
these items in one space

01:43:45.510 --> 01:43:47.550
so that our clinical colleagues,

01:43:47.550 --> 01:43:52.200
our ICBs and our local maternity systems

01:43:52.200 --> 01:43:55.110
and National NHS England,
Regional NHS England

01:43:55.110 --> 01:43:57.303
can work with one plan.

01:43:58.740 --> 01:44:00.930
I'd also, just before
I hand over to Matthew,

01:44:00.930 --> 01:44:03.210
like to draw your attention to

01:44:03.210 --> 01:44:06.540
the growing, retaining and
supporting our workforce

01:44:06.540 --> 01:44:08.040
because, as I say,

01:44:08.040 --> 01:44:11.280
we can have a a great evidence-based plan,

01:44:11.280 --> 01:44:13.860
but unless we have the workforce

01:44:13.860 --> 01:44:18.860
that feel and are valued,
respected, and invested in,

01:44:19.740 --> 01:44:23.550
we won't be able to
deliver this plan at pace.

01:44:23.550 --> 01:44:27.300
So the Workforce Retaining
and Supporting Section

01:44:27.300 --> 01:44:30.270
is very detailed with
lots and lots of numbers.

01:44:30.270 --> 01:44:34.680
But, in summary, what we want
to do to support our services

01:44:34.680 --> 01:44:36.600
is to resource appropriately

01:44:36.600 --> 01:44:39.780
to ensure that we have
consistent safe staffing

01:44:39.780 --> 01:44:43.050
and to ensure that staff
have the opportunity

01:44:43.050 --> 01:44:46.030
to have appropriate continuing
professional development

01:44:46.890 --> 01:44:50.280
supported by frameworks and guidance from,

01:44:50.280 --> 01:44:53.550
us, regions, and indeed, ICBs.

01:44:53.550 --> 01:44:56.340
And that includes our neonatal staff too.

01:44:56.340 --> 01:45:00.270
Maternity and Neonatal Services
are inextricably linked,

01:45:00.270 --> 01:45:02.700
as are midwives and obstetricians.

01:45:02.700 --> 01:45:05.760
And you'll see that reflected
throughout the plan.

01:45:05.760 --> 01:45:07.500
- Jacqui, I'm going to
have to get you to pass

01:45:07.500 --> 01:45:09.510
the baton to Matthew,
otherwise we're going to,

01:45:09.510 --> 01:45:11.760
we won't, we'll have
no time for questions--

01:45:11.760 --> 01:45:13.776
- [Jacqui] And that was
my full stop, thank you.

01:45:13.776 --> 01:45:15.214
- That's good timing.

01:45:15.214 --> 01:45:16.846
And Matthew, probably,

01:45:16.846 --> 01:45:18.454
you're fourth in the relay.
- I'll be succinct.

01:45:18.454 --> 01:45:21.540
- Succinct.
- Be quite succinct.

01:45:21.540 --> 01:45:22.373
- So,

01:45:23.280 --> 01:45:26.163
Culture Learning and Safety
is an important thing.

01:45:28.020 --> 01:45:30.030
Lots of what Jacqui's
described will create

01:45:30.030 --> 01:45:33.240
the right environment for
good cultures to flourish.

01:45:33.240 --> 01:45:35.700
Treat your staff well, they behave well,

01:45:35.700 --> 01:45:38.790
they will look after the
women we care for better,

01:45:38.790 --> 01:45:41.910
but we're also putting a lot
of professional training,

01:45:41.910 --> 01:45:44.550
leadership, surveys to understand insights

01:45:44.550 --> 01:45:48.210
so people can really measure
the culture, drive improvement.

01:45:48.210 --> 01:45:49.960
There's lots in there about how we

01:45:51.330 --> 01:45:53.343
equip our staff to do the job better,

01:45:54.390 --> 01:45:57.060
and there's lots about how we
improve the safety structure

01:45:57.060 --> 01:46:01.080
aligning with the NHS England's
wider safety strategy.

01:46:01.080 --> 01:46:04.080
So it integrates with
our piece of strategy,

01:46:04.080 --> 01:46:06.390
but we've, Jack and I
have also developed our

01:46:06.390 --> 01:46:09.630
Perinatal Quality Surveillance Structure,

01:46:09.630 --> 01:46:11.360
which means that we bring together

01:46:11.360 --> 01:46:14.970
whole other different
organisations at a regional level

01:46:14.970 --> 01:46:17.040
and they come together
at a national level.

01:46:17.040 --> 01:46:20.520
We chair that joint group
where we've got our regulators,

01:46:20.520 --> 01:46:25.410
like CQC, NMC, GMC, we
have our royal colleges.

01:46:25.410 --> 01:46:28.500
We have Embrace, who are
experts on perinatal mortality,

01:46:28.500 --> 01:46:32.100
NHS Resolution, who obviously
investigate the bad outcomes,

01:46:32.100 --> 01:46:35.250
HCIB and various other key groups,

01:46:35.250 --> 01:46:38.610
working collaboration, no longer a silo.

01:46:38.610 --> 01:46:42.210
We work together around the
table sharing intelligence

01:46:42.210 --> 01:46:45.030
to identify who needs a helping hand up.

01:46:45.030 --> 01:46:47.700
The work from Bill Kirkup
in "Reading the Signals"

01:46:47.700 --> 01:46:50.730
will provide an extra lens
on that with the data work.

01:46:50.730 --> 01:46:52.713
And we've got some real exciting work,

01:46:53.820 --> 01:46:57.036
both with AI teams to try
and pull out more learning

01:46:57.036 --> 01:47:00.050
from reports and also...

01:47:02.378 --> 01:47:06.090
the operational work that we've got.

01:47:06.090 --> 01:47:09.780
So understanding which services
are regularly understaffed,

01:47:09.780 --> 01:47:13.590
which services of too
much operational pressure.

01:47:13.590 --> 01:47:16.800
We can bring together the
culture, the operational work,

01:47:16.800 --> 01:47:20.550
the outcome data to really
get upstream of these problems

01:47:20.550 --> 01:47:23.040
so we know who needs a helping hand.

01:47:23.040 --> 01:47:24.450
The earlier we do it,

01:47:24.450 --> 01:47:27.150
hopefully the more trust can
solve their own problems,

01:47:27.150 --> 01:47:28.980
but we've got regions and
eventually we'll cover

01:47:28.980 --> 01:47:31.800
our National Maternity Safety
Programme to support that.

01:47:31.800 --> 01:47:32.723
- [Richard] Thank you.

01:47:33.840 --> 01:47:35.820
- There are other couple
of quick enablers.

01:47:35.820 --> 01:47:40.080
Digital, Tim, you've talked
about how important Digital is.

01:47:40.080 --> 01:47:42.060
We agree, we need your help.

01:47:42.060 --> 01:47:44.970
It's integral to what
we're doing in Maternity,

01:47:44.970 --> 01:47:46.170
and there's lots we are doing

01:47:46.170 --> 01:47:48.420
about standards and best practise.

01:47:48.420 --> 01:47:49.710
I can list them to you but--

01:47:49.710 --> 01:47:50.769
- [Richard] Don't.

01:47:50.769 --> 01:47:51.720
- Essentially,

01:47:51.720 --> 01:47:55.170
we are innovating at speed all the time

01:47:55.170 --> 01:47:56.580
and that's one of the
reasons we've driven down

01:47:56.580 --> 01:47:59.010
perinatal mortality so dramatically

01:47:59.010 --> 01:48:01.879
over the last few years
by all that innovation.

01:48:01.879 --> 01:48:03.300
- Thank you, I'm going to interrupt you,

01:48:03.300 --> 01:48:04.512
only because I think.
- No, I'm finished.

01:48:04.512 --> 01:48:06.990
- The answers that you
guys will have left to give

01:48:06.990 --> 01:48:09.480
to the questions if we,
but thank you very much.

01:48:09.480 --> 01:48:12.450
It's very clear, so comments or questions?

01:48:12.450 --> 01:48:13.283
Andrew, I know.

01:48:14.190 --> 01:48:17.130
- So, I welcome this report.

01:48:17.130 --> 01:48:18.750
As a kind of former Chief Exec,

01:48:18.750 --> 01:48:21.720
it's really good that you've
got one reference document

01:48:21.720 --> 01:48:24.000
to go to now and it's, you know,

01:48:24.000 --> 01:48:26.430
easy to read whether you are a clinician,

01:48:26.430 --> 01:48:29.700
a member of a trust
board or running an ICB.

01:48:29.700 --> 01:48:33.123
So I think we've made some
really, really strong progress.

01:48:34.650 --> 01:48:37.530
Ultimately, though, it
is the responsibility

01:48:37.530 --> 01:48:40.920
of all of our trust
boards to implement this.

01:48:40.920 --> 01:48:43.290
And they have to hold the ring

01:48:43.290 --> 01:48:46.950
in relation to all the other
prior competing priorities

01:48:46.950 --> 01:48:47.940
that they have.

01:48:47.940 --> 01:48:49.953
But we are asking, aren't we,

01:48:49.953 --> 01:48:53.067
that all boards, you know,
give attention to this

01:48:53.067 --> 01:48:56.402
and actually focus on the must do,

01:48:56.402 --> 01:49:00.420
the key changes we want
to see as a result of

01:49:00.420 --> 01:49:03.360
the Ockenden Report and the Kirkup Report.

01:49:03.360 --> 01:49:06.450
So it's around culture,
it's around staffing,

01:49:06.450 --> 01:49:08.520
it's around speaking up,

01:49:08.520 --> 01:49:10.320
which I think is, you know,

01:49:10.320 --> 01:49:13.800
the three key kind of dimensions.

01:49:13.800 --> 01:49:17.640
NHS England has a big role here to play,

01:49:17.640 --> 01:49:22.640
because in the past, we've
not necessarily been cited on

01:49:22.830 --> 01:49:25.530
organisations who are in difficulty.

01:49:25.530 --> 01:49:27.630
So I think, Matthew, what you've just said

01:49:27.630 --> 01:49:30.330
around spotting those organisations,

01:49:30.330 --> 01:49:33.000
that for various reasons are experiencing,

01:49:33.000 --> 01:49:36.210
you know, immense difficulties
in getting good outcomes

01:49:36.210 --> 01:49:38.340
or staffing or whatever it is,

01:49:38.340 --> 01:49:40.110
and we are there as the helping hand.

01:49:40.110 --> 01:49:42.840
I think that that is
the big difference here.

01:49:42.840 --> 01:49:45.580
And providing you've got,
you know, enough resource

01:49:46.631 --> 01:49:48.030
to handle that,

01:49:48.030 --> 01:49:50.340
I think that's a major step forward.

01:49:50.340 --> 01:49:53.850
It's a key ingredient that
we hadn't got in place

01:49:53.850 --> 01:49:57.360
three years ago when some
of us around this table,

01:49:57.360 --> 01:49:59.940
you know, heard the shocking
news around what happened

01:49:59.940 --> 01:50:02.040
in Telford and Shrewsbury.

01:50:02.040 --> 01:50:05.040
So I think we've moved
forward a hell of a lot,

01:50:05.040 --> 01:50:07.470
but I just want to emphasise, ultimately,

01:50:07.470 --> 01:50:10.080
it is the responsibility
of our trust boards

01:50:10.080 --> 01:50:11.880
to actually make this happen.

01:50:11.880 --> 01:50:14.160
- That's a very well made point, Andrew.

01:50:14.160 --> 01:50:17.883
Can I, I'm going to bring in
Wol, and then, I think, David.

01:50:18.758 --> 01:50:20.130
- Thanks, Ruth.

01:50:20.130 --> 01:50:21.380
I remember the challenge.

01:50:22.290 --> 01:50:23.880
So congratulations to
you and your colleagues

01:50:23.880 --> 01:50:26.700
for having done all the
work to pull a really,

01:50:26.700 --> 01:50:29.140
very clear plan together,

01:50:29.140 --> 01:50:30.900
and it, I mean, it it's so complete,

01:50:30.900 --> 01:50:33.690
it's very difficult to
actually find errors to pick.

01:50:33.690 --> 01:50:35.010
And, again, you guys have really,

01:50:35.010 --> 01:50:37.890
really put forward very
compelling rationale

01:50:37.890 --> 01:50:39.480
for why this should work.

01:50:39.480 --> 01:50:41.250
So my question is around

01:50:41.250 --> 01:50:43.560
the responsibilities and the feedback loop

01:50:43.560 --> 01:50:45.000
and the transparency.

01:50:45.000 --> 01:50:48.540
So I think this will only
work if that culture thing

01:50:48.540 --> 01:50:50.270
is absolutely there.

01:50:50.270 --> 01:50:52.410
And that culture means
that we don't report

01:50:52.410 --> 01:50:54.150
just when it's gone bad,

01:50:54.150 --> 01:50:57.287
we report also when it's
gone, it's nearly gone bad.

01:50:57.287 --> 01:50:58.170
- Right.
- Right?

01:50:58.170 --> 01:51:02.970
That the near misses are as
important as the issues arise.

01:51:02.970 --> 01:51:04.080
Now, my challenge would be

01:51:04.080 --> 01:51:05.790
in all the data and responsibility

01:51:05.790 --> 01:51:07.620
and stuff coming back up to the top,

01:51:07.620 --> 01:51:10.440
how do we find that and
how do we report on that?

01:51:10.440 --> 01:51:14.160
Because we'll learn so much
from that about the culture.

01:51:14.160 --> 01:51:16.350
Maybe it gives us a sort
of early warning signals

01:51:16.350 --> 01:51:18.840
as people who are not quite going there,

01:51:18.840 --> 01:51:19.673
et cetera, et cetera.

01:51:19.673 --> 01:51:20.520
So that's my only challenge,

01:51:20.520 --> 01:51:21.870
but great piece of work,

01:51:21.870 --> 01:51:24.020
and I'm really pleased to you guys to on.

01:51:24.020 --> 01:51:25.410
- So hold that question,

01:51:25.410 --> 01:51:27.720
and I think Andrew's
question was more looking

01:51:27.720 --> 01:51:30.990
for an affirmation back
that that point is,

01:51:30.990 --> 01:51:33.540
is clearly, those points
are clearly well understood,

01:51:33.540 --> 01:51:35.043
but David do you want to come in?

01:51:35.043 --> 01:51:37.410
- Yeah, thanks, Richard,
I think it builds on that.

01:51:37.410 --> 01:51:41.403
So congratulations to
you four and the teams

01:51:41.403 --> 01:51:42.903
that have developed this.

01:51:44.250 --> 01:51:46.710
A bit like Andrew, I've
sat through the reports

01:51:46.710 --> 01:51:48.450
back from Donna's report,

01:51:48.450 --> 01:51:50.913
all through my membership of the board,

01:51:52.585 --> 01:51:55.020
and I think what you've
developed is a really good plan.

01:51:55.020 --> 01:51:57.750
What I liked is, Jacqui,
you referred to it,

01:51:57.750 --> 01:52:01.447
the sections that say
"listening to families,"

01:52:01.447 --> 01:52:03.330
"women and families."

01:52:03.330 --> 01:52:04.953
What's our ambition here?

01:52:05.970 --> 01:52:08.820
How will we determine success
it plays at this point,

01:52:08.820 --> 01:52:10.290
but what are the outcome measures?

01:52:10.290 --> 01:52:11.820
How do we know we've got progress?

01:52:11.820 --> 01:52:13.440
What's the evidence?

01:52:13.440 --> 01:52:17.310
And, importantly, what's
the regulatory framework?

01:52:17.310 --> 01:52:19.410
And not just what the
regulators do but how,

01:52:19.410 --> 01:52:21.990
what is our feedback loops come to that?

01:52:21.990 --> 01:52:26.990
I almost think this sets a
benchmark for a delivery plan,

01:52:27.090 --> 01:52:30.870
to be honest, and I'd want
to commend you for that.

01:52:30.870 --> 01:52:33.780
Particularly the bit about culture.

01:52:33.780 --> 01:52:36.750
'Cause when Bill delivered
his findings on the culture,

01:52:36.750 --> 01:52:39.270
I was thinking, so how do
you operationalize this?

01:52:39.270 --> 01:52:40.800
I think he's absolutely spot on,

01:52:40.800 --> 01:52:42.990
but how do you operationalize it?

01:52:42.990 --> 01:52:45.150
And I think you've picked a where to begin

01:52:45.150 --> 01:52:46.320
to operationalize it.

01:52:46.320 --> 01:52:50.370
So, I really do commend that.

01:52:50.370 --> 01:52:52.680
I guess Richard, my question is,

01:52:52.680 --> 01:52:56.610
and I think it builds on
Andrew, and I hope Wol's point,

01:52:56.610 --> 01:52:59.130
which is, how do we as a board,

01:52:59.130 --> 01:53:01.620
not reserve the role of boards,

01:53:01.620 --> 01:53:04.200
trust boards or ICBs,

01:53:04.200 --> 01:53:06.480
but how do we know this
plan is being delivered

01:53:06.480 --> 01:53:09.741
and being effectively delivered
over that period of time?

01:53:09.741 --> 01:53:10.591
- Okay.
- And I,

01:53:11.880 --> 01:53:13.470
sitting through the debates,

01:53:13.470 --> 01:53:15.270
Ruth was emotionally moved

01:53:15.270 --> 01:53:17.160
the last time we had
a debate in this room.

01:53:17.160 --> 01:53:17.993
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:53:17.993 --> 01:53:20.883
- And I think all of us will probably say

01:53:20.883 --> 01:53:23.640
that this has shaken
us to different degrees

01:53:23.640 --> 01:53:25.650
and different impacts.

01:53:25.650 --> 01:53:27.663
And I think we owe it to

01:53:29.940 --> 01:53:33.690
the women that have
experienced, poor care,

01:53:33.690 --> 01:53:36.570
the reviews that have been done as,

01:53:36.570 --> 01:53:39.120
as well as the women that
are going to give birth,

01:53:39.120 --> 01:53:40.890
to actually continue to be vigilant

01:53:40.890 --> 01:53:42.750
in the way that we monitor this.

01:53:42.750 --> 01:53:47.070
Because whilst there is
a way down, set out here,

01:53:47.070 --> 01:53:50.820
I think Bill Kirkup's report
was absolutely spot on.

01:53:50.820 --> 01:53:54.570
Do we have the data to identify
the early warning signs?

01:53:54.570 --> 01:53:56.250
And I think that's the one that we need

01:53:56.250 --> 01:53:59.010
to be incredibly vigilant
on, not just the feedback,

01:53:59.010 --> 01:54:02.790
but can we really work
at early warning signs

01:54:02.790 --> 01:54:05.610
and begin to use that to
inform what we do differently?

01:54:05.610 --> 01:54:08.010
So we're not having
reports on what went wrong.

01:54:08.010 --> 01:54:10.140
Rather than reports on intervening early

01:54:10.140 --> 01:54:11.130
to improve something.

01:54:11.130 --> 01:54:13.770
So my question is, how do we,

01:54:13.770 --> 01:54:15.870
as a board, how do we monitor this?

01:54:15.870 --> 01:54:17.280
We might not be able to answer it today,

01:54:17.280 --> 01:54:19.920
but I'd just like, it needs
to be on our forward planner,

01:54:19.920 --> 01:54:20.753
basically.

01:54:20.753 --> 01:54:21.603
- So look, it is...

01:54:22.467 --> 01:54:25.023
so, so, so important
that we get this right.

01:54:27.030 --> 01:54:29.220
So just in terms of today,

01:54:29.220 --> 01:54:32.010
I mean, I've got three more
questions coming at you.

01:54:32.010 --> 01:54:34.740
So if you want to deal
quite quickly with those,

01:54:34.740 --> 01:54:35.970
but at particular David's comment,

01:54:35.970 --> 01:54:37.470
we should reflect actually as a board

01:54:37.470 --> 01:54:40.560
about how we're making sure we
are looking at the right data

01:54:40.560 --> 01:54:43.076
and then intervening at the right time.

01:54:43.076 --> 01:54:45.480
And to also Wol's point
about the near misses

01:54:45.480 --> 01:54:47.070
are actually as important in terms

01:54:47.070 --> 01:54:49.230
of informing us about what's going on.

01:54:49.230 --> 01:54:52.080
But you want to quickly
respond to those three points?

01:54:52.080 --> 01:54:53.777
So then there's three
will coming your way.

01:54:53.777 --> 01:54:56.703
- Really quick, so the near misses,

01:54:58.230 --> 01:55:01.140
we have much better data
now than we had before.

01:55:01.140 --> 01:55:03.960
We are pulling it together a
National Maternity Dashboard,

01:55:03.960 --> 01:55:06.360
where going to have some
statistical process controls,

01:55:06.360 --> 01:55:08.160
et cetera, on that, so
we can start to look

01:55:08.160 --> 01:55:11.590
where the peaks are in various outcomes

01:55:11.590 --> 01:55:14.340
before we lead to absolute tragedies.

01:55:14.340 --> 01:55:16.950
But, actually, I think
where I'm more excited is

01:55:16.950 --> 01:55:18.840
about how we're tracking implementation

01:55:18.840 --> 01:55:20.250
of best practise care

01:55:20.250 --> 01:55:24.090
because that's a further step upstream.

01:55:24.090 --> 01:55:25.620
If we can actually make sure people

01:55:25.620 --> 01:55:29.010
are delivering best practise
care in the first place,

01:55:29.010 --> 01:55:31.260
we'll have less of those
near misses as well.

01:55:31.260 --> 01:55:34.080
So I think that focus on implementation

01:55:34.080 --> 01:55:35.970
of really good care is where

01:55:35.970 --> 01:55:38.460
I think we'll get the biggest gains.

01:55:38.460 --> 01:55:41.190
As far as the early warning signs,

01:55:41.190 --> 01:55:44.587
I think it's something building
on what I talked before,

01:55:44.587 --> 01:55:48.060
there's a very good paper
in the BMJ, recently,

01:55:48.060 --> 01:55:49.860
about metrics and measuring

01:55:49.860 --> 01:55:52.440
and identified organisations
maybe running into trouble

01:55:52.440 --> 01:55:54.870
and they, one thing,
one of the key points is

01:55:54.870 --> 01:55:57.573
there's not a single metric for this.

01:55:57.573 --> 01:55:59.490
It's about triangulating the metrics,

01:55:59.490 --> 01:56:02.553
about people pulling the
different lenses together.

01:56:03.510 --> 01:56:04.343
That's important.

01:56:04.343 --> 01:56:09.343
So I think that mixture of
measuring the operational stress,

01:56:09.660 --> 01:56:13.290
the acuity and workload on trusts,

01:56:13.290 --> 01:56:16.500
triangulating that with
the culture, the morale,

01:56:16.500 --> 01:56:20.370
the outcomes with those external lenses

01:56:20.370 --> 01:56:24.030
that we're getting from
NHS resolution, Embrace,

01:56:24.030 --> 01:56:24.963
the colleges,

01:56:26.430 --> 01:56:30.030
HEE, HEE tells so much
about what the trainees feel

01:56:30.030 --> 01:56:31.470
about the service they're working in.

01:56:31.470 --> 01:56:35.070
They will tell you what the
culture is like in services

01:56:35.070 --> 01:56:37.367
now and today, and if
we talk to those people,

01:56:37.367 --> 01:56:39.180
we get the right answers.

01:56:39.180 --> 01:56:40.050
- So let me, I got to put,

01:56:40.050 --> 01:56:42.270
I'm going to bring in the
last three questions to Mary,

01:56:42.270 --> 01:56:45.849
you did signal, and then
Navina and then Munir.

01:56:45.849 --> 01:56:46.803
- Thank you.
- And then...

01:56:46.803 --> 01:56:48.330
I'll let you wrap up with.

01:56:48.330 --> 01:56:51.510
- I mean, I want to join
everybody else's congratulations

01:56:51.510 --> 01:56:55.743
and say how much I welcome
this particular strategy.

01:56:57.510 --> 01:56:58.343
I also want to say that, you know,

01:56:58.343 --> 01:57:00.630
it was really good that
we had the opportunity

01:57:00.630 --> 01:57:03.870
to comment on it earlier,
as board members.

01:57:03.870 --> 01:57:05.910
And I think it has been strengthened

01:57:05.910 --> 01:57:08.250
since the previous draft,

01:57:08.250 --> 01:57:13.250
and I'm delighted about
the focus on those people

01:57:13.530 --> 01:57:15.840
who have mental health problems

01:57:15.840 --> 01:57:18.510
who may need additional help.

01:57:18.510 --> 01:57:23.510
But my question is around the
14 maternal medicine networks.

01:57:24.780 --> 01:57:27.390
We do have a movement in our country

01:57:27.390 --> 01:57:30.120
that people would like natural birth.

01:57:30.120 --> 01:57:33.180
And how are you going to ensure

01:57:33.180 --> 01:57:36.630
that professionals explain properly

01:57:36.630 --> 01:57:40.260
to women and their families
where they need to go

01:57:40.260 --> 01:57:42.840
to one of these Maternal
Medicine Networks,

01:57:42.840 --> 01:57:46.980
and how far are people going
to have to go to those?

01:57:46.980 --> 01:57:48.497
- Thank you.

01:57:48.497 --> 01:57:49.330
Navina?

01:57:49.330 --> 01:57:50.163
- Very quickly,

01:57:50.163 --> 01:57:54.150
I think this is a report
that is really hopeful,

01:57:54.150 --> 01:57:58.110
and with that in mind, and my
background in co-production,

01:57:58.110 --> 01:58:01.860
and it's been co-produced
with women and their families,

01:58:01.860 --> 01:58:05.070
so can we take co-production
into the actual delivery of it

01:58:05.070 --> 01:58:07.800
in a way that it feels
like a promise, actually?

01:58:07.800 --> 01:58:09.220
It feels like a promise

01:58:10.375 --> 01:58:12.947
to women and families for the future.

01:58:12.947 --> 01:58:15.660
And so I wonder whether we
could develop that bit more

01:58:15.660 --> 01:58:17.760
in terms of how we take co-production

01:58:17.760 --> 01:58:18.930
of the delivery further.

01:58:18.930 --> 01:58:22.620
And I would be really keen to
be involved if it's possible.

01:58:22.620 --> 01:58:23.490
- Munir?

01:58:23.490 --> 01:58:26.490
- Thanks, again, welcome the
report and really great to see

01:58:26.490 --> 01:58:29.070
that the patient voice and the staff voice

01:58:29.070 --> 01:58:32.193
is given so much emphasis in the report.

01:58:33.030 --> 01:58:36.660
Just coming to the data,
the minimal relevant data

01:58:36.660 --> 01:58:38.940
that you need to collect to
be able to intervene early.

01:58:38.940 --> 01:58:42.000
There are groups in this
country that are developed

01:58:42.000 --> 01:58:44.850
world-leading expertise
and core outcome measures,

01:58:44.850 --> 01:58:46.920
and that committee that
you mentioned, Ruth,

01:58:46.920 --> 01:58:49.317
I'm happy to put you in
touch with those people

01:58:49.317 --> 01:58:51.090
who may be able to help
you with your committee

01:58:51.090 --> 01:58:52.803
in terms of core outcome measures.

01:58:54.480 --> 01:58:55.830
- Do you want to, Ruth,

01:58:55.830 --> 01:58:57.930
do you want to coordinate the
response to those last two?

01:58:57.930 --> 01:58:59.130
- Yes, Jacqui, do you want to just say,

01:58:59.130 --> 01:59:00.530
talk about the rebirth...

01:59:01.650 --> 01:59:03.900
work in terms of, and Matthew,

01:59:03.900 --> 01:59:05.398
do you want to kick off, Matthew, then?

01:59:05.398 --> 01:59:06.673
- Yeah, so,

01:59:06.673 --> 01:59:09.423
I'll start for Maternal
Medicine Networks very quickly.

01:59:10.620 --> 01:59:13.080
The important thing about
Maternal Medicine Networks,

01:59:13.080 --> 01:59:15.210
they're not just centres of excellence,

01:59:15.210 --> 01:59:16.470
they're covering the whole region.

01:59:16.470 --> 01:59:18.720
It's up-skilling all the spokes.

01:59:18.720 --> 01:59:22.383
It's every unit that
they have reached out to.

01:59:23.730 --> 01:59:25.050
That's really important because

01:59:25.050 --> 01:59:28.680
in terms of one of our problems is that

01:59:28.680 --> 01:59:32.550
when you are facing a woman
with really complex problems,

01:59:32.550 --> 01:59:34.417
it's very easy to drift into,

01:59:34.417 --> 01:59:35.820
"Oh, we'll do a caesarean section,"

01:59:35.820 --> 01:59:37.110
because you're trying to minimise

01:59:37.110 --> 01:59:38.790
the number of things that go wrong.

01:59:38.790 --> 01:59:42.150
What we see, is once you get
real experts involved in this,

01:59:42.150 --> 01:59:44.198
who can break it down and put

01:59:44.198 --> 01:59:46.290
the right mitigating issues in place,

01:59:46.290 --> 01:59:49.413
we actually reduce the
need for intervention.

01:59:50.370 --> 01:59:54.570
So really good antenatal
care helps promote

01:59:54.570 --> 01:59:56.610
women having uncomplicated,
straightforward births

01:59:56.610 --> 01:59:58.560
and the birth that they want to have.

01:59:58.560 --> 02:00:02.280
So it is, so the net,
so I wanted to advise,

02:00:02.280 --> 02:00:04.290
it's a network rather just a centre

02:00:04.290 --> 02:00:07.080
and it improves antenatal
care, which improves outcomes.

02:00:07.080 --> 02:00:09.080
- Yeah. but I really want to know

02:00:09.080 --> 02:00:12.210
if I've got X as a severe complication.

02:00:12.210 --> 02:00:13.170
- Yeah.

02:00:13.170 --> 02:00:16.890
- Is it that advice is going
to be given to my local team

02:00:16.890 --> 02:00:19.590
from a specialist team?

02:00:19.590 --> 02:00:22.620
I understand the issue of
networks, or might I have to go,

02:00:22.620 --> 02:00:23.610
just for arguments sake.

02:00:23.610 --> 02:00:25.261
- It's tailored solutions.

02:00:25.261 --> 02:00:27.720
So, some people will
need to go to the centre

02:00:27.720 --> 02:00:29.250
and see the world expert.

02:00:29.250 --> 02:00:30.180
- Exactly.
- Or even

02:00:30.180 --> 02:00:32.910
a single national centre
and see the right person.

02:00:32.910 --> 02:00:35.700
Other times, we will
up-skill the clinician

02:00:35.700 --> 02:00:36.533
at the front line.

02:00:36.533 --> 02:00:38.692
- I understand.
- So it's.

02:00:38.692 --> 02:00:39.525
- I understand that.
- We can do

02:00:39.525 --> 02:00:40.710
whatever the woman needs we can do.

02:00:40.710 --> 02:00:42.630
- But somehow that has got to be a,

02:00:42.630 --> 02:00:45.737
got across to women at the
same time as publishing this,

02:00:45.737 --> 02:00:48.930
but it isn't, much as there is choice,

02:00:48.930 --> 02:00:52.200
just sometimes to really
give them good care,

02:00:52.200 --> 02:00:55.380
there will be a need to do
exactly what you've said.

02:00:55.380 --> 02:00:56.580
- Perhaps we catch up afterwards.

02:00:56.580 --> 02:00:58.110
I can give you lots of examples

02:00:58.110 --> 02:00:59.040
of how it's happening all the time.

02:00:59.040 --> 02:01:00.630
It's really exciting.

02:01:00.630 --> 02:01:04.860
It's women are receiving this care today.

02:01:04.860 --> 02:01:06.706
- [Richard] Okay, thank you.

02:01:06.706 --> 02:01:07.709
Thank you.

02:01:07.709 --> 02:01:09.120
- Shall I conclude?
- Ruth, would you like to

02:01:09.120 --> 02:01:10.490
wrap this, yeah.
- Just conclude then.

02:01:10.490 --> 02:01:13.050
So working backwards, absolutely.

02:01:13.050 --> 02:01:15.180
Definitely take all the
help and the connections

02:01:15.180 --> 02:01:18.390
and I'm sure Edile will just really

02:01:18.390 --> 02:01:20.520
enjoy having that connection.

02:01:20.520 --> 02:01:23.610
Navina, yes, I think it is a promise,

02:01:23.610 --> 02:01:28.050
and absolutely, doing this
jointly into the future,

02:01:28.050 --> 02:01:30.900
as well as past and president,
would be really important.

02:01:33.091 --> 02:01:34.410
And, just generally,

02:01:34.410 --> 02:01:38.790
this is why it is a board,
as our responsibility,

02:01:38.790 --> 02:01:40.440
but it's, as Andrew said,

02:01:40.440 --> 02:01:44.500
it is primarily the
trust boards themselves

02:01:45.390 --> 02:01:48.420
who hold the responsibility
for safe maternity care.

02:01:48.420 --> 02:01:51.120
But that's why we've set out in this plan,

02:01:51.120 --> 02:01:53.280
different areas, different roles

02:01:53.280 --> 02:01:56.010
for different parts of the NHS.

02:01:56.010 --> 02:01:57.660
It is all of our responsibilities,

02:01:57.660 --> 02:02:01.050
but having that clarity of
who's responsible for what

02:02:01.050 --> 02:02:02.550
I think will help.

02:02:02.550 --> 02:02:05.460
Thank you, I hope that
we'll come back in X,

02:02:05.460 --> 02:02:06.783
six months or whatever,

02:02:07.680 --> 02:02:10.770
with an update so we can share
with you what we are doing,

02:02:10.770 --> 02:02:13.530
how are we getting on and how
the service is getting on too.

02:02:13.530 --> 02:02:14.880
- And, particularly, to that point,

02:02:14.880 --> 02:02:18.480
which is, are we seeing the data earlier,

02:02:18.480 --> 02:02:20.760
seeing the relevant data
that allows us to intervene

02:02:20.760 --> 02:02:23.820
and support early enough
rather than receiving,

02:02:23.820 --> 02:02:25.297
rather distressing reports about

02:02:25.297 --> 02:02:26.290
things that have gone wrong.
- And we might even bring

02:02:26.290 --> 02:02:29.070
Edile next time with us.

02:02:29.070 --> 02:02:30.520
- Yeah, very good, thank you.

02:02:31.821 --> 02:02:33.690
Well, I'm going to draw
that item to a close

02:02:33.690 --> 02:02:35.375
in interest of time.

02:02:35.375 --> 02:02:38.280
There are three or four
matters of governance

02:02:38.280 --> 02:02:41.400
just to deal with, I
think, relatively quickly.

02:02:41.400 --> 02:02:45.990
We've revised the a NHS England
Board Governance Framework,

02:02:45.990 --> 02:02:49.020
and I'm asking for the board's support

02:02:49.020 --> 02:02:51.960
to approve the changes to the
governance manual documents

02:02:51.960 --> 02:02:54.300
at standing orders, the
scheme of delegation

02:02:54.300 --> 02:02:56.373
and the standing financial instructions.

02:02:57.690 --> 02:02:59.790
And then, separately, within that actually

02:02:59.790 --> 02:03:01.200
to approve the establishment

02:03:01.200 --> 02:03:02.340
of something I referred to earlier,

02:03:02.340 --> 02:03:05.100
which is the Workforce Training
and Education Committee

02:03:05.100 --> 02:03:07.350
as of 1st of April,

02:03:07.350 --> 02:03:11.040
which will support the safe
transfer to NHS England

02:03:11.040 --> 02:03:13.620
and the continued robust oversight

02:03:13.620 --> 02:03:18.420
of workforce training and
education functions from HEE.

02:03:18.420 --> 02:03:21.090
So, board is okay and
happy to support those?

02:03:21.090 --> 02:03:22.473
Thank you very much.

02:03:23.400 --> 02:03:25.440
There are a couple of
other items which are

02:03:25.440 --> 02:03:27.540
either annual or biannual starred items.

02:03:27.540 --> 02:03:30.840
One is just to report on the
use of the NHS England seal.

02:03:30.840 --> 02:03:33.240
You've seen that provided to you.

02:03:33.240 --> 02:03:34.953
And that's for information.

02:03:35.910 --> 02:03:39.120
And then, separately, we
have the annual approval

02:03:39.120 --> 02:03:41.820
of the emergency, essentially
the emergency backup,

02:03:41.820 --> 02:03:45.000
so the Emergency Preparedness
Resilience and Response

02:03:45.000 --> 02:03:46.413
Assurance Report.

02:03:47.400 --> 02:03:49.770
It's an annual report
summarising that work.

02:03:49.770 --> 02:03:52.410
And it provides assurance to us as a board

02:03:52.410 --> 02:03:54.390
of our duties here

02:03:54.390 --> 02:03:57.090
and our preparedness to
respond to any emergencies.

02:03:57.090 --> 02:03:59.700
Are people happy to support that?

02:03:59.700 --> 02:04:00.873
Thank you very much.

02:04:02.010 --> 02:04:04.290
And, lastly, just for information,

02:04:04.290 --> 02:04:06.780
the board has asked us
to note the directions

02:04:06.780 --> 02:04:09.630
which have been issued to NHS England

02:04:09.630 --> 02:04:14.040
since the merger on the 1st
of February with NHS Digital.

02:04:14.040 --> 02:04:16.230
And that's in accordance
with our commitments

02:04:16.230 --> 02:04:18.857
around transparency, particularly
around data transparency.

02:04:18.857 --> 02:04:22.560
Is the board happy to note those?

02:04:22.560 --> 02:04:23.560
Thank you very much.

02:04:24.510 --> 02:04:26.550
Any other business before I close?

02:04:26.550 --> 02:04:28.050
Just looking around the table.

02:04:29.580 --> 02:04:32.640
In which case, can I thank
the board and the executives?

02:04:32.640 --> 02:04:33.960
I think there are, you know,

02:04:33.960 --> 02:04:37.050
good feedback and reports
from David and from Julian

02:04:37.050 --> 02:04:39.570
and, as always, from Amanda
as our Chief Executive.

02:04:39.570 --> 02:04:40.980
And then two very good discussions.

02:04:40.980 --> 02:04:44.220
I think one on innovation,
and secondly on maternity.

02:04:44.220 --> 02:04:46.290
And with that, let me call
the meeting to a close.

02:04:46.290 --> 02:04:47.123
Thank you.

